HT_DATA.load("detail", {"HxaD_trXwRE":{"wy":"HN returns to it for years as an elegant introduction to writing a lexer that is easy to follow even without a parsing background.","q":{"x":"It's far more interesting than it sounds, and even if you don't know much about lexing/parsing it's easy to follow.","i":7822010,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":42083842,"author":"hu3","month":"2024-11","story":"Rust for tokenising and parsing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Educational and elegant approach to lexical scanning.","quote":"I love Rob Pike's talk about lexical Scanning in Go (2011). Educational and elegant approach.","text":"Related, I love Rob Pike's talk about lexical Scanning in Go (2011). Educational and elegant approach. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12638297,"author":"grose","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds a lexer from scratch simply and elegantly using coroutines, with humor.","quote":"I love everything about this talk.","text":"Lexical Scanning in Go by Rob Pike [VIDEO] I love everything about this talk. It walks you through building a lexer from scratch in a simple and elegant way, through a very interesting use of coroutines. I appreciate the bits of humor in the talk as well."},{"id":7822010,"author":"TomNomNom","month":"2014-05","story":"Templating in Go","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains lexing in text/template; easy to follow even without parsing background.","quote":"It's far more interesting than it sounds, and even if you don't know much about lexing/parsing it's easy to follow.","text":"Rob Pike did a really good talk entitled 'Lexical Scanning In Go'[0], which covers how the lexing for text/template works. It's far more interesting than it sounds, and even if you don't know much about lexing/parsing it's easy to follow. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4130645,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2012-06","story":"Parsing huge XML files with Go","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pike on parsers returning functions that consume the next token.","quote":"Rob Pike has an excellent video about this","text":"Personally, I have a penchant for writing my own pull parsers. Its a mind-expanding exercise. The neat thing about Go is that parsers can return functions that consume the next token. Rob Pike has an excellent video about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44291371,"author":"Xeoncross","month":"2025-06","story":"Datalog in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Older talk whose lexing approach applies easily to other languages.","quote":"It's in Go, but you can apply most of it easily in other languages.","text":"If you enjoyed the state machine + parsing part and also I recommend the older \"Lexical Scanning in Go\" talk that Rob Pike gave: [VIDEO] It's in Go, but you can apply most of it easily in other languages. I'm so happy that modern languages like Rust, Zig, and Go support unicode/runes/graphemes natively. So many problem just disappear compared to Java, .net, C++, or scripting languages."},{"id":33413953,"author":"gorgoiler","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What piece of code/codebase blew your mind when you saw it?","stance":"rec","why":"Nicely done lexer that is just great engineering.","quote":"It’s just great engineering","text":"Rob Pike’s lexer was very nicely done and struck a chord with me. It’s not nearly as magical as some of the other things here. It’s just great engineering: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11619990,"author":"etatoby","month":"2016-05","story":"Writing a Compiler in Go","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the text/template parser as a model for writing a compiler.","quote":"As an introduction to it, I recommend the talk \"Lexical Scanning in Go\" by Rob Pike","text":"I would like to mention that most, if not all, of the Go compiler and related utilities is written in Go itself, with high quality, open source code that can be found in the \"go\" package of the standard library: [LINK] I would recommend using that as a template for one's own compiler, because it's written in idiomatic Go (originally by the language creators) and is cleanly split across abstraction boundaries (packages \"scanner\", \"parser\", \"ast\", and so on.) There's also a smaller, simpler parser in the \"text/template/parse\" package, that is used to parse the templating languages in \"text/template\" and \"html/template\". As an introduction to it, I recommend the talk \"Lexical Scanning in Go\" by Rob Pike: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10802973,"author":"landr0id","month":"2015-12","story":"Implementing a programming language in D: Lexical Analysis","stance":"rec","why":"Useful when writing a first lexer; the commenter modeled theirs on it.","quote":"I found Rob Pike's talk on lexical scanning to be pretty useful","text":"Wow, perfect timing! I'm currently writing my first lexer/parser in D as well ( [LINK] ) for a Scheme-like language used in Halo 3. I found Rob Pike's talk on lexical scanning to be pretty useful ( [VIDEO] ) and I've modeled mine pretty heavily after the text/template lexer: [LINK] ."},{"id":6417758,"author":"canthonytucci","month":"2013-09","story":"Less is exponentially more (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"One of several great Go talks by Pike, about an hour.","quote":"He has given some really great Go talks which are available on youtube, about an hour each","text":"For those who don't know who Rob Pike is, he's pretty awesome, helped create UTF-8 among other things, and now works on Go. [LINK] He has given some really great Go talks which are available on youtube, about an hour each: Origins of Go Concurrency Style - [LINK] Lexical Scanning in Go - [VIDEO] Go Concurrency Patterns (One of my favorites) - [LINK]"},{"id":5537318,"author":"thisishugo","month":"2013-04","story":"Building a toy template engine in Python","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a better alternative to regex for parsing templates.","quote":"There is a better way, as demonstrated in Robe Pike's video[1] detailing how the Go template language is implemented.","text":"I'm not sure I like the idea of parsing templates with regular expressions. There is a better way, as demonstrated in Robe Pike's video[1] detailing how the Go template language is implemented. Edit: which isn't meant to detract from the fact that this is a great article. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5173796,"author":"jbarham","month":"2013-02","story":"Function types in Go","stance":"rec","why":"Pike describes building the text/template lexer using recursive state functions.","quote":"There's a video by Pike describing how he built the parser at","text":"> Building a lexer out of a recursive function type actually sounds like a really cool thing to try Rob Pike did just that for Go's text/template package ( [LINK] ). See the source lexer source code at [LINK] and note that stateFn is defined recursively. There's a video by Pike describing how he built the parser at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":5276440,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2013-02","story":"Concurrency is not Parallelism. Rob Pike at Waza 2012 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Example of concurrency as a way to structure software, without parallelism.","quote":"A nice example for this is shown in his talk about lexical scanning in Go","text":"> What most people don't realize is that for concurrency to be useful, there must be parallelism. No, as far as I understand from this and other talks, Rob Pike argues that concurrency is useful even without parallelism: it's a better way to structure software. A nice example for this is shown in his talk about lexical scanning in Go: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3725016,"author":"drbawb","month":"2012-03","story":"Go is amazing, period.","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how goroutines and channels change how you design algorithms.","quote":"watching Pike's talk on lexing and parsing in Go really hits this point home.","text":"As an aside: watching Pike's talk on lexing and parsing in Go really hits this point home. It shows how language support for cheap threads (goroutines) and channels really change the way you come up with an algorithm. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42234360,"author":"rurban","month":"2024-11","story":"From string to AST: parsing (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's lexer talk explains why not to parse with regex.","quote":"The most famous one Rob Pike in his lexer talk 2011","text":"There are many explanations. The most famous one Rob Pike in his lexer talk 2011 [VIDEO] at 10.48 Or [LINK] Or several articles why you should not parse with regex, like [LINK]"},{"id":11979410,"author":"nulltype","month":"2016-06","story":"On the Complexity and Performance of Parsing with Derivatives","stance":"reference","why":"Pike talks about the text/template lexer, which supports unicode.","quote":"Here's a video of Rob Pike, developer of Go, talking about a lexer he built for the Go text/template package","text":"Here's a video of Rob Pike, developer of Go, talking about a lexer he built for the Go text/template package: [VIDEO] It supports unicode."},{"id":8619396,"author":"wilsonfiifi","month":"2014-11","story":"Implementing a shell for Bytengine using 'state functions'","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's use of state functions inspired the commenter's shell implementation.","quote":"Rob Pike mentions the use of state functions in his talk \"Lexical Scanning in Go","text":"I thought this would be a good opportunity to use 'state functions' to implement a shell for Bytengine. Rob Pike mentions the use of state functions in his talk \"Lexical Scanning in Go\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":5197867,"author":"zemo","month":"2013-02","story":"State Machines in Go","stance":"reference","why":"State function pattern matches the one from the talk.","quote":"same as in Lexical Scanning in Go","text":"type state struct { // add any fields you need to persist here } type transition func(*state) transition same as in Lexical Scanning in Go ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":4677250,"author":"eternalban","month":"2012-10","story":"Carbon: high level programming language that compiles to plain C","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Check out the std lib: [LINK] -- that's canonical Go code. It's my language of choice for personal projects. A neat and compact language that is very trivial to pick up, feels light, and yet sufficiently subtle to intellectually engage your mind. Very productive, good performance, and very portable. [VIDEO] caveat: /imho/ the runtime is not yet fully cooked. ETA is Go1.1 (end of year I believe) for beefed up internals."},{"id":28167119,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2021-08","story":"Jackson structured programming","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I thought it was about the other Jackson.","text":"I have to admit that when I first heard the Jackson reference in [VIDEO] I thought it was about the other Jackson."},{"id":38879391,"author":"sapiogram","month":"2024-01","story":"Go: What we got right, what we got wrong","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Like this 2011 talk[1] which demonstrated a classic coroutine code pattern implemented with goroutines and channels.","text":"> I think Go spent a lot of time implying that goroutines were some sort of special language magic, but I think everybody knows now that they are basically a really good implementation of green threads that can take advantage of some internal knowledge to do optimal scheduling to avoid context switches. It took me a while to work this out, and I got a lot of pushback from the go team when I pointed this out internally. This comment deeply fascinates me, because I get the same feeling every time I go back and read/watch early Go resources from Rob Pike and others, but I've never actually heard it articulated before. I've always thought that surely they weren't that ignorant about PL theory and history? Or maybe Rob Pike himself was, but surely his team knew better? It really feels like they thought they were making some special hybrid between threads and coroutines, combining the advantages of both. Like this 2011 talk[1] which demonstrated a classic coroutine code pattern implemented with goroutines and channels. But as time went on, goroutines became fully pre-emptive, and their programming model is now identical to threads. Using them as coroutines only leads to misery (read: data races) in my experience. They're faster than OS threads, sure, but that's an implementation detail at this point. When do you think they eventually realized they were re-inventing the same green threads Java had created and scrapped 10 years earlier? [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["f6kdp27TYZs",2],["3DtUzH3zoFo",1],["dX649lnKAU0",1],["tfAnxaWiSeE",1],["JxI3Eu5DPwE",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["frnLiXclAMo",1],["P73REgj-3UE",1],["3GObi93tjZI",1],["8XUutFBbUrg",1],["vsMydMDi3rI",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1]]},"QyJZzq0v7Z4":{"wy":"HN treats it as the standard answer to why FP isn't mainstream and why languages succeed.","q":{"x":"This talk by Richard Feldman is an excellent discussion about why Functional Programming is not the norm","i":28515333,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28515333,"author":"yewenjie","month":"2021-09","story":"Functional programming is not popular because it is weird (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent discussion of why FP is not the norm.","quote":"This talk by Richard Feldman is an excellent discussion about why Functional Programming is not the norm","text":"This talk by Richard Feldman is an excellent discussion about why Functional Programming is not the norm - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25567850,"author":"seer","month":"2020-12","story":"Functional programming in C++ (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Answers why FP isn't mainstream: historical inertia and chance.","quote":"Fascinating talk.","text":"This is indeed a very interesting question! I’ve also asked myself that time and time again, along the path of slowly discovering what this FP thing is all about over the course of my career. It does look wonderful, it does save you a lot of effort in some cases, where “some” is a vast swathes of computational problems. And its so damn elegant. A lot of examples are thrown around were a bunch of lambda calculus can result in 10-20 fold reduction in source code size. So where’s the catch?There has to be something wrong right? Well recently I stumbled upon a clojure conf talk that tried to tackle precisely this question. [VIDEO] The short of it is that its more of a historical inertia and chance than any specific hidden gotcha. Fascinating talk."},{"id":45849399,"author":"agentultra","month":"2025-11","story":"Why I love OCaml (2023)","stance":"rec","why":"Reasonable theories for why FP is not the norm.","quote":"Richard Feldman [0] proposed some of the most reasonable theories as to why functional programming isn't the norm.","text":"Why isn't it more popular? I think Richard Feldman [0] proposed some of the most reasonable theories as to why functional programming isn't the norm. Your language needs to be the platform-exclusive language for a widely used platform, have a killer application for a highly desired application domain, or be backed by a monster war-chest of marketing money to sway opinions. Since Feldman's talk, Python has grown much faster in popularity in the sense of wide use in the market place... but mostly because it's the scripting language of choice for PyTorch and AI-adjacent libraries/tooling/frameworks which is... a killer application. I like OCaml. I started evaluating functional programming in it by taking the INRIA course online. I spent the last four and half years working in Haskell. I've built some side projects in Zig. I was a CL stan for many years. I asked this question a lot. We often say, \"use the best tool for the job.\" Often that means, \"use the tool that's available.\" I think languages like OCaml, Rust, Haskell etc can be \"popular,\" but in the sense that people like to talk about them and want to learn them and be able to use them (at least, as trends come and go). It's different from \"popular\" as in, \"widely adopted.\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39190282,"author":"eduction","month":"2024-01","story":"A Tour of the Lisps","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form answer on why functional languages are not mainstream.","quote":"This is a great talk on exactly this topic from a Clojure conference, if you want a long answer.","text":"This is a great talk on exactly this topic from a Clojure conference, if you want a long answer. It focuses on the “functional” feature of Clojure but all of it applies to the whole language (and probably any good lisp) IMO. [VIDEO] My brief text answer (focused on Clojure): -python has been around about 20 more years than Clojure -the advantages of Clojure vs python/etc probably aren’t nearly as big as python vs a non memory managed, compiled, static language like C. That whole generation of “scripting” languages had the wind at their backs in a way Clojure and its contemporaries never quite will (though Clojure et al tend to be much better at concurrency and parallelism and this will help a lot) -unfamiliar syntax (not Algol like) and paradigm (not oop)- and the truth is many programmers back away slowly when a thing is too alien -hasn’t found a niche as big as data science or scientific computing or CRUD website building - I think python has aggregated some great academic niches, at least one of which (ML/data science) exploded in popularity. Ruby had the rails community. Clojure seems to have some popularity in fintech but has no big single niche yet that it dominates."},{"id":37315183,"author":"ah27182","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Why Did Python Win?","stance":"rec","why":"Lays out history of popular languages and why Python won.","quote":"the general history of popular programming languages, were well laid out in this talk","text":"I felt that some of your questions, as well as the general history of popular programming languages, were well laid out in this talk: [VIDEO] TL;DW: The presenter mentions that Python experienced a slow but consistent growth trend, leading to its current prominence. Other points he highlighted about its success align with much of the sentiment expressed here."},{"id":33630445,"author":"senand","month":"2022-11","story":"What’s so great about functional programming anyway?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why imperative languages are more popular, not better.","quote":"I recommend the following video titled \"Why isn't Functional Programming the Norm?","text":"That doesn't mean the imperative languages are \"better\", though, just more popular. I recommend the following video titled \"Why isn't Functional Programming the Norm?\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":31198232,"author":"randito","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: The Crystal programming language – what are your impressions?","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses why programming languages succeed.","quote":"There's a great talk","text":"There's a great talk -- [VIDEO] -- Why Isn't Functional Programming the Norm? – Richard Feldman -- that goes into this. It's a discussion of \"why\" programming languages succeed."},{"id":28520187,"author":"rg111","month":"2021-09","story":"Functional programming is not popular because it is weird (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Learned that Oracle spent $500 million promoting Java.","quote":"I learned in that talk, among other things that Oracle spent $500 million to promote and market Java.","text":"I recently watched this talk- Why Isn't Functional Programming the Norm? ( [VIDEO] ) Here's the HN thread- [LINK] I learned in that talk, among other things that Oracle spent $500 million to promote and market Java. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":25466963,"author":"blunte","month":"2020-12","story":"Casey Muratori on getting rid of OOP mindset [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Plausible explanations for why imperative programming is more common than functional.","quote":"This video gives some plausible explanations as to why imperative programming is more common than functional","text":"Keep in mind that what may seem like \"personality fit\" may be more related to \"learned imperative programming first\". As an analogy, depending on which spoken language a person learned first (as a child, _automatically), learning some new languages may be particularly difficult or quite natural. If we had all learned functional programming concepts first, our minds would be trained to think that way. We all learned functional (declarative) math, and we don't even think about it as such. It's just math. This video gives some plausible explanations as to why imperative programming is more common than functional: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24357583,"author":"agentultra","month":"2020-09","story":"Why is OOP still so widely spread?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on why languages go mainstream via network effects.","quote":"Richard Feldman gave an interesting talk on this in 2019 [0].","text":"Richard Feldman gave an interesting talk on this in 2019 [0]. He was trying to answer why functional programming isn't mainstream but the opposite is what we're posing here and I think it's for many of the same reasons. Network effects : a lot of these languages were mainstream earlier on and got buy in from a lot of folks who are invested in their continued adoption and success. Platform exclusivity : some companies push their own language ecosystems on their platforms and those languages are often OOP/multi-paradigm languages. Specifically to OOP we have to ask why is the particular style of C++/Java OOP the norm? Why didn't Eiffel, Smalltalk, or Self reach the same market? [0] [VIDEO] edit : forgot the link -- oops."},{"id":22148239,"author":"agentultra","month":"2020-01","story":"Haskell Problems for a New Decade","stance":"rec","why":"Explains FP's lack of adoption through network effects and platform exclusivity.","quote":"There's a great talk on why functional programming is not the norm","text":"> If in the next fifty years more than, say, 3% of programmers will be programming in a language like Lean, I will eat my hat. You're not even putting any stakes on the table. That's such a safe bet. It's unlikely you'd even have a chance at losing the odds are so stacked. There's a great talk on why functional programming is not the norm: [VIDEO] The short of it is: network effects . Haskell, OCaml, SML... they've never had the benefit of being the exclusive language to a popular platform like the web, macOS, or UNIX. They've not yet had a killer app like Rails or Numpy/SciPy/PyTorch. They've certainly not been foisted on the market with a war chest of marketing dollars like Java and C#. And they're not an incremental improvement on the status quo with an obvious, easy benefit. > Now, try to explain to a programmer what kind of object id is That's a rhetorical exercise. You can take a random snippet of APL or Fortran and the outcome would be the same. Every language requires training and effort to learn. I teach people Haskell and TLA+. If you think teaching people the value of a language like Haskell is hard you should see the blank stares I get on the first day teaching experienced developers TLA+. Interested folks will come around eventually and have their moment but it takes training and dedication to get there. > was chosen not with the best interests of programmers in mind I'm well aware. I converse regularly with the maintainers of mathlib and the community. I know th"},{"id":21859628,"author":"huy-nguyen","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues FP is not the norm for reasons other than merit.","quote":"Spoiler: not on the basis of merits.","text":"“Why isn’t functional programming the norm?” by Richard Feldman. Spoiler: not on the basis of merits. [VIDEO] “React to the future” by Jordan Walke. Why ReasonML is a logical extension of ReactJS’ programming paradigm. [LINK] “Typing the untyped: soundness in gradual type systems” by Ben Weissmann. The trade offs that various gradual type systems make based on their language constraints. [LINK] “Let’s program like it’s 1999” by Lee Byron. How the mutual feedback loop of abstraction, syntax and mental model drives the evolution of web technologies. [LINK]"},{"id":21460183,"author":"fulafel","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Why is functional programming not more popular?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues OOP became popular accidentally because of C++.","quote":"Richard Feldman puts up a (well founded IMO) argument that OO became accidentally popular because of C++.","text":"In the market of ideas, there usually isn't any reason that makes the good ideas unpopular. That's the default outcome. Some good ideas eventually make it to mainstream / conventional wisdom because there's some second factor that brings them to the spotlight. For languages it might be a killer app, big corporate investment, etc. In [VIDEO] Richard Feldman puts up a (well founded IMO) argument that OO became accidentally popular because of C++. C++ didn't do OO well, nor was OO the main benefit of C++, it just happened to be something that came with C++."},{"id":21306694,"author":"quickthrower2","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: Why are private/hidden methods so popular in OOP APIs?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk about programming languages in general.","quote":"I recommend watching it for some interesting talk about programming languages in general","text":"I know the answer, thanks to watching a recent video by Richard Feldman on why functional programming isn’t popular yet. I recommend watching it for some interesting talk about programming languages in general, it’s on YouTube at [VIDEO] Anyway what privates give you is what everyone wants from a programming language: modularity. We want to be able to expose an api to some code and for it to have some inner workings that we don’t see because: 1. If we know they can’t be touched we have some implicit guarantees about our program. E.g. the mouse pointer cannot go off screen even if we try to make a call to do that. 2. It is simpler for a programmer to understand the api which might be 1% of the size of the module itself with all private’s. 3. I can change my private’s without breaking your calls. Essentially a minor version change in Semvar parlance instead of a major breaking change. 4. Make impossible state impossible! By guarding the state. All these things require modularity, and OO uses classes and private members to provide that modularity. Elm uses modules and the exposing keyword (or lack of!) as a contrast. JS modules are similar with a lack of an export allowing you to hide something. That something you did export can use for state or functionality. There is an old IIFE trick that works this way also. Making everything public is a bad idea, as you miss out on modularity advantages. Not sure how Ruby hides things as I haven’t used it for a while but I’m sure there is a"},{"id":39143513,"author":"agentultra","month":"2024-01","story":"If Lisp is so great","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on language adoption and network effects.","quote":"Also related","text":"Also related: [VIDEO] Network effects likely explain a big part of it. Many of the \"popular\" languages today aren't necessarily great languages and adopted for their technical merits. Often they were the only language available on an exclusive platform everyone wanted to develop software on. Other times it was because folks wanted to write a particular kind of application and there just happened to be this framework in this weird language that made the task easy. We probably have more programming languages now than ever before but I think the market for new languages in the mainstream is relatively static. It shifts... but very slowly."},{"id":36892389,"author":"Leftium","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: What do FP languages have that JavaScript doesn't?","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote on FP features borrowed by non-FP languages.","quote":"","text":"FP is a way of thinking, which FP languages give nice features/syntax for. You can do non-FP style programming with an FP language. In fact, many FP language features have been borrowed by non-FP languages to the point people don't consider them \"FP\" anymore[0]. (Like JS `.map()` and `.reduce()`. Even Excel macros now support first-class functions[1].) Three features that would make FP thinking in JS easier: 1. immutable tuples and records[2] 2. pipe operator[3] 3. pattern matching[4] Major epiphany: According to Grokking Simplicity[5], good functional programming isn't about avoiding impure functions; instead it's about giving extra care to them. Impure functions depend on the time they are called, so they are the most difficult to get right. In the end, the purpose of all software is to cause some type of mutation/effect (flip pixels on a screen, save bits to storage, send email, etc). \"Functional Core, Imperative Shell\"[6] is probably the most useful FP concept. It is about pushing mutation/errors/impure functions to the outer edges of your code, so the inner code can focus on the \"happy path.\" Complex business logic lives in the inner core; the outer shell is often simple/branchless so unit tests aren't even needed. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK] [6]: [LINK]"},{"id":36150667,"author":"Leftium","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: Where did you first come across functional programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting claim FP features were borrowed by other languages.","quote":"","text":"FP didn't really start to \"click\" until I found \"Functional Core, Imperative Shell\"[1] last year (2022). The HN comments from that thread were very helpful, too. I didn't really grok FP until 2022, but I learned Scheme as early as 1999 for programming 101 (comp sci degree). We used the SICP textbook; looking back it seems SICP used an FP language to teach programming, but didn't explore some important FP concepts like Option/Either (a.k.a. Monads. Perhaps our class just didn't cover that part of the book?) Between 1999 and 2022, I dabbled in FP because I heard good things about it. But I think many FP practitioners could improve their marketing/teaching skills. Most FP texts seem to dive into mathematical jargon (monads, functors, etc) without even explaining why knowing these would be useful. So I'm writing a series of articles on FP. The first one is on why FP is worth learning: \"More Performant, Testable Code with Functional Programming. (FP language optional!)\"[2] The next article will be a curated list of articles/videos that I feel explain FP better than most; the ones I wish I had found way back when starting to dabble in FP. Also there's an argument many of FP's useful features have been copied by more imperative languages[3]. So people may be unwittingly doing FP even if they aren't using an FP language. For example, anonymous first class functions have been added to C++ and even Excel. JS has always had anonymous first class functions. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: ["},{"id":33436198,"author":"agentultra","month":"2022-11","story":"Functional programming should be the future of software","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Maybe it ought to be but it definitely isn't if you look at success as industry adoption. The author seems to have good intentions and covers all the talking points a new convert will discover on their own. However I'm afraid an article like this will do more harm than good in the end. There are too many network effects in play that go against a new paradigm supplanting the mainstream as it is. And the benefits of functional programming pointed out in this article haven't been convincing over the last... many decades. Without large, industry success stories to back it up I'm afraid any amount of evangelism, however good the intention of the author, is going to fall before skeptical minds. It doesn't help that of the few empirical studies done none have shown any impressive results that hold up these claims. Granted those studies are few and far between and inconclusive at best but that won't stop skeptics from using them as ammunition. For me the real power of functional programming is that I can use mathematical reasoning on my programs and get results. It's just the way my brain works. I don't think it's superior or better than procedural, imperative programming. And heck there are some problem domains where I can't get away from thinking in a non-functional programming way. I think the leap to structured programming was an event that is probably only going to happen once in our industry. Aside from advances in multi-core programming, which we've barely seen in the last cou"},{"id":29826342,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2022-01","story":"“I am literally losing sleep” over Java (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of why languages become popular; Java's marketing spend.","quote":"It was an accident of history that OOP became so popular.","text":"Richard Feldman (Elm) analyzes why programming languages become popular and says Java is unique. It became popular because Sun spent 100's of millions of dollars promoting it, even after the dot com crash. Others like Ruby, had a killer app. His thesis is object oriented programming wasn't inevitable or is the best way to program. It was an accident of history that OOP became so popular. Why Isn't Functional Programming the Norm? – Richard Feldman [VIDEO] Another Java tidbit is that Miguel de Icaza (Gnome, Mono) really wanted an open source higher level language on Linux so he approached Sun about Java and they wouldn't work with him, so he settled on C# and is probably the man most responsible for the move to cross platform C# and .NET (dotnetcore, v5) [LINK]"},{"id":21911613,"author":"6700417","month":"2019-12","story":"Show HN: A pure functional programming language targeting decentralized systems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, any new language needs to start somewhere, but it’s interesting to think we need new languages for existing domains and that they have any chance of success to begin with. Consider that by many accounts JavaScript, Java, and Python basically dominate in terms of number of developers and mindshare. These languages all arrived 25-30 years ago. I know some rankings claim C/C++ are far more popular than JavaScript but I just haven’t seen evidence of that in recent years. Other widely used languages include C, Objective-C, C++, C# and Ruby. The first three are older than the aforementioned languages, and Ruby is from the same era as Java/JavaScript, and C# isn’t far behind that. In terms of newer languages, the only ones I can think of offhand that seem to have serious traction are Swift and to a lesser extent Go. Some people might throw Kotlin in there, but I’m not sure how much actual traction it’s getting. Rust has been gaining traction slowly for ~8+ years and I’m hopeful it has a bright future but I don’t see a lot of actual projects using it today. The point is that it’s really hard to succeed if your primary reason to exist is to make existing practice slightly better. It’s very hard to displace any of the existing languages in the space of general purpose programming. You need to have libraries. You need to have at least syntax coloring support for several editors. You need language server support for VS Code. You need to generate Dwarf. You probably need a repl or we"}],"r":[["OB-bdWKwXsU",1],["Ee3EtYb8d1o",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["-4Yp3j_jk8Q",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["0Cu6EbELZ6I",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["APZCfmgzoS0",1],["C25qzDhGLx8",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["vK1DazRK_a0",1],["5fG_lyNuEAw",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1]]},"jVkLVRt6c1U":{"wy":"HN regulars send freelancers and consultants to it whenever clients don't pay or contracts come up.","q":{"x":"I watched this video from Mike Monteiro and I always refer to it since.","i":20134911,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":26937962,"author":"Mandatum","month":"2021-04","story":"Job descriptions should show a salary or salary range","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk about not getting paid in creative services.","quote":"this talk by Mike Monteiro is awesome","text":"Fuck you, pay me for those of you uninitiated, this talk by Mike Monteiro is awesome - about not getting paid in creative services: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20134911,"author":"Improvotter","month":"2019-06","story":"I didn't get paid, so I open-sourced my client’s project","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk about getting paid for work done.","quote":"I watched this video from Mike Monteiro and I always refer to it since.","text":"I watched this video from Mike Monteiro and I always refer to it since. It's about getting paid for the work you do. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13026384,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2016-11","story":"Freelance Isn't Free Act Passes in NYC","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous talk on getting paid, with attorney section on contracts.","quote":"You really need to watch this (very famous) talk by Mike Monteiro","text":"You really need to watch this (very famous) talk by Mike Monteiro, called \"Fuck You, Pay Me\": [VIDEO] Especially the section where his attorney starts talking (at about 7:20)."},{"id":13026528,"author":"MrFoof","month":"2016-11","story":"Freelance Isn't Free Act Passes in NYC","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows value of a lawyer-drafted contract for getting paid.","quote":"That $1250 makes me money","text":"Please watch this: Fuck You, Pay Me: [VIDEO] The $1250 I spent once to have a business attorney draft up a template for my services agreement is the best $1250 I ever spent in the past 7 years. That $1250 makes me money , because I never have to harass clients to get paid, because they know I have legal recourse if they don't. Since then I've maybe paid him $1000 to deal with things clients wanted to redline in my service agreement. That $2250 total (which is a legitimate business expense) is a pittance compared to what I would lose for a single unpaid invoice. Meanwhile, I always hear from freelancers that don't get paid, but claim lawyers are \"too expensive\". You know what's really expensive? Getting $0 for the last N days you worked instead of $X. $X is a hell of a lot more money, and unless you have a contract in place, you expose yourself to that possibility every time you engage with a client."},{"id":43640307,"author":"SamuelAdams","month":"2025-04","story":"No Pay, No Work; Early Career Lessons","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on getting paid for creative work.","quote":"I also recommend watching this talk if you have not seen it already.","text":"I also recommend watching this talk if you have not seen it already. Mike Monteiro: Fuck you, pay me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40944084,"author":"ClearAndPresent","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Freelancer's Dilemma – Client Won't Pay Despite Clear Agreement","stance":"rec","why":"Mindset for dealing with non-paying clients.","quote":"As a palette cleanser and loin girder","text":"As a palette cleanser and loin girder: [VIDEO] (You already guessed it was Mike Monteiro.) For this project: Did they refuse to pay actively (\"We won't pay you.\"), or passively (No replies to emails.) The latter might be incompetence. As others mentioned, specific legal advice depends on respective jurisdictions, but an offical lawyer's letter sometimes wakes up legal when accounting is drifting. Did you deliver the final access to the code / server / credentials? If not, gently bring servers, DBs, platforms to a halt pending final payments. Future projects: Contract, always. Up-front commencement payments, always. Price yourself higher in the initial quote, and ask for staged payments, such that by the time your delivery schedule is 50-75% done you are 100% paid. You control the source code accounts, you control the server credentials, you control the database access credentials, you hand over final access after the final check has cleared."},{"id":40944227,"author":"Zealotux","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Freelancer's Dilemma – Client Won't Pay Despite Clear Agreement","stance":"rec","why":"Gets freelancers into the mindset of protecting their work.","quote":"A classic video that might help you get in the right mindset","text":"Oh you just revived sweet memories of my times as a freelancer. As a freelancer, it's sometimes hard to get around the simple idea that you are your own company and that you must act like one: protect and defend your work at all cost. It took me months of time wasted, and thousands of dollars of work stolen to accept the fact I need to change my behaviour, as a freelancer you have roughly two approaches: - set boundaries early on, don't start any work with a predefined and agreed upon down payment, if the work is more than a few days long: set milestones as need be with payments required. - lawyer up, be flexible with clients (delays in payments are a common occurrence with any company) but don't be afraid to go after dishonest clients, if you can't afford it or don't feel like going this path then you need to set up as many contingencies as possible upfront (see previous point). A classic video that might help you get in the right mindset: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39318087,"author":"morphle","month":"2024-02","story":"I'm an engineer that needs to sell my services. Any good books on sales?","stance":"rec","why":"Advice for consultants: make contracts with a proper lawyer.","quote":"Very good advice for consultants and freelancers","text":"Very good advice for consultants and freelancers: [VIDEO] TL;DR: Make good consulting contracts with a proper lawyer"},{"id":28500503,"author":"somedangedname","month":"2021-09","story":"The Magic Behind the Scenes of Docker Desktop","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on valuing your labor and demanding payment.","quote":"Also watch this video that was doing the rounds on HN a couple of years ago","text":"You give people something cool for years at no cost and now they're pissed that they have to compensate you for your labor. The way that people value software these days is completely insane. People writing you hate mail are bad clients that you don't want and you can feel free to ignore them. Also watch this video that was doing the rounds on HN a couple of years ago: [VIDEO] Your app looks neat - I'll go try it out."},{"id":15160525,"author":"quanticle","month":"2017-09","story":"Fucking Sue Me (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"How to negotiate outdated boilerplate contract terms using a lawyer.","quote":"He says that a lot of companies have boilerplate contracts which have outdated provisions","text":"That really depends; some company's legal department might go overboard with the stuff they make you sign 'or no deal'. Mike Monteiro talks about this in his \"Fuck you, pay me\" talk [1]. He says that a lot of companies have boilerplate contracts which have outdated provisions like requiring you to hand over printouts of source code, or delivering software on floppy diskettes. The proper response is to have a lawyer yourself who can review the contract and explain to the company's legal counsel that 1) these contract provisions are unreasonable and outdated and 2) that striking these provisions will not create additional liabilities for the company. The reasons these contracts go overboard is because every time the company gets burned, it adds some scar tissue to the contract to prevent that same eventually from occurring again, regardless of whether it's applicable to the task at hand. Maybe it works different in different parts of the world, but we signed some crazy things (which were dealbreakers if not signed) in the past and no-one ever sued us for it I will be extremely honest. You got lucky. You put your head in the mouth of the lion, and it didn't bite. It doesn't mean that what you did was a good idea, it just means that you got away with it. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15162735,"author":"gravypod","month":"2017-09","story":"Fucking Sue Me (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Professional approach to contracts, payments and negotiation.","quote":"a reasoned explanation of negotiations, what to expect, and reasonable ways to react/behave","text":"This is some bad advice because there are people who will \"Fucking sue [you]\". If you want to learn about how a professional deals with contracts, payments, and contracting I'd say to check out \"Fuck you. Pay me\" [0] that gives a reasoned explanation of negotiations, what to expect, and reasonable ways to react/behave. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":9797790,"author":"wwkeyboard","month":"2015-06","story":"Ask HN: I'm owed 8k by a startup that's going under. What do I do?","stance":"rec","why":"Practical advice on getting paid by clients.","quote":"has a lot of good information","text":"It's a little late for you, but Mike Monteiro's \"Fuck you, pay me!\" talk has a lot of good information. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47639518,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-04","story":"Ask HN: How do you handle clients who don't pay on time?","stance":"reference","why":"Famous talk about clients who don't pay.","quote":"Reminder of Mike Monteiro's famous 2011 talk","text":"Reminder of Mike Monteiro's famous 2011 talk, \"Fuck You, Pay Me\": * [VIDEO] Which even Adam Savage (of Mythbusters fame) references: * [LINK]"},{"id":47661491,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2026-04","story":"What Being Ripped Off Taught Me","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Be paid or don't work. As an electrician currently self-stopped (for both non-payment & absenteeism, two months no contact , so far) I'm not budging. When you didn't show up for our last two on-site meetups (and still haven't contacted), I thought about filing a lien... but decided to just keep you from having tenants (by not finishing AC/water/electric). You'll get around to it..? When I told this LLC/owner \"you obviously aren't in a rush because you obviously aren't visiting the jobsite/me\" I sort of expected him to show within a few days. Then a few weeks. And now we're entering a few months. You have weeks of my punchlists (unresolved to do ), I have weeks of questions (what do you want ?) and you won't even do simple things like turning on power/water. Fuck you, pay me. [•] < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":47052646,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-02","story":"Can a Computer Science Student Be Taught to Design Hardware?","stance":"reference","why":"Funny, accurate career advice about not working for free.","quote":"Advice to students that is funny and accurate","text":"Hardware is artificially underpaid work, good positions are sparse in the US, and generally most engineers end up in niche coding environments. Most people that land a successful long career, also refuse to solve some clown firms ephemeral problems at a loss. The trend of externalizing costs onto perspective employees starts to fail in difficult fields requiring actual domain talent with $3.7m per seat equipment. Regulatory capture also fails in advanced areas, as large firms regress into state sponsored thievery instead. Advice to students that is funny and accurate =3 \"Mike Monteiro: F*ck You, Pay Me\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44011796,"author":"username223","month":"2025-05","story":"A Research Preview of Codex","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Does this mean people will be less incentivized to contribute to open source as time goes by? Yes. I certainly don't intend to put any free code online until I can legally bar AI bros from using it without payment. As Mike Monteiro put it long ago, \"F** you, pay me\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":42252948,"author":"killjoywashere","month":"2024-11","story":"Artificial Intelligence and the Future of Work","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on clients refusing to pay for creative work.","quote":"Who here has, at some time, had trouble getting paid by a client for work they were doing?","text":"> pro-free-cultural moral views Mike Montero would like a word \"Who in this room is now, or has at some time, been in creative services? \"Who here has, at some time, had trouble getting paid by a client for work they were doing? \"Raise your hand if any of these are familiar to you: \"'We ended up not using the work.' \"'It's really not what we wanted after all.' \"Alright. Who's familiar with Goodfellas ? \"Alright. 'We got somebody internal to do it instead.' \"'Fuck you. Pay me.'\" \"'Fuck you. Pay me.'\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32866045,"author":"rocket_surgeron","month":"2022-09","story":"YouTube ads appear to be ramping up, with as many as 10 unskippable ads","stance":"reference","why":"Invoked as slogan for paying creators.","quote":"","text":"People who don’t pay (or watch ads) are no different from the greedy dickheads who offer to pay artists and developers in “exposure”. Ether pay, or don’t use. Fuck You, Pay Me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11088618,"author":"davismwfl","month":"2016-02","story":"Ask HN: Slow paying client and how do you deal with them?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on getting paid by slow clients.","quote":"","text":"I have lived this many times over. You will always have clients like this, these are the best ways I have found so far. 1: do not give net 30 day terms unless a client has earned them or applied via credit. Some exceptions can be made for large firms, but many small to medium businesses are terrible about paying. Large businesses might be a little slow, but almost always pay consistently the same, unless there is a known issue. 2: If you do give net 30 terms (or anything more then 10 days), provide a discount for payment within 10 days. This makes almost all larger businesses prioritize your invoice and get you paid. You may think it is minor, but many businesses do this over hundreds or sometimes thousands of invoices and they save significant amounts of money doing so. 3: Make payment details clear within your TOS/contract. For my smaller consulting clients, if their invoice was unpaid after 3 days from the due date I stopped all work and they lost their spot in line for services. So essentially most of those clients had 7-10 days to pay me or I halted all work. I also had it up front that if that happened 2 times within a short period (defined in the contract) that all future work (if accepted) would be prepaid. This was part of my non-negotiable contract terms. For my SaaS product, I used similar terms, we billed their credit card for small/mid sized clients (generally under $2k/month) and if their card was denied or we were unable to collect that months service after 5 d"},{"id":9760366,"author":"jackmaney","month":"2015-06","story":"Business Can Pay to Train Its Own Work Force","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on insisting on payment for creative work.","quote":"","text":"> if a business invests $20k in training and $80k in salary, there is nothing stopping another employer from offering $90k in salary after training is complete. Then that business will have to pay better. You want me to stick around? Fuck you, pay me[1] (or give me some other reason that is compelling--to me, not to you--to stay). [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["awADEuv5vWY",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["QTJRwKOFddc",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["Gj8IA6xOpSk",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["lKhAd0Tyny0",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["j2Mu8qfVb5I",1],["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1]]},"ZuvK-od647c":{"wy":"HN recommends it as one of the clearest layman explanations of Bell's theorem.","q":{"x":"It's my favorite video for explaining how we know that the state is not determined until it has been measured.","i":10586532,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10586532,"author":"gibybo","month":"2015-11","story":"The quantum source of space-time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows why quantum state is undetermined until measured.","quote":"It's my favorite video for explaining how we know that the state is not determined until it has been measured.","text":"Check out [VIDEO] It's my favorite video for explaining how we know that the state is not determined until it has been measured. It's not just a decision we thought up on paper. It is real and well proven."},{"id":10205076,"author":"slaxman","month":"2015-09","story":"Informative YouTube channels","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains a difficult quantum topic in under ten minutes.","quote":"I can't recommend Veritasium and CGP Grey enough.","text":"I can't recommend Veritasium and CGP Grey enough. They are amazing YouTube Channels! Check out this video where Veritasium explains Quantum Entanglement. [VIDEO] It amazes me how he explains something so difficult in less than 10 minutes!"},{"id":33078503,"author":"GolDDranks","month":"2022-10","story":"Nobel Prize in Physics Awarded to Alain Aspect, John Clauser and Anton Zeilinger","stance":"rec","why":"Easily digestible explanation that Bell rules out hidden variables.","quote":"Here's an easily digestible video about this","text":"You understood correctly that quantum mechanics cannot be based on some hidden variable we haven't or cannot find. (Edit: oops, I re-read your comment and it seems you say the exact opposite thing? The point is that Bell's theorem rules hidden variables OUT. Here's an easily digestible video about this: [VIDEO] ) About probability: no one knows. It might be that there is a \"wave function collapse\" that has a probabilistic outcome. But it might be that there is no such a thing. Quantum mechanics that remain in the \"quantum\" realm are not probabilistic, it's only when you cross over to the classical world. So it might be equally well, that the Everett interpretation (so-called \"many worlds interpretation\") is true, and the probability is something physicist Sean Carroll calls \"self-locating uncertainty\": [LINK] That is, there are multiple \"yous\" that experience different outcomes, and you don't know which branch of the wavefunction you find \"yourself\" in, which \"you\" you are."},{"id":32044810,"author":"rhinokungfoo","month":"2022-07","story":"Record-setting quantum entanglement connects two atoms across 20 miles","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of Bell's inequality.","quote":"Veritasium has a good video explaining Bell's inequality","text":"Your example is akin to the Hidden variable theory (in the example the hidden variable will be the fact the two letters are copies). Bell's inequality tests have ruled out the hidden variable theory. Veritasium has a good video explaining Bell's inequality - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19406161,"author":"chii","month":"2019-03","story":"Experimental rejection of observer-independence in the quantum world","stance":"rec","why":"Less technical, more visual layman's explanation of Bell's result.","quote":"actually, this is a much better video","text":"> it's both simply because it hasn't been measured ... Meaning it's either one or the other A common misconception about quantum superposition is that the particle \"has\" some state, and we only use superposition to model that we \"dont know\". This is called the hidden-variable theory ( [LINK] ). It's been proven that this hidden variable theory can't be true because of [LINK] . This is completely non-intuitive. See this video for a laymen's explanation: [LINK] (you may also need to watch the prev. video about the EPR paradox [LINK] ). Edit: actually, this is a much better video [VIDEO] - less technical and more visual."},{"id":16307335,"author":"CookieMon","month":"2018-02","story":"You thought quantum mechanics was weird: check out entangled time","stance":"rec","why":"Nine-minute explanation of how Bell ruled out hidden information.","quote":"this 9 minute video explains how Bell proved that the particles can't have been given opposite","text":"If you watch for more than 4 minutes, this 9 minute video explains how Bell proved that the particles can't have been given opposite \"ball colors\" at the time they became entangled. [VIDEO] It's quite clever how he proved it, and that it's actually possible to rule out \"information that we're just not aware of\"."},{"id":13351913,"author":"strmpnk","month":"2017-01","story":"Bell's Theorem: No EPR “Reality”","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation using spin measurements with three detectors.","quote":"One of the better explanations I've seen is regarding measuring spin with three state detectors","text":"I don't think that explanation is clear. It takes a bit more work to get something which proves the experimental difference. One of the better explanations I've seen is regarding measuring spin with three state detectors: [VIDEO] This would give specific probabilities to certain outcomes (not just 50/50) IF there were a local hidden variable. (Note: the word local is important there. It means information encoded and carried by or with the particles themselves.)"},{"id":12299218,"author":"blueplanet200","month":"2016-08","story":"Interactive explanation of the quantum Bell test","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of Bell's inequality.","quote":"is a good overview that I really like for Bell's inequality.","text":"[VIDEO] is a good overview that I really like for Bell's inequality. Worth a watch if you have the time."},{"id":9053934,"author":"gbelote","month":"2015-02","story":"Quantum Physics and the Abuse of Reason","stance":"rec","why":"Explains a Bell test experiment in non-physicist terms.","quote":"There's a pretty good Veritasium video that explains one such experiment in non-physicist terms","text":"If quantum-entangled particles exist (they do) you can use them to coordinate in a way that's impossible within a \"hidden variable\" model of the universe. You can dig into this by searching for \"bell's inequality\" or \"bell test experiments\", there are a few different experiments that have been done many, many times. There's a pretty good Veritasium video that explains one such experiment in non-physicist terms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10431091,"author":"MichaelApproved","month":"2015-10","story":"Loophole-free Bell test ‘Spooky action at a distance’, no cheating","stance":"reference","why":"Video on measuring quantum particles that left lingering questions.","quote":"I saw this great Veritasium video about measuring quantum particles","text":"So, then is it correct to say that an outside observer can never tell if a particle he/she is looking at is actually entangled? This outside observer would simply observe that the particle has spin \"up\" or \"down\" but would never know that it was entangled earlier by someone else. This observer would never know it was in a superposition unless they had knowledge that someone had previously done this. Only the person who entangled the particle can clue the observer in on the fact that they were entangled. I can give you a particle, tell you it's in a superposition but you'd have no way to verify this. If you observe the particle, perhaps you'd see it's in spin \"up\" but that would be meaningless because that observation wouldn't tell you anything about whether it was entangled or not. You only know about its current state. There is nothing in your observation that shows \"hey the particle just changed from superposition to spin up\" Thanks for the replies! I watch \"word science festival\" and similar videos and I am fascinated by the quantum world but I have wondered these questions for a while and didn't know anyone who could answer them. I saw this great Veritasium video about measuring quantum particles but it still left me with the questions above [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["rFqL9CkNxXw",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["nvK8fua6O64",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["YGLNyHd2w10",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["o_4EX4dPppA",1]]},"I8Xc2_FtpHI":{"wy":"Several HN commenters credit the lecture series with helping them through depression or personal collapse and recommend it as long-form learning.","q":{"x":"this series genuinely helped me put myself back together after my ego was shattered","i":32335016,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32335016,"author":"8vectors","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Will I Ever Recover?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helped recover after a startup failure","quote":"this series genuinely helped me put myself back together after my ego was shattered","text":"Contrary to popular belief, the highest chance of success, and largest entrepreneurial successes, come from founders who are 47 years old when they start their companies. You are still 10 years away from the statistical \"peak\" to start your startup. Yes you will recover. This experience may be exactly what you need to become great. It's your choice to either hide from it and whither away in regret or face it head on and extract all the value it has to offer. Growth comes from painful reflection on our mistakes. Studying Stoicism and this lecture series really helped me get through the failure of my startup (Which I spent 7 years building and then had to sell for $1). [VIDEO] Note the lecturer is controversial politically now... but this series genuinely helped me put myself back together after my ego was shattered - I am way better off now (and more resilient) and years later building another company which is doing great so far."},{"id":25013737,"author":"Yan-solucracy","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: How to fix expectations and set up path for life?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helps understand how belief systems make sense of life","quote":"it won't solve everything but it will enable you to understand how to make sense out of life","text":"Watch Jordan Peterson's Maps of meaning course : [VIDEO] , it won't solve everything but it will enable you to understand how to make sense out of life, and how your belief system works, so you can make it work for you."},{"id":23251870,"author":"dagav","month":"2020-05","story":"Surviving Depression","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helped turn around depression and gave balance","quote":"I cannot overstate the profound effect they had on my life","text":"I highly recommend you listen to these lecture series. I cannot overstate the profound effect they had on my life, by giving me the tools I needed to turn my depression around and give me a sense of balance and control in my life. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20057000,"author":"RickS","month":"2019-05","story":"New Insights into Hikikomori – People Who Withdraw from Society for Years on End","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture series that helped recovery from depression","quote":"I have to give a lot of credit to a lecture series I was recommended on HN as well","text":"I know you're not looking for advice, so I'm not going to preach too hard at you, except to say that I recognize the somber lucidity of your tone, and I've been there. Depression's heaviest weapon is that it feels like the heaviest truth. It's a bit like being in a room within a room. If nothing outside can be perceived from within the walls, does it matter that it exists? I was surprised when I got out – I was sure it couldn't happen to me either. I didn't even realize it was happening to me until months in. Lots of things that I thought were concrete facts about myself and the world turned out to be configurable in ways I was ill equipped to predict until they happened. There was no secret or epiphany or \"one weird trick\". I mostly did this by pretending as if being were worthwhile. I wasn't interested in anything that happy, normal people were interested in, like meeting new people or wearing clean clothes. But my crude mental model of those people had enough fidelity to imitate them, at least somewhat. So I did, in earnest. Eventually the mask became the face. We are what we repeatedly do. I lost about 6 years before I started doing that, and it took about another 6 to 9 months before I noticed that anything had changed. I have to give a lot of credit to a lecture series I was recommended on HN as well: Maps of Meaning, from Jordan Peterson: [VIDEO] It's weird stuff. Took some getting through. I don't remember what the first guy said that made me stick with it, but I'm gl"},{"id":16050094,"author":"nicolashahn","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Archetypal analysis using Pinocchio, more impactful than the Bible series","quote":"found his 2017 Maps of Meaning lectures more impactful, personally.","text":"I like these ones (and even having attended Catholic high school, it was the first piece of media that made me actually realize how much human wisdom could be embedded in religious texts), but found his 2017 Maps of Meaning lectures more impactful, personally. He uses his archetypal lens on Pinocchio instead of Genesis for the first few lectures. There's a lot of content overlap but if you liked the Biblical series, definitely check out Maps of Meaning. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21658982,"author":"kleer001","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Why the Hate?","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form learning on why negativity and hate are adaptive","quote":"I can recommend over 120 hours of learning in the following","text":"Because the world, for the most part, is (and always has been) a dangerous place and hate has proven to be a very useful (highly conserved through evolutionary time) adaptation to help keep small and delicate bands of our ancestors safe for one more day. You can see this bias towards negativity easily in the structure of basic emotions. There is one positive affect (happiness) and waaaaay more negative affects (fear, anger, disgust, sadness) and only one neutral (surprise). And those negative emotions are all very distinct. If you want more detail I can recommend over 120 hours of learning in the following (which I've watched and digested). They'll walk you through the details from the bottom to the top. - Watch Robert Sapolsky's lectures: [LINK] Watch Jordan Peterson's Personality lectures: [LINK] and his Maps of Meaning Lectures: (or read the book or listen to the audio book) [VIDEO]"},{"id":16195631,"author":"nicolashahn","month":"2018-01","story":"What’s So Dangerous About Jordan Peterson?","stance":"rec","why":"Archetypes in stories like Pinocchio, palatable to non-religious viewers","quote":"I also found his 2017 Maps of Meaning lectures particularly interesting, and personally more relevant","text":"For those interested in his non-gender identity content: His series on the Bible: [LINK] is generally regarded (by those I know, at least) as Jordan at his best. He goes through the first few stories in the Bible and tries to bring meaning to them, not from the fundamental Christian lens of \"this is absolute truth,\" but more like: \"Here are some archetypal patterns about the world and how to act in it that our ancestors have discovered over thousands of years and distilled into stories. Maybe they hold truth that isn't the 'word of God' truth, but observations about the world that have utility to us even as a modern, rational, scientific civilization. I'll back this up by tying it to studies on psychology, neuroscience, evolution, and animal behavior, and show that variations of the same archetypal stories appear in non-Judeo-Christian societies all over the world.\" You can be a hardline atheist and still get tons of value out of these lectures. I also found his 2017 Maps of Meaning lectures particularly interesting, and personally more relevant: [VIDEO] He does a similar thing with the story of Pinocchio instead of the Bible for the first few lectures, which might be more palatable for some. There's a lot of overlap with the Bible series as well."},{"id":14730011,"author":"diyseguy","month":"2017-07","story":"Ask HN: Which sites you visit on a regular basis for knowledge and inspiration?","stance":"reference","why":"Jordan Peterson lectures being binge-watched","quote":"lately I've been binge watching the Jordan Peterson lectures","text":"news.ycombinator.com though I wish it had a way to collapse uninteresting conversations. Stephen Colbert: [LINK] and lately I've been binge watching the Jordan Peterson lectures: [VIDEO] ribbonfarm: though I usually only read the articles by Venkatesh Rao: [LINK] also bingewatching lessons on 2x from [LINK] . It used to be [LINK] but their servers are sluggish most of the time."}],"r":[["f-wWBGo6a2w",2],["kYYJlNbV1OM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["WEDIj9JBTC8",1],["UJ7Qc3bsxjI",1],["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1],["QmOF0crdyRU",1]]},"HCUct4NlxE0":{"wy":"HN treats him as a master class in salesmanship and live presentation, where how you sell matters more than what you sell.","q":{"x":"I urge you, I implore you, I beg you, stop what you're doing and watch Joe in action","i":1818405,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":8704977,"author":"patio11","month":"2014-12","story":"He sold potato peelers for $5 each and became a millionaire","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A sales pitch as performance.","quote":"You have to watch him work: it's a performance, in every sense of the word","text":"I heard about this gentleman on HN a few years ago. You have to watch him work: it's a performance, in every sense of the word: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1818405,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2010-10","story":"After 4 years of self-funded coding marathons I feel \"finished\", care to talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows what real selling looks like.","quote":"I urge you, I implore you, I beg you, stop what you're doing and watch Joe in action","text":"You seem to have been terribly misled. Only very rarely do products sell themselves. 99% of the time, the product is largely incidental to the sales process. Your idea doesn't matter one jot, what matters is how well you can connect to customers and really sell to them. Let me tell you about a fine English gentleman by the name of Joe Ades, now sadly no longer with us. Joe wore Savile Row suits and lived in a three-bedroomed apartment on Park Avenue. He spent most nights at the Café Pierre with his wife, sharing a bottle of his usual - Veuve Clicquot champagne. You might assume that Joe was a banker or an executive, but in fact Joe sold potato peelers on the street for $5 each, four for $20. I urge you, I implore you, I beg you, stop what you're doing and watch Joe in action - [VIDEO] That is what business looks like. Sometimes, once in a million, you luck upon a product so amazing the world beats a path to your door. For most of us, the best we can hope for is to be some chump with a thousand boxes of vegetable peelers. Anybody can sit out on the street with a box of peelers, but Joe sold them. Joe made his peelers sing, he made them seem like magic. He took a humble piece of stamped metal and created theatre. He did something so simple and strange and wonderful that people would buy a fistful of his peelers, just so they could tell their friends about this little Englishman they saw in Union Square. Look at the Fortune 500, tell me what you see. I see grocery stores, drugst"},{"id":1285598,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2010-04","story":"Ask HN: What makes a presentation great ?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Master of presenting and engaging a crowd.","quote":"That man has more feeling for presentation in his little finger than any amount of high flying consultants","text":"I don't think Steve Jobs is that great a presenter, Ballmer is (much) worse though. A good presentation and a good presenter are two different things. A good presenter can give a presentation about essentially boring material and make it live, be memorable. A bad presenter can turn a great presentation in to an ordeal. I've linked here once to a youtube video of Joe Ades, have a look at it: [VIDEO] The subject is 'potato peelers', from a street vendor no less. That man has more feeling for presentation in his little finger than any amount of high flying consultants that lecture on the subject. Watch the crowd he draws and how well he interacts with the people. Of course he's had years to hone his 'patter', but still, I can't imagine watching someone trying to sell a car, much less a potato peeler, and presenting is essentially a sales job. He manages to turn the mundane in to something interesting and engaging."},{"id":463912,"author":"iuguy","month":"2009-02","story":"Peeler Man, HN Salesmanship Hero, Passes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Street pitchman making a craft of selling peelers.","quote":"A really fantastic pitchman, great to watch and made an absolute craft out of his work.","text":"See the guy in action at [VIDEO] A really fantastic pitchman, great to watch and made an absolute craft out of his work."},{"id":3035405,"author":"bobds","month":"2011-09","story":"Ask HN: What can I sell in Times Square? ","stance":"rec","why":"How you sell matters more than what.","quote":"It doesn't matter what you sell. What matters is how you sell it.","text":"It doesn't matter what you sell. What matters is how you sell it. Case in point, Joe Ades with his vegetable peelers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3035415,"author":"pragmagic","month":"2011-09","story":"Ask HN: What can I sell in Times Square? ","stance":"rec","why":"Selling an experience rather than a product.","quote":"The main thing is that you sell an experience, not a product.","text":"Sell experiences. I'm not sure if you've heard of Joe Ades. He used to sell potato peelers in NY and has been mentioned on HN a few times: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I don't think it really matters what you sell, especially in this case where you just want to improve your sales technique. The main thing is that you sell an experience, not a product."},{"id":1209223,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2010-03","story":"Ask HN: What advice would you give college students about starting a business? ","stance":"rec","why":"Street sales talent worth studying.","quote":"There's a lifetime of experience in there","text":"I think one of the quickest ways to learn this is to jump in on the 'hard' end of sales, cold calls and approaching strangers. Have a look at this guy: [VIDEO] There's a lifetime of experience in there, not quite 'cold calling' but he's got amazing talent."},{"id":4795735,"author":"conover","month":"2012-11","story":"A breakdown of how I was talked out of $100","stance":"reference","why":"Similar street seller in Union Square.","quote":"","text":"A man named Joe Ades used to do something somewhat similar in Union Square. Apparently he was well known. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0fKBhvDjuy0",1],["Imquk_3oFf4",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["muCPjK4nGY4",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["BTHvs3V8DBA",1],["cfwwHa-7Ux8",1],["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["ckBfbvOXDvU",1],["n-AbPav5E5M",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1]]},"YKuRkGkf5HU":{"wy":"It is the go-to recommendation whenever someone asks about voice coding or RSI-friendly programming setups.","q":{"x":"There's a great strangeloop talk[2] demonstrating talon and the actual state of voice coding","i":34846444,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34846444,"author":"cube2222","month":"2023-02","story":"Numen: Voice Control for Handsfree Computing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demonstrates Talon and the real state of voice coding.","quote":"There's a great strangeloop talk[2] demonstrating talon and the actual state of voice coding","text":"It's worth mentioning Talon[0] here, which is a system for offline voice control as well, with great python-based scripting (and also supports eye tracking, though I haven't used it myself). Using your computer or programming with it works like a charm, with some interesting and impressive projects based on it coming out as well, like Cursorless[1]. There's a great strangeloop talk[2] demonstrating talon and the actual state of voice coding, which is how I discovered it (hint: it's much better than you'd expect, and straightforward to learn at that). [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] Disclaimer: not affiliated, just a happy occasional user"},{"id":33544250,"author":"glenjamin","month":"2022-11","story":"Hey, GitHub – Waiting list signup","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Learning to code vim by voice to combat RSI.","quote":"There was an excellent talk at Strange Loop a few years ago by Emily Shea","text":"There was an excellent talk at Strange Loop a few years ago by Emily Shea about how she'd learnt to code vim using her voice to combat RSI. [VIDEO] The demos are in Ruby, but I could imagine that languages with strong type-aware auto-completion could be easier to do."},{"id":23506916,"author":"tbabej","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Is there any work being done in speech-to-code with deep learning?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Emily Shea's code dictation setup with a live coding demo.","quote":"I found the following talk [1] by Emily Shea on her code dictation setup (based on Talon Voice) both insightful and impressive.","text":"While (likely) not using directly deep learning, I found the following talk [1] by Emily Shea on her code dictation setup (based on Talon Voice) both insightful and impressive. EDIT: Actual demo with coding starts at 18.00: [VIDEO] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30117864,"author":"kbenson","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Why is there no high quality method for voice control of a PC?","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of a voice coding tech stack, tweaked for writing code.","quote":"you might be interested in this tech talk[1] from Emily Shea","text":"For anyone interested in this topic, you might be interested in this tech talk[1] from Emily Shea. In it she demos a tech stack similar to what's mentioned here, to fairly good effect. It does appear that required a lot of tweaking on her part and is optimized for the writing of code, and I'm not sure how well it functions for more general contexts. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28912698,"author":"58x14","month":"2021-10","story":"Spatial Keyboard Navigation","stance":"rec","why":"Intriguing presentation on voice driven development.","quote":"I think you'll find this presentation by Emily Shea on voice driven development to be intriguing.","text":"I think you'll find this presentation by Emily Shea on voice driven development to be intriguing. [VIDEO] aside, novel HIDs are IMO a huge market gap that I'd really like to invest time in if anyone wants to chat."},{"id":27215727,"author":"cube2222","month":"2021-05","story":"Squeezebox Keyboard","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by a person who switched to voice programming permanently.","quote":"Here's a nice talk on strangeloop by a person who switched to it permanently","text":"Check out voice programming with Talon[0], I've recently been playing around with it (to be less reliant on my hands), and it actually takes little work to get started and fairly productive. The state of voice programming is much better than most people would imagine. Here's a nice talk on strangeloop by a person who switched to it permanently: [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":38122596,"author":"m463","month":"2023-11","story":"I designed my own keyboard layout. Was it worth it?","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter remembers this voice coding video.","quote":"I still remember this video","text":"> Soon we will be talking to our LLM's a lot People have been saying this for decades. I think of \"dragon naturally speaking\" and similar. It still doesn't seem mainstream. (maybe it is but not visible to me?) that said, I still remember this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27537080,"author":"b2gills","month":"2021-06","story":"Try APL","stance":"reference","why":"Emily Shea's talk on voice driven development.","quote":"There are two (nearly identical) talks by Emily Shea","text":"You can if you put in the work to set it up. There are two (nearly identical) talks by Emily Shea - Perl Out Loud [LINK] - Voice Driven Development: Who needs a keyboard anyway? [VIDEO]"},{"id":26118864,"author":"lunixbochs","month":"2021-02","story":"Typing, RSI, and what I do differently","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among talks about Talon voice control.","quote":"","text":"My project, Talon, is free and cross platform. It might have some options that can help you: [LINK] Some blog posts: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] Some talks: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"}],"r":[["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["5Vt8zqhHe_c",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["s_osQvkeNRM",1],["ZQc9-seU-5k",1],["Q8oCilY4szc",1]]},"cKPlPJyQrt4":{"wy":"HN calls it one of the best technical talks and the clearest explanation of decorators, generators and context managers.","q":{"x":"It's legitimately the best technical talk I think I've seen and I can't recommend it highly enough.","i":23088282,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36793028,"author":"charlysl","month":"2023-07","story":"Advanced Python Mastery – A Course by David Beazley","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains decorators, generators, context managers and the data model","quote":"the best explanation I've seen of decorators, generators and context managers.","text":"Another good (and entertaining) resource is James Powell's talk \"So you want to be a Python expert\" [1], the best explanation I've seen of decorators, generators and context managers. Good intro to the Python data (object) model too. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24593588,"author":"ransom1538","month":"2020-09","story":"YouTube Channels to Level Up Your Programming Skills","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Absolutely amazing Python expert talk by a true pro","quote":"this python expert video was absolutely amazing done by a true pro.","text":"I don't even use python. But this python expert video was absolutely amazing done by a true pro. James Powell [VIDEO]"},{"id":23088282,"author":"mattst88","month":"2020-05","story":"Resources to Learn Python in 2020","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gives core understanding of Python's data model","quote":"It's legitimately the best technical talk I think I've seen and I can't recommend it highly enough.","text":"I work professionally in C/C++ and the instances I needed to use Python I always felt like I was missing the core understanding of the language and was writing C in a different syntax. I found James Powell's PyData 2017 talk \"So you want to be a Python expert?\" ( [VIDEO] ) to be incredible at helping me to gain that core understanding of the language. It's legitimately the best technical talk I think I've seen and I can't recommend it highly enough."},{"id":46486063,"author":"kengoa","month":"2026-01","story":"Ask HN: Reading list for being a better engineer?","stance":"rec","why":"Understanding Python's data model improves engineering","quote":"I'd recommend watching this PyData talk","text":"Understanding Python's data model helped me become a better software engineer. I'd recommend watching this PyData talk (James Powell: So you want to be a Python expert? | PyData Seattle 2017 - [VIDEO] ) and then using the official doc as a reference which is also fun to read: [LINK]"},{"id":34570666,"author":"jphoward","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Which book would you recommend for learning Python in detail?","stance":"rec","why":"Deep Python talk with live Vim coding","quote":"shows you his coding approach in real time on Vim. Plenty of them are on YouTube and are worth a watch.","text":"James Powell frequently gives very deep Python talks at conferences like Pydata, and shows you his coding approach in real time on Vim. Plenty of them are on YouTube and are worth a watch. This one [1] entitled \"So you want to be a Python expert?\" has almost half a million views! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33766379,"author":"barefeg","month":"2022-11","story":"Data-Oriented Programming in Python","stance":"rec","why":"James Powell's PyData talks on Python","quote":"I recommend any of the talks by James Powell at PyData.","text":"I recommend any of the talks by James Powell at PyData. For example this one [VIDEO] Edit: maybe this one on Numpy may be more relevant: [LINK]"}],"r":[["dDsWTyLEgbk",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["ExgxwKnH8y4",1]]},"DpXy041BIlA":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a must-watch for its deadpan, absurdly elaborate engineering and cite it for ideas like diluting strong engines with random moves.","q":{"x":"if you like this kind of thing, you'll love Elo World.","i":21987531,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30974434,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2022-04","story":"Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deadpan, absurdly elaborate weird chess algorithms.","quote":"are a must watch.","text":"Tom7 is brilliant for anyone that hasn't seen him before. His Reverse Emulation video ( [LINK] ) and Weird Chess Algorithms ( [VIDEO] ) are a must watch. His dead pan delivery coupled with the ridiculous amount of effort he puts into things that don't _really_ matter is honestly inspiring."},{"id":48197369,"author":"Rendello","month":"2026-05","story":"Regex Chess: A 2-ply minimax chess engine in 84,688 regular expressions","stance":"rec","why":"Ridiculous chess engines pitted against each other and diluted Stockfish.","quote":"Tom7's video where he made a bunch of ridiculous engines and pitted them against each other","text":"This reminded me of Tom7's video where he made a bunch of ridiculous engines and pitted them against each other (and against \"diluted\" versions of Stockfish): [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36810543,"author":"iamevn","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter lists it among favorite Tom7 videos.","quote":"my favorites are: 30 Weird Chess Algorithms: ELO World","text":"Anything that catches your eye is good but my favorites are: 30 Weird Chess Algorithms: ELO World [VIDEO] Reverse Emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS! [LINK]"},{"id":36821507,"author":"nick-of-time","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among commenter's favorite Tom7 videos.","quote":"Mine are \"Harder Drives\"[1], \"ELO World\"[2], and \"Printable x86\"[3]","text":"Mine are \"Harder Drives\"[1], \"ELO World\"[2], and \"Printable x86\"[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":21987531,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2020-01","story":"Training GPT-2 to Play Chess","stance":"rec","why":"Fun related chess-algorithm video.","quote":"if you like this kind of thing, you'll love Elo World.","text":"It's unknown what role if any BPE plays. I was surprised to discover that the final probability of a move is equal to the probability of each token from the root prompt, i.e. even though \"1.Nf3 e5\" is encoded as ['1', '.', 'N', 'f', '3', ' e', '5'] the probability of e5 seems unaffected by the fact that Nf3 is 3 tokens as opposed to one. You're right that coming up with a token mapping could help things. It's a bit tricky to do that right now. Your options for fitting a custom vocab seems to be \"use sentencepiece to fit a vocab, then modify the gpt-2 codebase to use the sentencepiece library for decoding\". I am honestly not sure if the output of sentencepiece is compatible with traditional encoders. What I mean is, it doesn't seem to generate an encoder.json + vocab.bpe file. It seemed to be some other kind of format. So I'm not sure if the tooling that has evolved around OpenAI's encoder format would be applicable there. I really don't know, though. According to this slatestarcodex comment, someone got superior results on solely algebraic notation (which looks like g1f3 instead of Nf3): [LINK] Another extension that might help is to periodically inject the full FEN board state. This was the format we were going to try next, which injects the full FEN after every move: [LINK] I'm so happy to get to work with GPT-2 1.5B. It's been a lot of fun to train. By the way, if you like this kind of thing, you'll love Elo World. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21921396,"author":"rictic","month":"2019-12","story":"Immortal Game","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing video on unusual chess engine strategies.","quote":"you'd probably enjoy watching tom7's video on unusual chess engine strategies","text":"Only somewhat related, but if you found these ideas amusing, you'd probably enjoy watching tom7's video on unusual chess engine strategies: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20572531,"author":"symmetricsaurus","month":"2019-07","story":"Survival in chessland [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Tom7's chess video; his other videos are worth watching.","quote":"If you haven’t seen them his other videos are well worth watching as well.","text":"Tom7 made a video also: [VIDEO] If you haven’t seen them his other videos are well worth watching as well."},{"id":20573375,"author":"onnodigcomplex","month":"2019-07","story":"Kriegspiel","stance":"rec","why":"Tom7 predicts hidden opponent pieces with a neural net, then uses Stockfish.","quote":"Great video anyway.","text":"Suckerpinch (Tom) recently had a video[1] about predicting the position of the oponents pieces from knowning just white. I don't think he's aware of this. Great video anyway. He trained an NN to predict the pieces and then used Stockfish on the resulting position. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46595789,"author":"Uehreka","month":"2026-01","story":"The chess bot on Delta Air Lines will destroy you (2024) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Dilutes strong engines with random moves to rate weak bots.","quote":"In tom7’s Elo World, he does this (“dilutes” strong Chess AIs with a certain percentage of random moves)","text":"> I figured it's how they were able to weaken a chess engine back in the day; can't adjust the overall strength, so add random blunders. In tom7’s Elo World, he does this (“dilutes” strong Chess AIs with a certain percentage of random moves) to smooth the gradient since otherwise it would be impossible to evaluate his terrible chess bots against something like Stockfish since they’d just lose every time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40638949,"author":"jerf","month":"2024-06","story":"The rarest move in chess [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Charts where chess pieces start, end up, and their survival rates.","quote":"Tom7's Elo World chess video has where pieces start and end up, and their survival rates, as a chart","text":"\"I was initially hoping to get some insight on e.g. which pairs of squares had the fewest moves for a given piece etc.\" This may not be quite what you were asking for, but it's close, and has the advantage that I can link it right now. Tom7's Elo World chess video has where pieces start and end up, and their survival rates, as a chart: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31013346,"author":"mixmix","month":"2022-04","story":"Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Contains a playful misleading optics explanation around 10:20.","quote":"check out his \"30 Weird Chess Algorithms","text":"Tom's really mastered his skill of casual misleading, he adds these \"wait, what?\" moments to every video. A more notorious example will be his explanation of how optical reflection works, check out his \"30 Weird Chess Algorithms\" ( [VIDEO] ) around 10:20. Edit: spelling."},{"id":20576729,"author":"rrauenza","month":"2019-07","story":"Kriegspiel","stance":"reference","why":"Tournament of weird chess algorithms from SIGBOVIK papers.","quote":"An intricate and lengthy account of several different computer chess topics from my SIGBOVIK 2019 papers.","text":"I had just looked up this wiki article a couple days ago based on this video: [VIDEO] , \"30 Weird Chess Algorithms: Elo World\" An intricate and lengthy account of several different computer chess topics from my SIGBOVIK 2019 papers. We conduct a tournament of fools with a pile of different weird chess algorithms, ostensibly to quantify how well my other weird program to play color- and piece-blind chess performs. On the way we \"learn\" about mirrors, arithmetic encoding, perversions of game tree search, spicy oils, and hats. Is this also why it showed up just now?"},{"id":48759943,"author":"fuglede_","month":"2026-07","story":"Kimi K2.7 Code is generally available in GitHub Copilot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"In fact, you don't even need an LLM tournament when you can have tom7's Elo World tournament: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ar9WRwCiSr0",2],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["tD5NrevFtbU",1],["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["IroPQ150F6c",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["VVUeqxXwCA0",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["KSWqx8goqSY",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1]]},"niKblgZupOc":{"wy":"HN recommends it for showing how old developers exploited the smeary nature of composite signals and what CGA really looked like on period hardware.","q":{"x":"The 8-Bit guy did a great piece on CGA that goes into detail about how old developers abused the fuzzy smeary nature of composite signals","i":22670372,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":13936483,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-03","story":"Secret colours of the Commodore 64","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows artifacting that produces extra colours","quote":"the 8-bit guy does a GREAT video on this","text":"If you want to see how this type of colour magic works in video form, the 8-bit guy does a GREAT video on this: [VIDEO] He shows all the interesting artifacting that makes these extra colours possible."},{"id":29316340,"author":"nzach","month":"2021-11","story":"Trackers: The Sound of 16-Bit [video]","stance":"rec","why":"CGA video among recommended retro computing content","quote":"I'm leaving some random links to great channels that produce this kind of content","text":"Your comment got me thinking: \"Wouldn't be nice if existed an 'awesome list' for this kind of content?\" After a brief google search the best I could find is this [0] and it seems to be abandoned. Does anyone know a better source for this type of content? I'm leaving some random links to great channels that produce this kind of content: - CGA Graphics - Not as bad as you thought! [1] - DVD-RAM: The Disc that Behaved like a Flash Drive [2] - The Story of Super Mario Bros. 3 | Gaming Historian [3] [0] - [LINK] [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK]"},{"id":26495977,"author":"laumars","month":"2021-03","story":"Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade EGA/VGA Comparison","stance":"rec","why":"What CGA really looked like on period hardware","quote":"The following YouTube video does a good job describing what CGA was really like back in the day","text":"Bare in mind the displays of the time would have blended the pixels. Likewise those who complain that CGA is “only 4 awful colours” have clearly never used a proper CGA system the way it was originally intended. The following YouTube video does a good job describing what CGA was really like back in the day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22670372,"author":"jandrese","month":"2020-03","story":"Why do color TVs make black and white snow?","stance":"rec","why":"How developers exploited composite signals for better graphics","quote":"The 8-Bit guy did a great piece on CGA that goes into detail about how old developers abused the fuzzy smeary nature of composite signals","text":"The 8-Bit guy did a great piece on CGA that goes into detail about how old developers abused the fuzzy smeary nature of composite signals to output somewhat decent graphics. The tricks however didn't work when people upgraded to proper monitors and as a result a lot of CGA games look much worse today than they did on the intended equipment of the day. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21969731,"author":"gecko","month":"2020-01","story":"The Polygons of Another World: IBM PC","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of what CGA could do on composite","quote":"has a great overview of what was possible","text":"CGA colors were designed for display on composite; the issue is most people were using digital cables, which had the garish colors. [VIDEO] has a great overview of what was possible."},{"id":12509869,"author":"qwertyuiop924","month":"2016-09","story":"A Pixel Artist Renounces Pixel Art (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains CGA composite color artifacting","quote":"this video has some more information on the phenomenon","text":"That's a thing more relevant on PC: this video has some more information on the phenomenon (I think) you're talking about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41137206,"author":"lomase","month":"2024-08","story":"The effect of CRTs on pixel art","stance":"reference","why":"CGA was not meant to look as seen on RGB monitors","quote":"Developers did definitly took advantages of everything they could to show more colors on the screen","text":"Did you ever played a ms-dos game in CGA mode? It was not suposed to look like that! [VIDEO] Developers did definitly took advantages of everything they could to show more colors on the screen, remember all those demos?"},{"id":41137724,"author":"actionfromafar","month":"2024-08","story":"The effect of CRTs on pixel art","stance":"reference","why":"PC games made for composite video","quote":"Even PCs had games for composite video","text":"PC monitors with RGB were a different ballgame, at least at that low resolution there was no distortion or bluriness as you say. Even PCs had games for composite video: [VIDEO] TVs though, especially on RF, were pretty darn blurry."},{"id":38064208,"author":"johannes1234321","month":"2023-10","story":"The IBM 5153's True CGA Palette and Color Output","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates composite versus RGBI difference","quote":"This video demonstrates the difference","text":"One thing about CGA is that it was meant to be output via composite video instead of VGA-style RGBI. This video demonstrates the difference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36184872,"author":"Ekaros","month":"2023-06","story":"A new line drawing method for the cycle savvy","stance":"reference","why":"Composite tricks yielding extra colours","quote":"You can make funny tricks when passing image as composite.","text":"[VIDEO] You can make funny tricks when passing image as composite. Where with those you can produce other colours. And state of computers were quite limited and primitive then. On other hand for business use, those are still acceptable, everything was text anyway."}],"r":[["Tv6aJRGpz_A",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["ev3vENli7wQ",1],["rIAXIubSTkc",1],["MxT6IwUtLSU",1],["l70iRcSxqzo",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1]]},"IAF8DjrQSSk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an enthusiastic demo of spreadsheet-like reactive notebooks, which commenters think new notebook projects should copy.","q":{"x":"I think every new notebook project should take ideas from Julia's Pluto","i":25565730,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":29694825,"author":"wodenokoto","month":"2021-12","story":"Bad Data and Data Engineering: Dissecting Google Play Music Takeout Data","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Pluto's spreadsheet-like reactive notebook model.","quote":"the JuliacCn presentation on these is absolutely wonderful","text":"I think notebooks are great for building a presentation / reports of a data exploration, but I agree that they shouldn't represent programs. Rstudio won't export an R-markdown notebook to PDF without rerunning all cells sequentially. It's great for keeping the code \"in check\", but for things that takes hours to run, it can be quite annoying. I do think most of the problems with notebooks go away if you somehow force sequential running before presenting it to other people. Another really cool take on notebooks are Julia's Pluto Notebooks. Like a spreadsheet, there is no sequence that cells are run in. Everything is updated simultaneously. It's kinda hard to explain, but the JuliacCn presentation on these is absolutely wonderful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25565730,"author":"mathnmusic","month":"2020-12","story":"Niex: Jupyter Notebooks but Using Elixir","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presents Pluto ideas that new notebook projects should adopt.","quote":"I think every new notebook project should take ideas from Julia's Pluto","text":"I think every new notebook project should take ideas from Julia's Pluto: [VIDEO] Working directly with the source files is just one of the niceties it has."},{"id":24993126,"author":"snicker7","month":"2020-11","story":"An Introduction to Pluto","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful JuliaCon talk presenting Pluto.","quote":"I would like to draw attention to the wonderful JuliaCon talk presenting Pluto","text":"It is linked in the article, but I would like to draw attention to the wonderful JuliaCon talk presenting Pluto: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24133760,"author":"pmdulaney","month":"2020-08","story":"JuliaCon 2020 in Retrospective","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduces Pluto, a reactive notebook that updates like a spreadsheet.","quote":"Watch the following video made by Pluto's creators","text":"The game changer for ordinary technical programming folks from JuliaCon 2020 is the introduction of a new \"reactive\" notebook, Pluto.jl. \"Reactive\" means that -- unlike Jupyter -- it automatically updates the whole notebook, like a spreadsheet, when you change a value in any cell. I am a Julia and Jupyter newbie, but I picked up on it easily. Watch the following video made by Pluto's creators: [VIDEO] Go here for Pluto installation instructions: [LINK] One thing that tripped me up, which is a generic Julia REPL issue: You enter the Julia package manager with \"]\" on the Julia> command line -- but how do you exit the package manager? Answer: Either Backspace or Ctrl-C."},{"id":25225194,"author":"socialdemocrat","month":"2020-11","story":"Ditch Excel and Use Julia Data Frames","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates automatic dependencies and sliders updating plots in real time.","quote":"Demonstration of Pluto.jl here","text":"The Pluto notebook for Julia does exactly the same. It creates dependencies between code cells and data automatically. If you change any data or code, that code ripples through through the rest of the notebook. So e.g. you could bind a slider to a variable and if that variable is used in a plot, dragging that slider will modify the plot in realtime. Demonstration of Pluto.jl here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25230486,"author":"socialdemocrat","month":"2020-11","story":"Ditch Excel and Use Julia Data Frames","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Pluto's changes rippling through calculations and plots.","quote":"The closest may be Pluto.jl","text":"The closest may be Pluto.jl [VIDEO] In Pluto plots, calculations get bound to data and when you change that data the effects immediately ripple through. Personally if I work with table data in Julia, I will use it with something like Table Tool [LINK] It lets you view and edit a CSV file. Saving and loading CSV files from Julia is very quick anyway. You can use a couple of key strokes to bring back a previous command. You also got some newer ones such as Tad: [LINK]"},{"id":24304085,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2020-08","story":"Pluto.jl – a reactive, lightweight, simple notebook","stance":"rec","why":"Playful, enthusiastic presentation of Pluto.","quote":"the playful enthusiasm in the presentation video linked in the description just makes me smile","text":"Really happy to see that the good ideas from Observable notebooks are being copied elsewhere. I don't use Julia myself at the moment (although I think it's a beautiful language), but I know some people who will be very happy with this! Also, the playful enthusiasm in the presentation video linked in the description just makes me smile: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24307902,"author":"ddragon","month":"2020-08","story":"Pluto.jl – a reactive, lightweight, simple notebook","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Pluto builds a dependency graph between cells.","quote":"It's mentioned in the video [1], it does static analysis of the code to create a graph of dependencies","text":"It's mentioned in the video [1], it does static analysis of the code to create a graph of dependencies (for example which cell uses a variable defined by another cell), so when you update any cell it will find what cells are affected by the change (the downstream nodes on a directed acyclic graph) and only evals the code on them (instead of running everything). Julia is particularly good for those kind of code analysis since it's a very Lispy language. It also does a trick of creating new modules to manipulate scope to make deleted variables/import/cells invisible (and therefore free to be garbage collected). [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["uxC1fPE1QEE",1],["T647CGsuOVU",1],["PZbqAMEwtOE",1]]},"YX40hbAHx3s":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as the best short, funny introduction to P vs. NP and complexity classes for people with no background.","q":{"x":"This 10 minute video is a great introduction to the P = NP problem and computational complexity theory if you have no context.","i":12895743,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":35909234,"author":"sgrove","month":"2023-05","story":"Problems harder than NP-Complete","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of complexity classes and relation to reality","quote":"My absolute favorite overview of this","text":"My absolute favorite overview of this - the video goes over both the explanation of the spaces, but also how it relates to a sort of understanding of reality itself! [VIDEO]"},{"id":16024348,"author":"sgrove","month":"2017-12","story":"Classic Nintendo Games Are NP-Hard (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Revisited video explaining P vs NP","quote":"There's a fantastic video I refer to every now and then","text":"There's a fantastic video I refer to every now and then if I'm a bit unsure about it, definitely recommend it - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14499814,"author":"jlg23","month":"2017-06","story":"The asymmetry in storytelling between math and the other sciences","stance":"rec","why":"Funny short P vs NP overview and complexity zoo glance","quote":"very funny and also provides a cursory glance at what lies beyond P vs NP","text":"> I would guess the P!=NP proof would be an example. Unfortunately, it doesn't have any easy description that the average person would understand. \"Hard. Really hard. Don't be ridiculous.\". Now backtrack 2 small examples on paper. Oh, look, the number of steps here grow, but these grow even faster! Especially mathematics when explained to a layperson so much depends on the language used. If one manages to keep the rest of the complexity zoo[1] out of the explanation and invests time into very basic examples, a lot things can be explained that otherwise the average person would not understand. [1] [LINK] EDIT: Looking for a video I stumbled upon last week that had a very nice, short explanation of P vs NP (which I did not find again), I found this one: Not lay-person simple, but very funny and also provides a cursory glance at what lies beyond P vs NP, i.e. the complexity zoo: [VIDEO] (10 min)"},{"id":13320262,"author":"d23","month":"2017-01","story":"P vs. NP","stance":"rec","why":"Understandable P vs NP explanation without background","quote":"Very understandable even without any background on the topic","text":"A guy at a company I used to work for made a great video on this. Very understandable even without any background on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12895743,"author":"austinl","month":"2016-11","story":"The P=?NP Poll (2002) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible 10 minute introduction to P vs NP","quote":"This 10 minute video is a great introduction to the P = NP problem and computational complexity theory if you have no context.","text":"This 10 minute video is a great introduction to the P = NP problem and computational complexity theory if you have no context. I've shown this to plenty of friends (including those outside of computer science). Essentially, \"does being able to quickly recognize correct answers mean there's also a quick way to find them?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":48776792,"author":"PandaRider","month":"2026-07","story":"Markets are competitive if and only if P = NP","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as explanation of P and NP","quote":"This video explains in detail","text":"I have taken algorithms courses. This video explains in detail: [VIDEO] In short, P means Polynomial time (i.e. markets can solve computation problems efficiently) and NP means Non-Deterministic Polynomial time (i.e. markets can verify solutions of computation problems efficiently but solutions are found by luck). If P != NP, it means luck CANNOT be engineered and markets are competitive."},{"id":19599003,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-04","story":"Do you believe in P = NP? – the 3. PollPaper [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as additional related item","quote":"","text":"I took a class from Gasarch in the 80's at UMD. He's a freaky smart guy! I swear to God (and have said before [1]): He wore a t-shirt that said: \"P=NP? I don't know, and I don't care!\" How times have changed! [1] HN discussion: [LINK] of Scott Aaronson's article \"My 116-page survey article on P vs. NP: better late than never\": [LINK] Also: P vs. NP and the Computational Complexity Zoo [VIDEO]"},{"id":10782955,"author":"ColinWright","month":"2015-12","story":"Integer Factoring via Graph Three Colouring","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote citation for NP-completeness","quote":"","text":"Because Graph Three Colouring[0][1] is NP-Complete[2][3] we can use it to solve any NP problem. Integer factoring is an NP problem[4] so we should be able to factor integers by using Graph Three Colouring. This post shows one way of doing that, and explains the techniques used to show directly that Graph Three Colouring is NP-Complete. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["kKySEUrP7LA",1],["NWSZ4c9yqW8",1],["7lm3K8zVOdY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["skos4B5x7qE",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1],["VeAdryYZ7ak",1]]},"oFeoS41xe7w":{"wy":"Recommended for Baldwin's riveting delivery and as an example of serious debate between opposing worldviews.","q":{"x":"I was watching James Baldwin's speech at Cambridge University the other day and was riveted the entire time","i":13798546,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":41179487,"author":"padolsey","month":"2024-08","story":"The Private Life: On James Baldwin","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Baldwin's articulate blend of cerebral and emotive speaking","quote":"He is articulate in a way that seems to blend the cerebral with the emotive.","text":"I know only pieces of his life, but whenever a video or audio clip of Baldwin pops up, I listen voraciously. He is articulate in a way that seems to blend the cerebral with the emotive. He seems to summon forth both Maya Angelou and Christopher Hitchens; The level of articulateness is so rare. I wish I had him on tap as a podcast, ... endlessly ruminating. If you want a slice.. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13798546,"author":"benbreen","month":"2017-03","story":"My adventures in digital history","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Baldwin is a natural public speaker, riveting throughout","quote":"I was watching James Baldwin's speech at Cambridge University the other day and was riveted the entire time","text":"Thanks for posting this. I started teaching my first history lecture course this January and very quickly came up against what the author mentions - not only students but also teachers have a lot of trouble sustaining attention for more than 30-40 minutes of straight-ahead lecturing. It's a medium that I do think works well if the person doing it is a natural public speaker (I was watching James Baldwin's speech at Cambridge University the other day and was riveted the entire time [1]) but unfortunately there's virtually no training in public speaking provided by a typical PhD. If anything PhDs are rewarded for learning how to speak in a way that doesn't work at all in a classroom - hyper-attuned to nuance but allergic to making the kind of memorable declarative statements that students seem to want for their notes. I noticed from grading midterms that my students fixated on phrases like \"the Catholic Church is the ghost of the Roman Empire\" or \"Cogito ergo sum\" but had trouble with things we spent a lot more time on, like the difference between natural philosophy and science. I haven't taught a digital history-focused class yet but have been experimenting with interspersing my lectures with class periods where the students research an historical topic in groups and then present their findings to the class. One unexpected benefit I've noticed from doing this is that the act of physically getting up and moving around the classroom, then standing up to present seems to do wonde"},{"id":25339119,"author":"js2","month":"2020-12","story":"What the Church Meant for James Baldwin","stance":"rec","why":"Baldwin debating Buckley","quote":"Also watch Baldwin debate Buckley","text":"Also watch Baldwin debate Buckley: [VIDEO] (And as an aside, if you haven't had your fill of Buckley after that, watch Best of Enemies: Buckley vs. Vidal ) I recently came across an opinion piece Baldwin wrote for the NYT in 1967. I won't try to summarize it because it's short enough and I can't do it justice. [LINK]"},{"id":23312089,"author":"ceilingcorner","month":"2020-05","story":"The Ghetto and the Mansion","stance":"rec","why":"Calm rational discussion between opposing worldviews","quote":"it's interesting to watch old episodes of Firing Line on YouTube.","text":"This is a pretty weak article with a smarmy attitude and mostly just a hit piece on Buckley (who I certainly have no love for) and a lionization of Baldwin. It's also filled with quite a few misleading, if not actually false, statements. See the paragraph mentioning Malcom X for an example. Nonetheless, it's interesting to watch old episodes of Firing Line on YouTube. Buckley doesn't generally come across as the smarter person, but the guests themselves are usually worth watching. The show is a calm, rational discussion between people with diametrically opposed worldviews. This seems almost unthinkable in the shouting match, tribalistic media we have today. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11641868,"author":"rtpg","month":"2016-05","story":"Why James Baldwin Still Matters","stance":"rec","why":"Baldwin and Buckley debate","quote":"There's a really interesting debate between Baldwin and William Buckley up on youtube.","text":"There's a really interesting debate between Baldwin and William Buckley up on youtube. Riviting stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12972320,"author":"tptacek","month":"2016-11","story":"A Black History Museum","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Because there is in fact no such thing as a \"white person\". Believe it or not, there is a coherent shared cultural identity of American black people, most of whom are descended from slaves brought here from Africa who had their original cultures stripped from them, and who belonged to a community segregated against legally into the 1970s. That those people came from several different countries on the continent of Africa is of little meaning, because their culture was deliberately cut off from its roots, and was built largely anew in the United States. Your inclination is to assume that if \"black culture\" is a thing, then \"white culture\" must also be a thing. No, that is not the case. What there is in addition to \"black culture\" is: * Irish-American culture, which has a museum, parades, and a holiday, * Italian-American culture, which has a museum, parades, and a holiday, * German-American culture, which has a museum and parades, * Polish-American culture, which has a museum, parades, and a holiday I can go on. The notion that there is some notional \"white culture\" is artificial, a lie begun in earnest in the 20th century. No, really. Before \"white culture\", the Anglo- and Germanic-American communities in the US targeted Italians, the Irish, and the Polish (as well as Catholics of all nationalities). When the writing appeared on the wall for end of Jim Crow, \"white culture\" emerged as a solidarity movement among those people prejudiced against blacks and black culture. This is"}],"r":[["N1pIYI5JQLE",1],["v1WiCGq-PcY",1],["iYJKd0rkKss",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["JrMiSQAGOS4",1]]},"hER0Qp6QJNU":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for its take on managing young employees and on how technology and approval-seeking affect behavior.","q":{"x":"Simon Sinek explains it perfectly","i":14716693,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14716693,"author":"Swizec","month":"2017-07","story":"Why I deleted my Facebook account","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sinek on tech and approval-seeking","quote":"Simon Sinek explains it perfectly","text":"I too feel exactly this way with almost everything. Facebook, Twitter, Slack, Email, Messages ... it's kind of tough to escape. Impossible even. And here's the worst part: We grew up with this shit. My generation was one of the first (1987) to have their formative social years immersed in this shit. That means we'll never escape. Ever. We're literally wired to seek approval of strangers on the internet instead of the people around us. It feels more ... correct? Real? True? That red circle with a number literally gives more enjoyment than a close friend saying \"Heh that's cool\". The worst part is that there's likely no cure. Rehab, maybe. But rehab for this shit doesn't exist yet. Plus try explaining to your boss that you're not reading email and slack and you're never coming back because you're in notification rehab. Simon Sinek explains it perfectly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18509935,"author":"richrichardsson","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: Why not more hiring of junior devs, then on-the-job-training?","stance":"rec","why":"Pertinent point around the 8m30 mark","quote":"worth to watch before that to get where his point is coming from","text":"> There is only so much impact I can make in this position I'm reminded of this talk : [VIDEO] around the 8m30 mark is the pertinent point, but worth to watch before that to get where his point is coming from."},{"id":17826148,"author":"vesak","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Codebase at my work is a complete mess, what should I do?","stance":"rec","why":"May resonate with workplace frustration","quote":"This Simon Sinek interview about millennials in the workplace might resonate with your situation","text":">Should I just go and basically say that this person did a bad job? You wouldn't be correct in saying that. That person delivered a project that works. In the beginning of a project, that's all that matters. >I'm so frustrated and depressed by it. Should you feel like that? It sounds like you're working to make things better. This Simon Sinek interview about millennials in the workplace might resonate with your situation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15665119,"author":"audiolion","month":"2017-11","story":"Sean Parker unloads on Facebook “exploiting” human psychology","stance":"rec","why":"How dopamine hits affect millennials","quote":"Hereis a great talk by Simon Sinek on how these dopamine hits and how technology are affecting the millennial generation","text":"Hereis a great talk by Simon Sinek on how these dopamine hits and how technology are affecting the millennial generation [VIDEO]"},{"id":13419960,"author":"cj","month":"2017-01","story":"How much does employee turnover really cost?","stance":"rec","why":"Sinek on managing young talent","quote":"this video by Simon Sinek on \"Millennials in the Workplace\" can be enlightening","text":"> \"Instead, focus on growth, impact, and care\" Since a lot of tech companies employ young talent (relative to other industries), this video by Simon Sinek on \"Millennials in the Workplace\" can be enlightening: [VIDEO] Forewarning, he goes off the deep end on a few points, but IMO the general ideas are valid."},{"id":13442189,"author":"rebootthesystem","month":"2017-01","story":"How Do You Measure Leadership?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here are a couple of videos that discuss the general topic","text":"Well, there are others factors at play today. Here are a couple of videos that discuss the general topic: [VIDEO] [LINK] I have seen and continue to see some of the behaviors described in these two videos and it is deeply disturbing. Attempting to lead people with deep social challenges is an exercise in frustration and futility. Leadership, in this context, is a very different thing than in what I'll call more traditional settings. It almost has to be reduced to appeasement and coddling. Latte's and ice cream. We have a generation of adults who behave as petulant children half their age did in prior generations. Except they are in a 25 year old body. Some of these 25 year olds today would be slapped out of the building by 25 year olds a generation or two ago. They are weak, oversensitive, self-serving, entitled, delicate and disconnected from reality. This is how you end-up with some of the crazy stuff coming out of outfits like Facebook and Google. They are completely devoid of real world social and business skills yet interact and affect the personal and business lives of millions. One example that comes to mind are account suspensions and cancellations without even a shadow of customer care or service offered. If you can't swipe or click a problem away the option to actually engage with a real human being and exercise the ability to resolve problems simply isn't there. How do you lead these people? Well, first they have to grow up. I suspect that will happen once they get"}],"r":[["bwDrHqNZ9lo",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1]]},"e3msMuwqp-o":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as the capstone of Hamming's course and as career advice about working on important problems.","q":{"x":"It's the last lecture in a wonderful 31-lecture course","i":27615496,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27615496,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2021-06","story":"Things 10x People Do","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hamming asks if you work on the most important problems.","quote":"It's the last lecture in a wonderful 31-lecture course","text":"This reads a lot like Hamming's talk You and Your Research applied to programming. It's the last lecture in a wonderful 31-lecture course, about maths, computers, physics, technology, illustrated through Hamming's life. He'd spend his lunch hours chatting with people at Bell Labs, asking them if they were working on the most important problems in their field, and if not, why not?! [VIDEO]"},{"id":25467057,"author":"teddyh","month":"2020-12","story":"John Carmack: Career Advice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hamming on careers and doing research that matters.","quote":"I prefer Richard Hamming’s series","text":"I prefer Richard Hamming’s series: [VIDEO] “ I have given a talk with this title many times, and it turns out from discussions after the talk I could have just as well have called it \"You and Your Engineering Career,\" or even \"You and Your Career.\" But I left the word \"Research\" in the title because that is what I have most studied. ”"},{"id":39058099,"author":"Tangurena2","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: Good books on philosophy of engineering?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture version of Hamming's essay on always learning.","quote":"lecture version","text":"It is a great book, and it includes an amazing essay by him titled You and Your Research [0][1] - which explains why it is important to \"always be learning\". 0 - [LINK] 1 - [VIDEO] (lecture version)"},{"id":32287818,"author":"teddyh","month":"2022-07","story":"Allocating resources where they solve the most important problems","stance":"rec","why":"Hamming's question about working on important things.","quote":"If what you are doing is not important, and if you don't think it is going to lead to something important, why are you working on it?","text":"“If what you are doing is not important, and if you don't think it is going to lead to something important, why are you working on it?” — Richard Hamming [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1]]},"3GVO-MfIl1Q":{"wy":"HN people share it as an inspiring talk linking skateboarding creativity to hacking and open-source knowledge sharing.","q":{"x":"talks a bit about the relationship between hacking and skateboarding in his TED talk","i":22893209,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":16839599,"author":"iam_takada","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on innovating in your field.","quote":"His talk really inspired me follow my creativity.","text":"Talk on innovating in your field by Rodney Mullens: [VIDEO] Grew up playing Tony Hawk. His talk really inspired me follow my creativity."},{"id":27707133,"author":"psychomugs","month":"2021-07","story":"The YouTube revolution in knowledge transfer (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Mullen on epistemology in skateboarding and parallels with open source.","quote":"has a great TED Talk on epistemology in skateboarding","text":"This has been extremely pronounced with skateboarding. Technical tricks that used to take years to land are now being done effortlessly by kids filming throwaway clips shot vertically on their phones. Rodney Mullen, the godfather of basically every trick that involves jumping up and flipping the board and landing on it, has a great TED Talk on epistemology in skateboarding [1]. The biggest block is often just to see proof that something can be done. He also draws apt comparisons with the open source community. Interestingly, he apparently invented a lot of his tricks in isolation on a farm; some genius thrives in the woodshed. Thinking in McLuhanisms, these new mediums have undoubtedly extended epistemology in very revolutionary ways, but it's definitely amputated them as well. The dime-a-dozen landscape and portrait photos that are nigh indistinguishable, the wild west of the content treadmill economy, and the anxiety of influencers [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24027981,"author":"lloeki","month":"2020-08","story":"Skate Warrior 1992, 1999, 2020","stance":"rec","why":"Rodney Mullen's TED talk drawing parallels between skaters and hackers.","quote":"Godfather of skateboarding","text":"Cue Rodney Mullen’s TED talk: “Pop a Ollie and Innovate!”. If anyone’s in a position to draw such a comparison between skaters and hackers, it’s the Godfather of skateboarding. Also, “This is Eric Koston”, the comparison is indirect, but the section around “we’re trespassers” resonates deeply. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":22893209,"author":"ridv","month":"2020-04","story":"For Donald Knuth, good coding is synonymous with beautiful expression","stance":"rec","why":"Mullen on the relationship between hacking and skateboarding.","quote":"talks a bit about the relationship between hacking and skateboarding in his TED talk","text":"Really awesome seeing someone talk about skateboarding here on HN as it relates to the subjects covered by Knuth in this article. I grew up skateboarding and I credit the years those years with a huge amount of learning I didn't even know I was doing. To expand upon what you said: it's amazing when you take that trick, a kickflip for example, and you're so confident in your consistency, that you apply it to something new. Kickflip to rock to fakie, kickflip down a set of stairs, kickflip to 50-50, and so on. The consistency you gain from that trick opens up the door to a whole new world of combinations. There's so much about skateboarding, the culture (or counter culture in some cases) around it that translate so well to programming/hacking (and life in general). The trials of learning something new. The acceptance and overcoming of failure. The respect you shown and earned when you're at the park and you or someone else puts down a sick trick. One of my favorite creative minds in skateboarding, Rodney Mullen, talks a bit about the relationship between hacking and skateboarding in his TED talk. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27266267,"author":"lloeki","month":"2021-05","story":"Tony Hawk on his skateboarding legacy","stance":"reference","why":"Mullen explains his fundamental approach to skateboarding.","quote":"See e.g how Mullen explains his fundamental approach to skateboarding","text":"Hmm, not too sure about that comparison, Tony Hawk is really good, but to me a Carmack of skateboarding would be some one more like Rodney Mullen or Daewon Song. See e.g how Mullen explains his fundamental approach to skateboarding in \"Pop a ollie and innovate\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":16101757,"author":"lloeki","month":"2018-01","story":"Snow shovelling can kill","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm on to 37 and started skateboarding 10 months ago. Took back my way to block climbing which I didn't practice for 15 years. Also started calisthenics. This is after a sizable weight gain and huge drop in fitness down to rock bottom. I was quite athletic when I was 18-22, but stopped doing sports with any regularity around 23-25 and gradually degraded physically. Some day around 32 I woke up and felt so bad in my own body I decided to put an end to this over the next 7-10 years (since it was the time I took to dig myself into that hole I figured out it would take about the same time to reliably dig myself out of it), changed my diet (this is key!), and started running, which I hate, but basically the only thing I could manage to do. Went like 500m at a ridiculously slow pace (something like 10min/km) and started puking right on the sidewalk . This is when I really realized Things Were Definitively Not OK, and that all the parties and fun and eating and drinking we're not worth that . End result seven years later, lost the 20kg I had in excess, ran 18km in 1h30, stopped running regularly but (now that I'm more solid) swapped for sports I enjoy much more, and I feel so good it's like I could tear walls apart with my bare hands! In many ways I'm in as good or better a shape than I ever was even in my 20s. Random sample of newly met strangers give me between 8-15 years less than my age, and \"less than 30, for sure\". It's never too late. Pace yourself, one step at a time. Set re"}],"r":[["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["ZfuierUvx1A",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["msN87y-iEx0",1],["DJFKl_5JTnA",1]]},"62tIvfP9A2w":{"wy":"Recommended as a well-produced, accessible explanation of Giant Steps and the harmonic ideas behind Coltrane's work.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this Vox explainer on John Coltrane's","i":34286183,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34286183,"author":"peteforde","month":"2023-01","story":"Jazz Is Freedom","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the math behind Coltrane's Giant Steps","quote":"I highly recommend this Vox explainer on John Coltrane's","text":"I highly recommend this Vox explainer on John Coltrane's \"Giant Steps\", for anyone interested in learning about the math behind one of Jazz' greatest innovations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45115903,"author":"ghostpepper","month":"2025-09","story":"John Coltrane's Tone Circle","stance":"rec","why":"Condensed, accessible version of the tone circle ideas","quote":"this short YouTube video touches on some of the same ideas in a much more condensed and accessible version","text":"If you don’t have the time (or the musical background) for the full article, this short YouTube video touches on some of the same ideas in a much more condensed and accessible version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45119334,"author":"zzzeek","month":"2025-09","story":"John Coltrane's Tone Circle","stance":"rec","why":"Well produced video with sound on the subject","quote":"I found a well produced video by Vox on the subject","text":"Nice site but we're at the point in \"Show an In Depth Thing on Hacker News\" where circles like these need to be presented with a live GUI that includes sound and colors as you spin the circles around to play different chords. towards my craving for instant gratification with sound and lights I found a well produced video by Vox on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39603107,"author":"elbelcho","month":"2024-03","story":"Miles Davis and the recording of Kind of Blue","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible explanation of Giant Steps","quote":"watch this explainer on Coltrane's Giant Steps.","text":"If you're looking for a comprehensible to mortals explanation of the genius that can be found in Jazz, watch this explainer on Coltrane's Giant Steps. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25652591,"author":"davidwf","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What YouTube channels do you subscribe to?","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a personal list of channels","quote":"","text":"I keep a Google Document of these to share just in case people ask about my YouTube interests. I Actually Watched All of Them I have actually watched ALL of the videos on these channels and I don’t even feel bad about it :-) I actually like new videos from some of the ones below (like NativLang, for example) but they maybe don’t hold up in the early videos. - Space Time: PBS Physics, sooo good -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Lindsay Ellis: Really, really intelligent video essays about film and culture -- [LINK] -- Great First Video: [LINK] - Periodic Videos: Chemistry, including a video for every element -- [LINK] -- Great first video (obviously): [LINK] Other Math and Science - 3Blue1Brown: Amazing math visualizations -- [LINK] -- Great first video, how e^pi*i works: [LINK] - NumberPhile: Fun math videos -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Eons: Biology! -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Infinite Series: Math math math, really really hard math -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - SciShow: Science! More poppy but fun -- [LINK] -- Silly first show: [LINK] -- Actual physics: [LINK] - VSauce: Science-y? -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Practical Engineering: -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] Other Humanities - Alliterative: Connections via language -- [LINK] -- Great first video about gin and gimlets: [LINK] - Bon Appetit: Such fun food stuff! -- Gourmet Makes: [LINK] -- It’s Alive: [LINK] - Masaman: Genetics and human migration (and"}],"r":[["QWveXdj6oZU",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["K8gV05nS7mc",1],["v0YEaeIClKY",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1],["cBDJDZAo41c",1],["BLGFriOKz6U",1],["EUrOxh_0leE",1]]},"1Y1ya-yF35g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a funny but sobering overview of nuclear weapons safety failures.","q":{"x":"John Olivers episode on it is also amazing.","i":20304497,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":47729381,"author":"Jamesbeam","month":"2026-04","story":"Great at gaming? US air traffic control wants you to apply","stance":"rec","why":"Report on poor state of nuclear security.","quote":"Reminds me a lot of his report on nuclear security.","text":"Reminds me a lot of his report on nuclear security. [VIDEO] A lot of times as a citizen I think you feel that something is \"off\" with different Government jobs but can’t put what exactly. And then you watch one of those reports and be like \"holy duck, how can it be this bad and what are they doing to my people and with my taxes?\" Different country, but a lot of times when dealing with the government I think why are the people working there always grumpy, and then one gave me the \"tour\" of what they have to deal with that is hidden from the public eye. Working toilets? Nah, they had to go outside around the building into Porta Potty’s. He showed me like fifty places in the building with mold. Not the fun white one you get on cheese. I am talking about black fungus out of stranger things eating half the wall. Some offices had signs saying working in a different office today with the date printed to 1998. Inside water was dripping from the ceiling. He is like that’s why we are grumpy. Ever since I bring a piece of cake and some hot coffee when I have to deal with government employees and thank them for their service. They are allowed to be grumpy working under conditions I would expect from a third world country."},{"id":20304497,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2019-06","story":"NSA Improperly Collected U.S. Phone Records a Second Time","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing episode on nuclear weapon accidents and lost weapons.","quote":"John Olivers episode on it is also amazing.","text":"Also, a list of the nuclear weapons which got lost [LINK] There is a drove of stories around nuclear weapons, I also enjoyed \"Missile doors left open while Air Force nuclear officer slept\" with the explanation, that they left it open for the food delivery guy. [LINK] John Olivers episode on it is also amazing. [VIDEO] For a weird one meet one of the masterminds responsible for them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15324354,"author":"yeukhon","month":"2017-09","story":"‘Criticality safety event’ occurred at LANL’s plutonium facility","stance":"rec","why":"Covers outdated launch protocol and weapons security problems.","quote":"Watch [1] because reading the rest just make everyone dizzy.","text":"Nuclear warhead security has always been problematic, not just the facility producing the materials. Watch [1] because reading the rest just make everyone dizzy. Our launch protocol relies on very outdated technology. Not that there is anything wrong with old technology in general (as long as the protocol is well-established)... We don't even want to bring up the recent unfortunate Navy crashes... we ought to really step up. \"Shoot yourself in the foot.\" [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":8104686,"author":"1qaz2wsx3edc","month":"2014-07","story":"Two nuclear bombs nearly detonated in North Carolina in 1961","stance":"rec","why":"Good report on nuclear weapon accidents.","quote":"John Oliver did a good report on it.","text":"I'm guessing this popped up here after appearing on reddit yesterday. John Oliver did a good report on it. [VIDEO] [LINK] It's pretty frakked up. Edit: Oh, and the cold war is heating up: [LINK] None of this is settling."},{"id":21993708,"author":"PeterStuer","month":"2020-01","story":"Germany is moving beyond nuclear power, but at what cost?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for humans struggling with nuclear responsibility.","quote":"","text":"The pro-nuclear lobby likes to pretend it offers a clean solution. I really want to believe, as it would make things easy. Sadly, not only is nuclear not clean, the worst part is humans can't handle the required responsibility for this technology. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15160920,"author":"mikejb","month":"2017-09","story":"CTBTO statement on the unusual seismic event detected in the DPRK","stance":"reference","why":"Report on US nuclear weapons safety.","quote":"John Oliver's piece on Nuclear Weapons safety in the US","text":"I don't understand how that addresses the NK's advantage point. Also, if risk is an actual issue, how do 10 nukes in NK justify this extend of worry, in contrast to the weapons Russia and the US have? (For some reason I'm reminded of John Oliver's piece on Nuclear Weapons safety in the US [1]). If humanity is to be worried about NK having them, why shouldn't we be equally worried about Russia, China, France, the US, etc.? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12669066,"author":"d0mine","month":"2016-10","story":"U.S. government accuses Russia of hacking campaign to influence elections","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on nuclear weapons and military power.","quote":"","text":"Again, Russia won't even get a bronze medal by the \"active military\" metric. Unless you would call North Korea a superpower too; it is the poor metric. I give you nuclear weapons. Only US has more nuclear weapons. But does it matter in the real world? [VIDEO] (imagine what happens in Russia that has military budget 10 times less than US and no less corruption). Though I still don't understand the logic: Russia has nuclear weapons and therefore it is a superpower compared to US and China? (non-existent political power, isolated, under economic sanctions--what the word \"superpower\" even means in that case). I understand something has to justify an enormous US military budget--if you don't have real threats; you have to invent them. It wouldn't be unusual that a war were started under false pretences anyway. (if a presidential candidate can laugh while talking about the rape of a 12-year-old girl; she wouldn't even blink before launching the next war [LINK] )"},{"id":9419619,"author":"allendoerfer","month":"2015-04","story":"The Woman Who Ate Chernobyl's Apples","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for trusting developed nations with nuclear technology.","quote":"","text":"My problem with nuclear disasters or really any kind of bigger scale environmental damage is, that you have no control over other people's actions and failures. You therefore can not argue, that the technology is fine as long as we all use it securely, because you simply can not ensure that for countries you have no control over. And it is not only the small countries. We trust developed nations with nuclear technology, when we really should not [0]. Fukushima happened, many smaller scale incidents in the US happened [1] and in Germany the nuclear waste is a constant issue [2]. Unfortunately when using a shared resource like \"world security\", the free rider problem occurs, so everybody keeps using it. When you enlarge the time period in which we use nuclear power/weapons long enough the probability, that we mess things up on an even larger scale than Chernobyl or Fukushima ultimately approaches 1. While I hate, that people forget that nuclear power does run on fuel and we have to handle the waste for generations to come, I am very thankful, that they also forget to think of peak uranium [3], which in my mind as a layman might solve the problem. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["sGG-A80Tl5g",1],["sycgL3Qg_Ak",1],["JZ017D_JOPY",1]]},"Jd3-eiid-Uw":{"wy":"It is the classic reference for head-tracked parallax displays, cited whenever new 3D or holographic display tech appears.","q":{"x":"Can confirm, the 3D effect is 100% spot on","i":26861336,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":12967797,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2016-11","story":"Shadows and Blur Effects in Modern UI Design","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome head-tracking demo with more from the same creator.","quote":"Check out Johnny Lee's awesome","text":"Check out Johnny Lee's awesome \"Head Tracking for Desktop VR Displays using the WiiRemote\"! [VIDEO] More great stuff: [LINK]"},{"id":1036791,"author":"slyn","month":"2010-01","story":"Proof of Concept - See through OLED screen on a laptop","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Head-tracking demo from a channel full of hardware and software hacks.","quote":"Check out his entire youtube channel, it is full of interesting software/hardware hacks.","text":"Specifically: [VIDEO] Check out his entire youtube channel, it is full of interesting software/hardware hacks."},{"id":26861336,"author":"mattowen_uk","month":"2021-04","story":"This is what Doom looks like on a holographic display","stance":"rec","why":"Head-tracked perspective convincingly makes the screen feel like a window into 3D.","quote":"Can confirm, the 3D effect is 100% spot on","text":"> [VIDEO] I did this at the time. I downloaded his Wii app that shows the little red/white discs, and bought a battery powered Wii-bar thing that had the IR leds in it and strapped it to my head. Can confirm, the 3D effect is 100% spot on, even the smallest move of your head caused the display to adjust perspective and your brain is totally fooled into thinking the Wii display is a just a window into a 3D scene. tbh, it was the only cool thing I actually did with my Wii!"},{"id":48369194,"author":"cubefox","month":"2026-06","story":"Webcam head tracking, webcam to control in‑game FOV","stance":"reference","why":"Classic demo of monitor behaving like a window via head position.","quote":"Johnny Lee's classic Wii demo","text":"Wouldn't it be better to use head tracking to get the position of the head relative to the monitor, so the monitor behaves like a window? Like in Johnny Lee's classic Wii demo [1]. The way it currently works (rotating the view upon head rotation) doesn't really make sense because a monitor is not a head mounted display. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45711470,"author":"il_nets","month":"2025-10","story":"Glasses-free 3D using webcam head tracking","stance":"reference","why":"Original Johnny Lee head tracking video cited as the source.","quote":"","text":"You're probably recalling OG Johnny Lee video: [VIDEO] That's cool, what were the benefits/drawbacks of that compared to other VR headsets would you say?"},{"id":42192211,"author":"DontNoodles","month":"2024-11","story":"Hand Tracking for Mouse Input (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Original idea the commenter tried to reimplement with face tracking.","quote":"Johnny Lee's amazing idea","text":"I tried to implement Johnny Lee's amazing idea ( [VIDEO] ) using mediapipe face tracking. I could not move much far using simple webcams since it was getting difficult to determine the distance of the face from the camera when the face was turned. I had an Inel RealSense 415 depth tracking camera from a different project and it took care of the distance thing at least. But the jitter thing had me stumped for a long time and I put the project away. With your ideas, I get the strength to revisit it. Thanks!"},{"id":37345150,"author":"guiambros","month":"2023-09","story":"Wii Architecture – A Practical Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Johnny Lee's novel wiimote projects that never became commercial products.","quote":"using the then new wiimote in completely novel ways","text":"... or the various projects by Johnny Lee [1], using the then new wiimote in completely novel ways [2][3]. 15 years later, I'm still surprised these proof-of-concepts didn't evolve into something more commercial. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":34915368,"author":"jhallenworld","month":"2023-02","story":"Show HN: Parallax wallpaper engine for Linux and Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Wii remote used in reverse to create a fake window effect.","quote":"You need to use the Wii remote in reverse so you can make a fake window.","text":"You need to use the Wii remote in reverse so you can make a fake window. [VIDEO] (can't believe this was 15 years ago now..)"},{"id":28695588,"author":"vyrotek","month":"2021-09","story":"Simula One: an office-focused, standalone VR headset built on top of Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Wii head-tracking project as inspiration for window-like video calls.","quote":"this \"pancake\" problem reminded me of the old Wii head-tracking project.","text":"I absolutely agree. I'm very much a whiteboard hand-wavier guy. I explain with gestures as much as I do with the actual drawing (might be an Italian thing too). That said, this \"pancake\" problem reminded me of the old Wii head-tracking project.[1] There might be an interesting hybrid opportunity with that idea and 3d avatars. I feel like the head-tracking hack never really took off because it only worked as a solo experience. But since remote work is mostly us all individually sitting at a computer I could see it working better. Combine this idea with a large dedicated monitor/tv and now you have something that would literally just feel like a window/portal to the person you're talking to. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26860760,"author":"teekert","month":"2021-04","story":"This is what Doom looks like on a holographic display","stance":"reference","why":"Wii remote head tracking made a display act like a window in 2007.","quote":"it looks exactly like watching a display with a Wii remote on your head","text":"Cool, but, it looks exactly like watching a display with a Wii remote on your head [0, 2007]. What’s so special? Is it actually 3D this time? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25643669,"author":"xaedes","month":"2021-01","story":"Show HN: 3D-Parallax, labelfree 3D experience from a 2D image using parallax","stance":"reference","why":"Early 2007 demo of head-tracked parallax 3D using a Wii remote.","quote":"In my opinion the resulting 3D effect is MUCH better than viewing in stereo with one picture per eye.","text":"\"We offer an experience of 3D on a single 2D image using the parallax effect, i.e, the user is able move his real-time tracked face to visualize the depth effect.\" Since there are no examples I can't be sure if this is is what I think it is, but IF it is: I want this on huge monitor for any 3D game instead of clunky headgear VR or tiny smartphone AR. Months ago I also tested this with a small 3D visualization and very crude head tracking. The effect is damn awesome! To be able to move around in real space with the rendering adapting to it, makes it so immersive, even for my very crude tests. In my opinion the resulting 3D effect is MUCH better than viewing in stereo with one picture per eye. Here is an example from someone else from 2007: [VIDEO] From 2012: [LINK] Obviously this works for only one person viewing it, but does that really matter? There are a LOT of use cases where only a single person uses a single monitor for viewing, especially in these times. In fact it is the standard."},{"id":24810567,"author":"mdorazio","month":"2020-10","story":"Sony Unveils 'Spatial Reality Display' with Eye-Tracking for Glasses-Free 3D","stance":"reference","why":"Early proof of eye-tracked 3D display technology from 2007.","quote":"the tech has been proven dating back all the way to 2007","text":"What's interesting about this is that the tech has been proven dating back all the way to 2007 [1] and also re-implemented by enthusiasts many times over the years with better eye tracking via CV. What Sony has done here is refine it and package it in a way that's nice for end users. Personally, I'd like to see this implemented more cheaply in larger displays to act as virtual windows for indoor environments, like [2] from 2010, but with 3D video/rendering. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":21567500,"author":"mikeleung","month":"2019-11","story":"Colossal Holograms","stance":"reference","why":"2007 head-tracking demo using infrared LEDs and a Wii remote.","quote":"its amazing its been 12 years since that demo","text":"This reminds me of [VIDEO] (2007) Johnny Lee's demonstration using head tracking with infrared led's and a wii remote to implement the same thing. Obviously that limits the effect to the individual's head being tracked, but its amazing its been 12 years since that demo and we're still trying to commercialize the same affect."},{"id":10900558,"author":"Tepix","month":"2016-01","story":"A Canvas Made of Pixels","stance":"reference","why":"2007 head-tracked 3D display using a Wiimote.","quote":"you can also make the image it displays 3D as seen in this video from 2007","text":"Nice job! If you have a camera that tracks the viewer, you can also make the image it displays 3D as seen in this video from 2007: [VIDEO] (back then it used a Wiimote to track the head position)."},{"id":7273396,"author":"magicalist","month":"2014-02","story":"Project Tango","stance":"reference","why":"Johnny Lee's awesome Wii controller demos from 2007.","quote":"Johnny Lee was the guy with the awesome Wii Controller demos back in 2007","text":"\"- Johnny Lee and the ATAP-Project Tango Team\" Johnny Lee was the guy with the awesome Wii Controller demos back in 2007 (can't believe it's been that long). [VIDEO] edit: here's the full set of demos: [LINK] (also, I'm assuming it's the same guy, but his site says he's at Google now)"},{"id":6413416,"author":"aylons","month":"2013-09","story":"Go Pro on a soaring eagle [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates motion parallax as a strong 3D depth cue.","quote":"If you doubt it, look at this video","text":"Actually, stereoscopic vision is overrated. There are many hits our brain uses to make the 3D model of the world we have on our minds. Stereoscopic vision is one of them, but really just kick in for objects nearer than you arm cam hold. For distant objects, our brain uses other hints - that's why people aiming at far objects close one of their eyes. One of the strongest is the parallax effect, specially for medium distance and relative distance between objects. If you doubt it, look at this video: [VIDEO] Relative size and color fading works well for big distances - this is the prominent effect in parachuting videos. These are only two mecanisms, and immersion is not only about 3D sensation. Full periphery vision is of great value for this (IMAX, anyone?). Sound hints, brigthness, contrast, and a lot of other hints also help our brain making the 3D model."},{"id":4077142,"author":"wtracy","month":"2012-06","story":"Anamorphic Projections and the Floating Mona Lisa","stance":"reference","why":"More visually impressive demo of the same technique.","quote":"his demo is much more visually impressive.","text":"Johnny Lee's work with the WiiMote uses the same technique, and his demo is much more visually impressive. :-) [VIDEO]"},{"id":2721901,"author":"rkalla","month":"2011-07","story":"Augmented Reality 3d Video on iPad with Kinect ","stance":"reference","why":"Wii head-tracking 3D demo as a clever hack.","quote":"This reminds me a lot of what Johnny Lee did with the Wii soon after it was released with head-tracking 3D[3]","text":"So damn clever... releasing an SDK for the Kinect[1] was one of the smartest things Microsoft could have ever done and I think has gone a long way to contribute to it being the fastest selling piece of hardware in history[2] (did anyone know that? I sure didn't, and barely believed it reading it). This reminds me a lot of what Johnny Lee did with the Wii soon after it was released with head-tracking 3D[3] Part of me wants to stop using a computer and take up beet-farming because the cool factor seems so much less than what these guys are doing. Anyone on HN actively toying with a Kinect and want to share some video? [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2135535,"author":"arohner","month":"2011-01","story":"Why 3D doesn't work and never will.","stance":"reference","why":"Stunning 3D effect from a Wiimote and a standard TV.","quote":"This guy built a stunning demonstration giving a 3d effect with a wiimote and a standard TV","text":"This particular implementation will probably never work, but that doesn't mean \"3D movies\" in some form will never work. This guy built a stunning demonstration giving a 3d effect with a wiimote and a standard TV: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16522715,"author":"jordache","month":"2018-03","story":"A UI Experiment with the iPhone X’s Front-Facing Camera","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yo mark willson of co.design. Were you around 10 years ago? Let's tame the article title a bit huh? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1]]},"PdO3IP0Pro8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fun, amazing overview of writing systems whenever the alphabet or cuneiform comes up.","q":{"x":"I also recommend Thoth's Pill, an amazing historical overview of writing systems","i":21911132,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":25753646,"author":"MrsPeaches","month":"2021-01","story":"Who Invented the Alphabet?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun deep dive including Mesoamerican writing.","quote":"Highly recommend Thoth’s Pill [1] for a fun video on the subject.","text":"Highly recommend Thoth’s Pill [1] for a fun video on the subject. Goes deep but keeps it relatively light. Has the added bonus of going into Mesoamerican writing systems which are often underrepresented in these analyses. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21911132,"author":"HocusLocus","month":"2019-12","story":"How writing began, and other unexpectedly funny stories about cuneiform [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated history of writing with glyphs drawn in real time.","quote":"I also recommend Thoth's Pill, an amazing historical overview of writing systems","text":"I also recommend Thoth's Pill, an amazing historical overview of writing systems that breaks new ground in animated video production... as they are describing alphabet systems and letter figures they are drawn in real time and presented on a clear crisp background. Where most documentaries we've seen over the years show glimpses of photographs of ancient writing samples, often dimly lit or weathered and faded, tending to make it more about documents than language -- these folk are writing script and glyphs on computer, presenting detailed traces or brushing the characters and building alphabets, explaining down to the character/sound. For children this is precious. If I had seen something like this when I was young, it would have sucked me in and maybe changed the course of my life. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35219302,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2023-03","story":"Abecedarium","stance":"rec","why":"Animated history of writing.","quote":"you will enjoy \"Thoth's pill - an Animated history of writing","text":"If you like these topics, you will enjoy \"Thoth's pill - an Animated history of writing\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NcOb3Dilzjc",1],["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["vaCVh2SAZY4",1]]},"WIyTZDHuarQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, intuitive introduction to the pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) interpretation of quantum mechanics.","q":{"x":"an excellent pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) theory video by Veritasium[1]","i":18269477,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18269477,"author":"garfieldnate","month":"2018-10","story":"Interpretations of quantum mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent pilot wave theory video prompting interest in interpretations.","quote":"an excellent pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) theory video by Veritasium[1]","text":"I found this while following up on an excellent pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) theory video by Veritasium[1]. I never knew there were so many interpretations! 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17642759,"author":"rimher","month":"2018-07","story":"David Bohm, Quantum Mechanics and Enlightenment","stance":"rec","why":"Showcases the pilot wave idea.","quote":"Great video that showcases the Pilot Wave idea","text":"Great video that showcases the Pilot Wave idea: [VIDEO] I really do hope that we can find a way to make sense of everything in Physics, but Bohm's description of the infinite in the article is rather fascinating. Maybe there's no way out after all"},{"id":13157753,"author":"DiThi","month":"2016-12","story":"The “dead and alive” cat myth","stance":"rec","why":"Explains deterministic interpretation of quantum mechanics.","quote":"This video explains it well","text":"A bit unrelated, but there's a possibility that quantum mechanics are not exactly probabilistic and that they're actually deterministic, while still being compatible with observed quantum phenomena. This video explains it well [VIDEO]"},{"id":12895722,"author":"lisivka","month":"2016-11","story":"Close to absolute zero, particles exhibit their quantum nature","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the pilot wave idea without long books.","quote":"for explanation. No need to read long books.","text":"I highly recommend to watch this video first: [LINK] , to get the idea. Then this: [VIDEO] , for explanation. No need to read long books."},{"id":46031579,"author":"rhdunn","month":"2025-11","story":"New magnetic component discovered in the Faraday effect","stance":"reference","why":"Oil surface in a petri dish replicates the effect.","quote":"Veritasium did a video on this [1] with a surface of oil to replicate the effect on a petri dish.","text":"Veritasium did a video on this [1] with a surface of oil to replicate the effect on a petri dish. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13402203,"author":"goatlover","month":"2017-01","story":"The Trouble with Quantum Mechanics","stance":"reference","why":"Oil droplets as a classical analogue of the pilot wave.","quote":"a classical version of the pilot wave, and it does reproduce some quantum behavior, including the double slit","text":"There is the silicone oil droplet phenomena which is a classical version of the pilot wave, and it does reproduce some quantum behavior, including the double slit. That a pilot wave has been discovered (granted at macro scale) does make one reconsider. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13090090,"author":"joantune","month":"2016-12","story":"First real evidence of a strange quantum distortion in empty space","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot wave explanation questioned against new virtual particle evidence.","quote":"does this mean that this explanation for Quantum mechanics has been vindicated?","text":"\"In the classical physics of Newton and Einstein, the vacuum of space is entirely empty, but the theory of quantum mechanics assumes something very different. According to quantum electrodynamics (QED) - a quantum theory that describes how light and matter interact - it’s predicted that space is actually full of 'virtual particles' that pop in and out of existence and mess with the activity of light particles (photons) as they zip around the Universe.\" So, does this mean that this explanation for Quantum mechanics [VIDEO] has been vindicated? i.e. the double slit with the fields being the cause of the oddity instead of... something that is hard to explain.. including the observer effect"},{"id":13090501,"author":"contravariant","month":"2016-12","story":"First real evidence of a strange quantum distortion in empty space","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot wave theory discussed against virtual particles.","quote":"","text":">So, does this mean that this explanation for Quantum mechanics [VIDEO] has been vindicated? If anything the virtual particles are an alternative to the pilot wave theory. Instead of having quantum effects mediated by virtual particles the pilot wave theory uses a 'pilot wave'. Now as far as I know most phenomena caused by virtual particles can be explained by the pilot wave, but I'm not sure how easily. Either way, results like these which seem to be in favour of the existence of virtual particles are weak evidence against the pilot wave theory."}],"r":[["8SkdfdXWYaI",1],["nsaUX48t0w8",1]]},"d6PyyatqJSE":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a deeper, more legitimate interview on fasting mechanics than typical diet content.","q":{"x":"For those interested in a deeper dive on the benefits of water-only fasting","i":18007596,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19258104,"author":"itchyouch","month":"2019-02","story":"Exercise, fasting shown to help cells shed defective proteins","stance":"rec","why":"Legitimate information on fasting and its mechanisms","quote":"Here's some legitimate information on a fast.","text":"There's health benefits and potential longevity benefits. I would hardly call fasting a fad. It has been around through the ages. Here's some legitimate information on a fast. [VIDEO] We know enough about some of the mechanisms involved and how it can promote healing the body by offering it a \"reset\" so-to-speak via autophagy."},{"id":18007596,"author":"bodhibyte","month":"2018-09","story":"Molecule produced during fasting has anti-aging effect on vascular system","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper dive on water fasting, fasting-mimicking diet, time-restricted feeding","quote":"For those interested in a deeper dive on the benefits of water-only fasting and diets that try to mimic its benefits","text":"I'm on a day 4 of a water-only fast and have completed a two-week medically supervised fast as well as many other shorter fast. As an anecdote, I can say water-only fasting in combination with a high nutrient density plant-based diet has helped me overcome some serious allergies and other health conditions. As an added bonus, I've lost 65 pounds from my peak weight over the last few years and my weight has been stable. It's not a panacea, but I've found it to be a net benefit. For those interested in a deeper dive on the benefits of water-only fasting and diets that try to mimic its benefits, as well as time-restricted feeding, see: Valter Longo, Ph.D. on Fasting-Mimicking Diet & Fasting for Longevity, Cancer & Multiple Sclerosis [1] Dr. Valter Longo on Resetting Autoimmunity and Rejuvenating Systems with Prolonged Fasting & the FMD [2] Dr. Satchin Panda on Time-Restricted Feeding and Its Effects on Obesity, Muscle Mass & Heart Health [3] Dr. Satchin Panda on Practical Implementation of Time-Restricted Eating & Shift Work Strategies [4] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [Edit to fix typos and formatting]"},{"id":16557818,"author":"pgt","month":"2018-03","story":"The antidepressant effect of sleep deprivation","stance":"rec","why":"Good interview about intermittent fasting","quote":"Good interview about I.F.","text":"Wow! I awoke early this morning from a bit of a bender last night and have a similar feeling of mania (the positive, productive kind). At first I attributed it to a GABA rebound, but thinking back, a sleep fast has historically injected a sense of urgency and vigour. I have been fasting intermittently after reading Valter Longo's metabolic oxidation research (the term they used for aging research to get it published). I skip breakfast and try to leave 16-18 hours between meals. Have been losing weight but keeping muscle tone almost effortlessly. My skin has also improved. No negative effect so far. Good interview about I.F.: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13689028,"author":"ndb6000","month":"2017-02","story":"Fasting leads to stress resistance, self-renewal, and regeneration (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Interview covering fasting mechanics and clinical trials","quote":"I enjoyed this recent interview with Valter Longo on the subject of fasting","text":"I enjoyed this recent interview with Valter Longo on the subject of fasting: [VIDEO] They discuss underlying mechanics and various clinical trials and much more..."},{"id":19522621,"author":"dpatrick86","month":"2019-03","story":"History of Biosphere 2","stance":"reference","why":"Longo discusses Walford's Biosphere 2 influence on his work","quote":"Starts around here around 00:01:45...","text":"Interestingly, the legacy of Biosphere 2 includes the recently emerging fasting and fasting-mimicking science demonstrating some interesting healthspan promoting properties. Dr. Roy Walford of Biosphere 2 helped kick off the caloric restriction field thanks to his experiences. Valter Longo explains that his tenure in Walford's lab as one of the preludes to some of his great work demonstrating that some of the benefits of CR are achievable by using discrete periods of fasting as opposed to long-term restriction, which has obvious challenges of practicality and undesirable effects like emaciation just to name a few. He talks about Dr. Walford's time in biosphere 2 and how it impacted both Walford's work and, ultimately, his own. Starts around here around 00:01:45... [VIDEO]"},{"id":18202649,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-10","story":"Jason Fung explains why intermittent fasting diets work","stance":"reference","why":"One of several interviews with Longo on the diet","quote":"Some interviews with him on it (there are more on youtube)","text":"Dr. Valter Longo says you need only 5 days of fasting. [LINK] Also, it seems to regenerate β cells for Type 1 diabetes humans, although I would imagine the \"regular\" fasting would do that, too since it's a more extreme version of the FMD: [LINK] Some interviews with him on it (there are more on youtube): [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Cym4TZwTCNU",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["-R-eqJDQ2nU",1]]},"uqKGREZs6-w":{"wy":"Recommended as a sobering, accessible overview of the practical challenges of living on Mars.","q":{"x":"sobering run down of the realities when living on mars","i":20160885,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40790106,"author":"notTooFarGone","month":"2024-06","story":"Why Nothing Can Grow on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Explains radiation and sustainability difficulties of a Mars colony.","quote":"Nice kurzgesagt video","text":"I think you vastly underestimate the amount of radiation and how hard it would be to build a colony that is protected enough and is \"self sustaining\". Nice kurzgesagt video: [VIDEO] If we manage to terraform mostly using robots and bacteria that would be so much easier."},{"id":26942855,"author":"Etheryte","month":"2021-04","story":"Elon Musk on Mars efforts: “A bunch of people will probably die.”","stance":"rec","why":"Outlines challenges of inhabiting Mars: radiation, temperature, microdust.","quote":"Kurzgesagt has a great video outlining many of the challenges [0].","text":"There is no sugarcoating it, Mars is a hostile environment and to claim otherwise would simply be lying. Between the radiation, temperature, microdust and more, inhabiting Mars will be very hard. Kurzgesagt has a great video outlining many of the challenges [0]. Regardless of how meticulously you prepare, mistakes on Mars will lead to death and it's good to be honest and upfront about it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20160885,"author":"abledon","month":"2019-06","story":"Four Mars missions are launching next year","stance":"rec","why":"Sobering rundown of realities of living on Mars, like soil shielding.","quote":"sobering run down of the realities when living on mars","text":"sobering run down of the realities when living on mars: [VIDEO] The layers of soil we'd have to cover our buildings in really got me!"},{"id":19388543,"author":"gtsteve","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: I am 33 yrs young, will we form a colony in Mars within my lifetime?","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of what a Mars base would look like and its problems.","quote":"Perhaps watch this video for a nice overview of what that might look like","text":"Perhaps watch this video for a nice overview of what that might look like: [VIDEO] I think you'll probably see baby steps towards it but there are a lot of problems to overcome."},{"id":19142312,"author":"chriskanan","month":"2019-02","story":"Mars One, which offered 1-way trips to Mars, declared bankrupt","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Mars to Stay is a legitimate idea and makes a lot of sense for colonization","text":"So many comments here are really depressing and lack a sense of adventure and romance. Yes, Mars One did not have a sound plan, but it inspired a certain kind of person who wants a grand adventure with impact. Mars to Stay is a legitimate idea and makes a lot of sense for colonization: [LINK] The Mars One idea of turning the colonists life into a reality TV show would be the first reality TV show I'd ever want to watch and would make a fantastic documentary. So, while Mars One was severely flawed, I think the Mars to Stay philosophy makes a lot of sense if we want to colonize Mars. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Ufmu1WD2TSk",1],["2wq9x2QcZN0",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1]]},"yQ6VOOd73MA":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on consciousness, anesthesia and perception as a fascinating look at how much of experience the brain constructs.","q":{"x":"Both of these videos are great resources on how much of what you experience of reality is made up or filled in by your brain","i":39800635,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39800635,"author":"pixl97","month":"2024-03","story":"Visualization 'demonic' face distortions in a case of prosopometamorphopsia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great resources on how much of reality the brain fills in.","quote":"Both of these videos are great resources on how much of what you experience of reality is made up or filled in by your brain","text":"Perception Deception: Nova [LINK] Your Brain - Who's in control: Nova [VIDEO] Both of these videos are great resources on how much of what you experience of reality is made up or filled in by your brain at any given moment. I believe it's the Perception Deception episode that shows us how little of our vision is actual HD quality and that an external observer doing eye tracking on you can present a different reality to you than a 3rd party looking at the same object you are (on a screen at least, little bit harder in the real world)."},{"id":36323603,"author":"lloydde","month":"2023-06","story":"Consciousness and Anaesthesia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"NOVA documentary episode touching on anesthesia and the brain.","quote":"Both episodes are fascinating and eye opening.","text":"The topic, but not this exception, is touched on in “Your Brain: Who's in Control? | Full Documentary | NOVA | PBS“ Episode 2 of a two-part series, premiered May 24, 2023. Both episodes are fascinating and eye opening. 08:36 Anesthesia and the Brain [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":45561804,"author":"pixl97","month":"2025-10","story":"The neurons that let us see what isn't there","stance":"rec","why":"Companion NOVA episode to Perception Deception on vision.","quote":"Also Your Brain: Who's in control Nova PBS","text":"When talking about vision and what you see and don't I like bringing up the Your Brain: Perception Deception Nova PBS episode. [LINK] Also Your Brain: Who's in control Nova PBS [VIDEO]"},{"id":38894260,"author":"seadan83","month":"2024-01","story":"Focus and Flow: trade-offs in programmer productivity (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Musician in MRI shows prefrontal cortex inhibition during improvised rap.","quote":"a PBS documentary that looked at a musician in an MRI rapping a memorized piece vs an ad-hoc rap.","text":"I think the title suggests larger questions. What is Flow? How many different kinds of flow states are there? To what extent are they equal? I recently listened to \"Your Brain: Who's in Control?\" [1], a PBS documentary that looked at a musician in an MRI rapping a memorized piece vs an ad-hoc rap. They found the musician's prefrontal cortex was inhibited when doing the ad-hoc exercise. The prefrontal cortex is used as a \"filter\" center, to predict and evaluate the potential outcome of actions and basically to tell you when something is a bad idea. When developers are in flow, and they are creating brand new code - do their prefrontal cortex'es also become inhibited? Is that the same kind of flow state? I believe there are multiple types of flow state: - gardening/choirs, time drops away and you lose yourself in the trimming, edging and mundane gardening tasks - gaming, when I was younger and after an approx 16 hour binge of Civilization, my mind was still in the game. I was asleep and was still thinking in-game, solving problems, and wondering \"where is the end turn button.\" Being in that level of flow state, the thoughts of 'end turn', 'move unit' - just occurred, my mind's connection to the game through my body was erased, the motor movements to click, hotkeys, all gone from conscious thought and just automatic. - music: Type (A) - playing a well known piece, it just happens. Type (B) Improv, jazz musicians do this all the time for hours on end. - biking/exercise: after a f"},{"id":37668047,"author":"pixl97","month":"2023-09","story":"Common infections can spark psychiatric illnesses in children","stance":"reference","why":"Lists PBS Your Brain documentaries.","quote":"PBS: Your Brain: Who's in Control?","text":"PBS: Your Brain: Perception Deception [LINK] PBS: Your Brain: Who's in Control? [VIDEO] Rationalization is mostly post ad hoc."}],"r":[["lXfEK8G8CUI",1],["uOSnQM1Zu4w",1],["j4kI2h3iotA",1],["P55t6eryY3g",1],["ZeecOKBus3Q",1],["F_7S1eqKsFk",1]]},"43CMV8KIs3E":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as the best explanation of the ruling, in more depth and with more accuracy than press coverage.","q":{"x":"I'd suggest watching the excellent video by Hoeg Law.","i":28490542,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28490542,"author":"simondotau","month":"2021-09","story":"Epic vs. Apple: Judgment Day – Who Won? Who Lost? and Why?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed ruling walkthrough noting where media coverage was wrong.","quote":"I'd suggest watching the excellent video by Hoeg Law.","text":"I'd suggest watching the excellent video by Hoeg Law. He has followed the case from the outset and goes through the ruling in intricate detail. Even within the first few minutes he starts noting how much of the media coverage is wrong or overstated. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30205214,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-02","story":"Apple will charge 27% commission for alternative payment systems in Netherlands","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent overview of the decision, though long; summary in first 15 minutes.","quote":"There's an excellent overview of the US Federal court's 180 page decision on Youtube, but it's rather long.","text":"There's an excellent overview of the US Federal court's 180 page decision on Youtube, but it's rather long. The diction is good, so it's still understandable when set to run at a faster speed. The first 15 minutes is a summary and the balance of the video is a more detailed look. [VIDEO] TL;DW, the judge makes it clear that Apple can still charge a cut even if developers use someone else's payment system. She also hints strongly that Epic screwed up by not challenging the size of Apple's cut instead of challenging their right to take a cut."},{"id":29170676,"author":"rvz","month":"2021-11","story":"Judge orders Apple to allow external payment options for App Store","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the ruling in full depth, countering media spin.","quote":"it's better to watch the Hoeg Law video explaining the ruling in full depth","text":"Absolutely correct. The Verge and other media reporters continues to omit this important note from the ruling itself from [0]: Under all models, Apple would be entitled to a commission or licensing fee, even if IAP was optional [0] (p68) Seems like The Verge loves to spin their way into misinformation since they know that the majority of readers won't read the ruling text at all. For those that don't have time, it's better to watch the Hoeg Law video explaining the ruling in full depth. [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28497012,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2021-09","story":"Epic vs. Apple injunction doesn't allow for alternative in-app payment mechanism","stance":"rec","why":"Two and a half hour summary of the 185 page judgement.","quote":"HoagLaw offers a two and a half hour summary of the 185 page judgement","text":"If you're willing to wade through a very long YouTube video, HoagLaw offers a two and a half hour summary of the 185 page judgement: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28497614,"author":"rvz","month":"2021-09","story":"Epic vs. Apple injunction doesn't allow for alternative in-app payment mechanism","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at the trial correcting media misinformation.","quote":"I also recommend those interested to watch the first 4 minutes of the Hoeg Law video and even the whole video","text":"Precisely. As I and many others already highlighted it is not over yet and they are both likely going to appeal this. The media outright jumped into conclusions with their headlines and caused this hype of misinformation to spread very quickly and now we have to cut thought it and correct the wrongs they reported. I also recommend those interested to watch the first 4 minutes of the Hoeg Law video and even the whole video for an in depth look into the trial. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["29RkHH-psrY",1],["pBiWtJJN5zk",1],["JRcXUuQYR90",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1],["g6bOwQdCJrc",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1]]},"VESMU7JfVHU":{"wy":"Recommended in moka pot threads as the best way to see how the pot works from the inside.","q":{"x":"There's also a fantastic Youtube video in a neutron beam facility of a Moka pot","i":39101774,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39101774,"author":"jarvist","month":"2024-01","story":"The humble brilliance of Italy's moka coffee pot (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full brewing process visible in neutron beam.","quote":"There's also a fantastic Youtube video in a neutron beam facility of a Moka pot","text":"There's also a fantastic Youtube video in a neutron beam facility of a Moka pot, in which you can see the full process of brewing. The water and plastic handle appear 'black' as hydrogen scatters neutrons so strongly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37345221,"author":"msds","month":"2023-09","story":"X-ray CT scans of coffee equipment: Aeropress, Fellow kettle, Moka pot","stance":"rec","why":"Neutron imaging shows water inside the moka pot.","quote":"they're much cooler looking if you're doing neutron imaging and can see the water","text":"CT is the wrong imaging tech for Moka pots at least - they're much cooler looking if you're doing neutron imaging and can see the water: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29349322,"author":"simon_acca","month":"2021-11","story":"The Humble Brilliance of Italy's Moka Coffee Pot (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Neutron imaging shows how a moka pot works.","quote":"have a look at this neutron imaging of the pot in action","text":"For anybody wondering how a moka pot works, have a look at this neutron imaging of the pot in action: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FihU5JxmnBg",1]]},"agzNANfNlTs":{"wy":"Cited as a thoughtful video essay and for its model of in-group hierarchy in political threads.","q":{"x":"I think Innuendo Studios/Ian Danskin explains this mechanism very very well","i":31602104,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31602104,"author":"gaze","month":"2022-06","story":"Microsoft Purview: Additional classifiers for Communication Compliance (preview)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the mindset defending billionaires and hierarchy as natural","quote":"I think Innuendo Studios/Ian Danskin explains this mechanism very very well","text":"Yeah it's not necessarily true. I think Innuendo Studios/Ian Danskin explains this mechanism very very well [VIDEO] Many people think Jeff Bezos should exist and have his wealth because he got there by playing the game better than everyone else, and that this game is just the way things are. He earned it. Attempts to change the game will just make everything worse and people won't get what they deserve, and thus these attempts are unethical. Equal societies are an absurd liberal fantasy. My attempts to advocate that Jeff Bezos shouldn't have the money he does are actually just selfish attempts to cheat at the game and stuff my own pockets with money and get something I haven't earned. The real issue here is a lack of discipline. Watch the rest of the videos. People who think like this largely can't be argued with."},{"id":45229441,"author":"Terr_","month":"2025-09","story":"Life, work, death and the peasant: Rent and extraction","stance":"rec","why":"Useful model for understanding conservative versus liberal base assumptions","quote":"I've found useful, from a (leftist) video essay","text":"> I sometimes wonder if we could design a better system today [...] optimizing for values we have today like freedom, balance of power and equality of opportunity. I think it's important to point out that some people... don't seem to share the same ground-assumptions, and it's forming a rather sharp divide in modern US politics. There's a model for analyzing \"how could you think that\" disagreements which I've found useful, from a (leftist) video essay: > See, when you talk to our conservative friend, you operate as though you have the same base assumptions [...] > Since we live with both of these frameworks [democratic egalitarianism, capitalist competitive sorting] in our minds, and most of the things we do in our day-to-day lives can be justified by either one, we don't often notice the contradiction between them, and it's easy to imagine whichever one tends to be our default is everyone else's default as well. [...] > Your conservative friend thinks you're naive for thinking the system even can be changed, and his is the charitable interpretation [...] Many conservatives assume liberals [...] know The Hierarchy is eternal, that there will always be people at the top and people at the bottom, so any claim towards making things equal must be a Trojan-horse for something that benefits them. [...] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19585309,"author":"pxc","month":"2019-04","story":"The golden age of YouTube is over","stance":"rec","why":"Example of thoughtful video essay content on YouTube","quote":"lengthy, thoughtful, and often artistic video essays and skits addressing culture and politics","text":"My favorite corner of YouTube— lengthy, thoughtful, and often artistic video essays and skits addressing culture and politics— is flourishing right now. Some examples: Unfolding Ideas – [LINK] ContraPoints – [LINK] Philosophy Tube – [LINK] Lindsay Ellis – [LINK] Innuendo Studios – [VIDEO] HBomberguy – [LINK] and so on. And it seems like it's only in the past couple of years that many of these channels have reached their current levels of artistry and production value. Most stuff on YouTube is trash, but there's plenty else to watch."},{"id":43725292,"author":"phatskat","month":"2025-04","story":"Federal Government's letter to Harvard demanding changes [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the shrinking circle of the in-group","quote":"","text":"> I guess it's not fascism if its against people you disagree with. What’s fun about fascism ™ is that the circle of people being agreed with gets smaller as power is concentrated more towards a central figure. What’s his face, the Jewish white supremacist? Surely somewhere deep in the back of his mind is the warning bell that will start going off if we reach a certain stage of fascism. The “out group” starts with the most marginalized and vulnerable and moves inward. [There’s Always a Bigger Fish]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":43437098,"author":"slimscsi","month":"2025-03","story":"The Road Not Taken Is Guaranteed Minimum Income","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as relevant to attitudes about how poor people spend money","quote":"","text":"This mentality is exactly the problem. The idea that people with more money should get to decide how people with less money get to spend it. And if they don't sped it the way they want, Its waste. reminds me a lot of Innuendo Studios \"Always a bigger fish\" video [VIDEO]"},{"id":42154853,"author":"phatskat","month":"2024-11","story":"Maybe Bluesky has \"won\"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the idea that the in-group circle keeps shrinking","quote":"","text":"This is what we see in fascism and authoritarianism - the goal is to always make the circle smaller. You start with immutable traits (race, gender, hair color, etc) to chunk out a large part of the population, but once they’re gone, that small circle needs to get smaller. There’s Always a Bigger Fish[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26899351,"author":"random314","month":"2021-04","story":"Amazon 2020 Letter to Shareholders","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as backing for argument about hierarchy and conservatism","quote":"","text":"Standard conservative thinking. Society is meant to be hierarchical, bottom feeders are supposed to serve the small percentile of elites. This is the natural order of things. Social support systems for the bottom feeders makes the bottom feeders entitled. Democratic government focus inevitably moves towards social programs which must be pushed back hard by the elite. Most elites inherit their elite status. We must ignore this and talk about anecdotes of how some software engineer made a lot of money in Silicon valley. Buying bitcoin and TSLA a few years ago is also an undeniable marker of extraordinarily elite thinking, which society must reward. We must never bluntly state our beliefs but cloak it in other acceptable words, so you don't get canceled by the SJWs and bottom feeders. The naturally superior and their offspring will and must be allowed to ascend the social hierarchy. BTW, conservatives have larger amygdala. Anterior cingulate cortex, an area that helps detect errors and resolve conflicts, tends to be larger in liberals. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["ICp2-EUKQAI",1],["jzMFoNZeZm0",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1]]},"lHLpKzUxjGk":{"wy":"Recommended whenever latency, buffering or VR comfort comes up, as an accessible account from someone who dealt with it directly.","q":{"x":"a lecture where he describes the difficulties in VR in a manner that anybody with minimal programming experience can understand","i":15044607,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14801104,"author":"chubot","month":"2017-07","story":"Terminal and shell performance","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Carmack on end-to-end latency across hardware and software in VR.","quote":"This talk blew my mind and made me feel like a terrible engineer.","text":"If you care about end-to-end latency, I highly recommend this talk by John Carmack: [VIDEO] This talk blew my mind and made me feel like a terrible engineer. He's talking about end-to-end latency in VR, which actually has a commercial motivation because VR products with high latency will make you sick. (this obviously doesn't happen with shells and terminals!) He's talking about the buffering and filtering at every step of the way. And it's not just software -- sensors have their own controllers to do filtering, which requires buffering, before your OS kernel can even SEE the the first byte, let alone user space. On the other side, display devices and audio output devices also do nontrivial processing after you've sent them your data. It's an integrated hardware/software problem and Carmack is great at explaining it! It's a dense, long-ish talk, but worth it."},{"id":35994625,"author":"Dig1t","month":"2023-05","story":"Apple's Reality Pro headset compromised","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack on VR nausea and how to solve it.","quote":"There's an excellent video of John Carmack talking about this and how to solve it.","text":">Also, prolonged use causes nausea in a large percentage of people. There's an excellent video of John Carmack talking about this and how to solve it. This is much less of an issue now. [VIDEO] I agree with you though that there aren't any killer apps for VR/AR yet, and it remains to be seen if there will be any at all."},{"id":15044607,"author":"waivek","month":"2017-08","story":"John Carmack on Functional Programming (2013) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible lecture on the difficulties of VR.","quote":"a lecture where he describes the difficulties in VR in a manner that anybody with minimal programming experience can understand","text":"[VIDEO] I disagree. See the above link for a lecture where he describes the difficulties in VR in a manner that anybody with minimal programming experience can understand."},{"id":14801116,"author":"chubot","month":"2017-07","story":"Terminal and shell performance","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack on end-to-end hardware/software latency.","quote":"an excellent talk by John Carmack on this very subject","text":"Oh definitely, see my comment here about an excellent talk by John Carmack on this very subject (end-to-end hardware/software latency in VR): [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36541200,"author":"hoten","month":"2023-06","story":"How does Linux handle writes?","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack lecture on reducing VR latency mentioning buffers.","quote":"John Carmack's lecture on systems engineering / reducing latency for VR mentions buffers repeatedly","text":"John Carmack's lecture on systems engineering / reducing latency for VR mentions buffers repeatedly: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["XUT8ec24anM",1]]},"4F72VULWFvc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a thorough treatment of replacing conditionals with polymorphism and why flags and deep ifs can be code smells.","q":{"x":"This talk gives a great overview of why boolean flags (rather, if-statements) can be a code smell","i":31464312,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31464312,"author":"Mr_P","month":"2022-05","story":"Flags Are a Code Smell (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Why boolean flags and deep if-statements are a code smell.","quote":"This talk gives a great overview of why boolean flags (rather, if-statements) can be a code smell","text":"It's not so much the existence of the flag, itself, but rather using an if-statement at the deepest-level of the call stack to conditionally modify behavior. This talk gives a great overview of why boolean flags (rather, if-statements) can be a code smell: [VIDEO] OP's blogpost advocates for data-oriented design (e.g. Entity Component Systems) as a mechanism for avoiding this, whereas the talk I've linked advocates for OOP. Both mechanisms are equally valid (imho) and are inline with widely-adopted industry practices for software architecture."},{"id":1454656,"author":"VBprogrammer","month":"2010-06","story":"Criminal Overengineering","stance":"rec","why":"Better argument for OO principles than the blog.","quote":"I believe the google clean code talks gives a far better arguement than I ever could.","text":"I completely disagree with the example given in this blog. I don't have time to give a full explaination but I believe the google clean code talks gives a far better arguement than I ever could. On the general principle I agree that overengineering is to be avoided, but I actually think the example shows a clear disregard for Object Orientated principles. [VIDEO]"},{"id":837935,"author":"derwiki","month":"2009-09","story":"Using Polymorphism instead of conditionals when programming","stance":"rec","why":"More comprehensive treatment of replacing conditionals with polymorphism.","quote":"which is more comprehensive than the posted article.","text":"Google posted a tech talk about this a while ago: [VIDEO] which is more comprehensive than the posted article."},{"id":38285324,"author":"tacitusarc","month":"2023-11","story":"Push ifs up and fors down","stance":"reference","why":"Making decisions early via polymorphism is an old idea.","quote":"It was old 15 years ago.","text":"I think this is actually a great example of why `if` should be \"pushed up.\" The objective of the code is to perform an particular operation on the walrus, given a condition. The is actually ifs being instead of polymorphism and a type system. Why does the walrus have these two functions, which must be called in different scenarios? Why not have a single function, and two types, and the correct type is passed in? Even given the current structure, we could accomplish this: // There are better ways to accomplish this, depending on the language func frobnicate(walrus) { return walrus.frobnicate(); } func transmogrify(walrus) { return walrus.transmogrify(); } // Somewhere high in the code if condition { processing_function = frobnicate } else { processing_function = transmogrify } // Somewhere else in the code for walrus in walruses { processing_function() } If the decisions are made as early as possible, they do not need to be littered throughout the code. The guts of the code can run without branches, performing the same operations every time, the output only modified through the construction graph. Of course, this is not a new idea: [VIDEO] It was old 15 years ago."},{"id":11873793,"author":"efraim","month":"2016-06","story":"Anti-if: The Missing Patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Misko Hevery talk using a similar example.","quote":"Misko Hevery did a Google Tech Talk about this some years ago","text":"Misko Hevery did a Google Tech Talk about this some years ago, he also use a very similar example with switching on the birds. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5157394,"author":"wcoenen","month":"2013-02","story":"How A Pull Request Rocked My World","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on eliminating conditionals with polymorphism.","quote":"This google tech talk is basically about the same thing, eliminating conditionals with polymorphism","text":"This google tech talk is basically about the same thing, eliminating conditionals with polymorphism: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["u9KNtnCZDMI",1]]},"Mvse4Xhzwuk":{"wy":"HN recommends the series for explaining why unleaded avgas has been so hard to approve, usually in threads about FAA and EPA fuel decisions.","q":{"x":"AVWeb made 2 videos on the subject of leaded avgas and on how hard it is to get it approved.","i":32688024,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48874770,"author":"aftbit","month":"2026-07","story":"Leaded Gas Was a Known Poison the Day It Was Invented (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good series on G100UL approval and FAA delays.","quote":"Avweb has done a good series of videos on this.","text":"We're working on it, slowly but surely. This has been a known problem for a long time, at least since 2009 if not earlier. GAMI's G100UL was approved as a 100LL replacement in 2022, but there is still some controversy. Avweb has done a good series of videos on this. There were some real engineering concerns and some typical aviation conservative decision making. But really, it's a tragedy of the FAA fumbling the ball for decades at this point. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":38042348,"author":"DigiDigiorno","month":"2023-10","story":"EPA moves towards banning leaded aviation gas","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful, humorous summary of the unleaded avgas saga.","quote":"here are a few insightful videos that summarize the story","text":"The uphill battle of unleaded avgas leaves both the main players in the industry and the regulators themselves looking bad—but in a banal kinda way. I enjoy AVWeb on YT for AV news (really, I just enjoy the humor/personality of Paul Bertorelli lol), here are a few insightful videos that summarize the story: The Long, Twisted And Slightly Ridiculous Story of Avgas Part 1 (14:22) [LINK] Part 2 (15:15) [VIDEO] and G100UL Approved Now What? (When the FAA approved the first unleaded 100-octane avgas a year ago) [LINK]"},{"id":32688024,"author":"feb","month":"2022-09","story":"FAA approves unleaded fuel for piston fleet","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how hard approving unleaded avgas has been.","quote":"AVWeb made 2 videos on the subject of leaded avgas and on how hard it is to get it approved.","text":"Impressive work by GAMI who are working on it since decades. AVWeb made 2 videos on the subject of leaded avgas and on how hard it is to get it approved. In addition, the FAA launched several failed attempts with the industry at large. * [LINK] The Long, Twisted And Slightly Ridiculous Story of Avgas Part 1 * [VIDEO] The Long, Twisted And Slightly Ridiculous Story of Avgas Part 2 When reading the announcement, it looks like some municipalities in California started banning the sale of leaded avgas and I suspect that helped to push the FAA to finally move in approving G1000UL. Not surprisingly, they will start providing it to the California market before the other states. In the announcement on avweb.com ( [LINK] ), they also credit Mark Baker from the AOAP to encourage the FAA to complete the project."}],"r":[["9F-WngVMJBQ",3]]},"SAc0vQCC6UQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the creator's own account of why Node was built and how he thinks about humility.","q":{"x":"He didn't want to use an ecosystem that had existing blocking code.","i":3983911,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":6500015,"author":"sturadnidge","month":"2013-10","story":"Rubinius 2.0 released","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into why Dahl started fresh rather than extend Ruby","quote":"This video gives some good insight into what led to his decision to start fresh","text":"The creator of Node was something of a Rubyist, i believe the main reason was around the stdlib / ecosystem not being event oriented, making it difficult to build non-blocking applications. EventMachine may well have strategies to defeat this, I just don't know. This video gives some good insight into what led to his decision to start fresh rather than try and change things in Ruby [VIDEO] So that's probably the biggest thing about Node - everything in the core is built with event orientation in mind, so you know you're not going to hit something that will block and kill you, and that minset is pervasive in userland too."},{"id":3983911,"author":"SjuulJanssen","month":"2012-05","story":"The love and hate of Node.js","stance":"rec","why":"Why Node was built for non-blocking IO on a clean slate","quote":"He didn't want to use an ecosystem that had existing blocking code.","text":"This might answer your question: [VIDEO] TLDR: He didn't want to use an ecosystem that had existing blocking code. He wanted node to be pure in te sence that nothing was blocking. Starting on a clean slate enabled him to get everything pure non-blocking from the ground up. He admits that Js isn't the best language. But his rationale for not using an existing ecosystem (when it comes to IO) is not bad."},{"id":3354462,"author":"nodesocket","month":"2011-12","story":"Trolls (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Ryan Dahl on being humble","quote":"Love what Ryan Dahl has to say about being humble.","text":"Love what Ryan Dahl has to say about being humble. [VIDEO] Honestly, happens almost daily, especially in SF. We meet awesome developers, but they are frankly arrogant assholes. This sort of entitlement results in those people trolling and flaming. Be humble, there are many smart people in the world; you're not that awesome."},{"id":6247913,"author":"dsego","month":"2013-08","story":"An Absolute Beginner's Guide to Node.js","stance":"reference","why":"Why Node was created for event-loop servers","quote":"History of Node.js","text":"Node was created to make scripting fast servers easy. Nothing more, nothing less. Because JS (as implemented in the browser and known by many programmers) didn't carry the baggage of blocking IO calls, Ryan thought it would be a good language for an event-loop based server. History of Node.js: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["ycPr5-27vSI",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["EWX5B6cD4_4",1]]},"VkIJbpdTujE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible introduction to Gaussian splats, made by people who understand the subject.","q":{"x":"Here's a much better explainer from some people who seem to actually understand the subject matter and not just the buzzwords","i":40230704,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46677587,"author":"mxfh","month":"2026-01","story":"Gaussian Splatting – A$AP Rocky Helicopter Music Video","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested starting point to catch up on Gaussian splatting.","quote":"but start here: TUM AI Lecture Series","text":"You're on HN, not reddit. OP linked to a special interest blog, not an explainer piece. The burden to catch up here is on the reader. The search input is right at the bottom: [LINK] but start here: TUM AI Lecture Series - The 3D Gaussian Splatting Adventure: Past, Present, Futur (George Drettakis) [LINK] or here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42069541,"author":"yokto","month":"2024-11","story":"Show HN: SuperSplat – open-source 3D Gaussian Splat Editor","stance":"rec","why":"Good introductory video on Gaussian splats.","quote":"Computerphile have a good introductory video on Gaussian Splats","text":"Not a reading guide, but Computerphile have a good introductory video on Gaussian Splats: [VIDEO] Individualkex also has a couple videos on the high level ideas: [LINK]"},{"id":40230704,"author":"ghostly_s","month":"2024-05","story":"Show HN: Maps and Splats – Mashup of 3D tile maps with Gaussian Splats","stance":"rec","why":"Explainer from people who understand the subject.","quote":"Here's a much better explainer from some people who seem to actually understand the subject matter and not just the buzzwords","text":"Here's a much better explainer from some people who seem to actually understand the subject matter and not just the buzzwords (and yes, the end result is basically a highly-detailed point cloud): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["BdHK_r9RXTc",1],["LKnqECcg6Gw",1],["3GJUM6pCpew",1]]},"j02b8Fuz73A":{"wy":"HN uses it as the classic example of rapid visual app building and of a polished early Jobs presentation, often with timestamps pointing to the database demo.","q":{"x":"There's a video of steve jobs building a fairly complex DB app without a single line of code, including joins.","i":2543050,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":4969667,"author":"ben1040","month":"2012-12","story":"The legacy of NeXT lives on in OS X","stance":"rec","why":"Shows NeXTSTEP 3 UI that resembles OS X.","quote":"this 20 year old video with Steve Jobs giving a demo of NeXTSTEP 3.","text":"Relevant - this 20 year old video with Steve Jobs giving a demo of NeXTSTEP 3. I first saw this after having bought a Mac running OS X 10.2 and was surprised by how the user-facing stuff was still very similar to its roots (maybe not nearly as much now). [VIDEO]"},{"id":2312730,"author":"_frog","month":"2011-03","story":"Steve Jobs on Apple's brand strategy in 1997","stance":"rec","why":"Young Steve Jobs perfecting his presentation style.","quote":"It's interesting to see a young Steve Jobs perfecting his presentation style.","text":"Not as relevant but I saw this video a while back: [VIDEO] It's interesting to see a young Steve Jobs perfecting his presentation style."},{"id":10097,"author":"henning","month":"2007-04","story":"The greatest Demos/screencasts you've seen","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive Steve Jobs NeXT demo.","quote":"I thought Steve Jobs's NeXT demo from 1992-1993 was pretty impressive.","text":"I thought Steve Jobs's NeXT demo from 1992-1993 was pretty impressive. [VIDEO] It certainly would have seemed space-age in 1992."},{"id":11841628,"author":"kitsunesoba","month":"2016-06","story":"NeXTstep Manual, Systems Programming with Objective-C and Driver Kit (1995)","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier NeXTSTEP demo showing how little AppKit changed.","quote":"Relevant video from 4 years earlier","text":"Relevant video from 4 years earlier: [VIDEO] As someone who has developed for Apple platforms for the better part of a decade now, it's pretty amazing just how little Cocoa/AppKit have changed over the years. Only in the past couple of OS releases are we starting to see some larger shifts. The crazy part is that this hasn't really hurt the APIs at all; sure, some bits are starting to look a little dusty and could use some attention, but even so Cocoa (both mobile and desktop variants) remain the best UI toolkit for consumer-facing application development. Qt, GTK, etc get the job done but aren't nearly as pleasant to use."},{"id":3366378,"author":"Jach","month":"2011-12","story":"I’m too lazy to be a HTML developer","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of building a database GUI app without SQL.","quote":"Around 23:00 Jobs starts to demo making a GUI application that talks to a backend DB and shows pictures","text":"> How many times do you have to re-learn file IO? What are you defining as file IO? What do you mean by re-learn--do you mean forgetting and having to learn again, or learning some new fact that changes your total understanding of the thing? What has gotten me in the past is learning how some particular language does file IO[1], which might mean learning something new about file IO I didn't know before (but didn't matter for what I wanted to do at the time) that expanded my perception. Though I guess the concepts of \"read\", \"write\", \"rewrite\", and \"append\" are the same. I really don't think you get any more universality in desktops than you do in the web world. You might get more in the web world if you restrict your universe to the web world, due to a limited selection of choices on the front-end. (And backend too if you go the traditional route of learning LAMP with PHP on a shared host provider.) Related to the other comment on RAD systems, even NeXTSTEP in '89-'95 had the WYSIWYG concept applied to everything as one of its major selling points. [VIDEO] Around 23:00 Jobs starts to demo making a GUI application that talks to a backend DB and shows pictures, and you don't need to know SQL or joins or anything. So I don't think the desktop world has been any better in regard to keeping abstractions at bay or offering universal modes of thought (hey fork()!). The first time I programmed something with tkinter I kept asking \"Where's my user-defined main loop? Where's my event h"},{"id":2942150,"author":"lr","month":"2011-08","story":"Play on Heroku","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs introduces EOF around the 25 minute mark.","quote":"Steve introduces Enterprise Objects Framework (EOF) at about 25 minutes in","text":"Steve introduces Enterprise Objects Framework (EOF) at about 25 minutes in: [VIDEO] EOF was one of the key components of making NeXT such a powerful OS, and why it was so heavily used on Wall Street. When they created WebObjects, they, of course, put EOF behind it, and yes, it is by far the most impressive ORM I have ever used. Thankfully, a well known WO developer, Andrus Adamchik, has developed Cayanne using the same principals behind EOF: [LINK]"},{"id":2543050,"author":"spitfire","month":"2011-05","story":"Ask HN: Is there software for building software?","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs builds a DB app with no code.","quote":"There's a video of steve jobs building a fairly complex DB app without a single line of code, including joins.","text":"Apple/NeXT used to have enterprise object framework. There's a video of steve jobs building a fairly complex DB app without a single line of code, including joins. We still have nothing like it. [VIDEO] See about 25 minutes in."},{"id":868139,"author":"ynniv","month":"2009-10","story":"Introducing Atlas: A Visual Development Tool for Creating Web Apps","stance":"reference","why":"Precedent for visual web app builders.","quote":"Seek to 23:10 -","text":"Well executed? Absolutely. Innovative? Well... not exactly. Seek to 23:10 - [VIDEO] (Man, look at that hair!) I don't mean to downplay 280north's work - I'm very impressed with what they're doing. It just bothers me when people see a derivative work and think it to be original. (Ahem, Chris Hughes)"},{"id":4305152,"author":"Create","month":"2012-07","story":"Sir Tim Berners-Lee Stars in Olympic Opening Ceremony ","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Having used the interface builder to dump the stuff coming from the network?","text":"Having single-handedly invented WWW is a nonsense, put a failed physicist aside reinventing himself. Not to mention [S]GML, the stuff wired though using RPC. Claiming credit for other's hard work and even visions without attribution is even worse, especially in science (\"Institute for Web Science\"? wtf). There have been lots of prior art graphical networked markup hypertext systems, ie. Xerox, Walker's Symbolics Document Examiner etc. Having used the interface builder to dump the stuff coming from the network? [VIDEO] I am bitter because of the manipulative sociopaths abusing their position and power."}],"r":[["nU8DcBF-qo4",1],["kZRE7HIO3vk",1]]},"o6L6XeNdd_k":{"wy":"Recommended as a more approachable introduction to category theory than the more formal lecture series people often start with.","q":{"x":"Milewski's YouTube lectures gets mentioned a lot, but I couldn't make much sense of them before watching Tom LaGatta's more informal and conversational presentation","i":14210943,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14210943,"author":"aputsiak","month":"2017-04","story":"Category Theory, Syntactically (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Informal, conversational category theory presentation that is easier to follow.","quote":"Milewski's YouTube lectures gets mentioned a lot, but I couldn't make much sense of them before watching Tom LaGatta's more informal and conversational presentation","text":"nLab has been one of the best sources I've come across. My aim is to express and work with philosophical logic in a more formal way, and nLab is a great source for that as well. Milewski's YouTube lectures gets mentioned a lot, but I couldn't make much sense of them before watching Tom LaGatta's more informal and conversational presentation [VIDEO] Good luck and keep at it."},{"id":7964099,"author":"gosub","month":"2014-06","story":"Mathematics is full of wonderful but relatively unknown theorems","stance":"rec","why":"Nice introduction to category theory, pointing to nLab.","quote":"is also a nice introduction","text":"Category theory by Tom LaGatta[1] is also a nice introduction. He suggests taking a look at the nLab website[2], a wiki of Maths and Physics from the categorical point of view. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":7688609,"author":"mjhoy","month":"2014-05","story":"Thinking in Types","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible talk introducing category theory.","quote":"you might try this video","text":"I couldn't really tell you anything about category theory. But I am beginning to grasp monads. (At least in the context of Haskell programs.) I think the important thing is to see how they work, see what they do. btw, you might try this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8736866,"author":"antimora","month":"2014-12","story":"A concise introduction to category theory","stance":"reference","why":"Lists nLab and books as category theory resources.","quote":"","text":"Tom LaGatta in this video on Category Theory listed this website as number one resource on category theory. [VIDEO] There he also lists some books on category theory. I am putting a direct link when he mentions the references."}]},"rgGmKD87U3M":{"wy":"HN links it in spreadsheet-reinvention threads as the clearest look at what Improv did differently, with a distinctive NeXTstep aesthetic.","q":{"x":"for anyone who hasn't seen Lotus Improv before and is both curious about what it did differently","i":38116275,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48836238,"author":"adamrezich","month":"2026-07","story":"EVE Online's Carbon engine is now open source: Fenris Creations explains why","stance":"rec","why":"Opening minutes worth watching for the vibes alone","quote":"This video is worth watching the opening few minutes of just for the vibes alone","text":"One thing I didn't like about it (and briefly experimented with, in a prototype for my own game in a similar vein), was, much like EVE, it's called a “spreadsheet game”, but there's no spreadsheets in the game , or any sort of API bridge that makes doing stuff with the game in an external spreadsheet environment easier. I understand that this may just be part of the appeal of these sorts of games for many people—reading through stat blocks and wiki pages to figure out what numbers and what formulas need to go where to build the Ultimate Spreadsheet for your purposes (though in the end doesn't everyone just use existing Ultimate Spreadsheets created by others, or at least use them as reference?). And trust me, I did have a lot of fun building my Google Sheets infrastructure to coordinate business and production between myself and the three friends I got to (briefly) play Prosperous Universe with me. Also, don't get me wrong, I also enjoyed the subsequent rabbit-hole of learning how spreadsheet implementations actually work, including historical alternative spreadsheet implementations that had a lot of cool ideas like Lotus Improv [0]. But, in the end, I just wasn't having enough fun with Prosperous Universe to keep playing after awhile. [0] This video is worth watching the opening few minutes of just for the vibes alone (trust me) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38116275,"author":"adamrezich","month":"2023-11","story":"A modern, open-source spreadsheet that goes beyond the grid","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how Improv differed from standard spreadsheets","quote":"for anyone who hasn't seen Lotus Improv before and is both curious about what it did differently","text":"for anyone who hasn't seen Lotus Improv before and is both curious about what it did differently from \"standard\" 1-2-3/Excel spreadsheet programs and wistfully nostalgic for late 80s/early 90s corporate vibes, check out [VIDEO]"},{"id":37748906,"author":"adamrezich","month":"2023-10","story":"PicoCalc: A Fully-Functional Clone of VisiCalc","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Improv's ideas with fantastic 80s-90s vibes","quote":"Lotus Improv seems like it was awesome","text":"I've never used it, Lotus Improv seems like it was awesome [VIDEO] (plus the 80s–90s vibes of this video are simply incredible) I think there's still a ton of room for spreadsheet tech to evolve, but nobody seems to really be trying anymore, because Excel is the Gold Standard that everyone seems comfortable with. for example: why do neither of the mainstream spreadsheet apps (Excel, Google Sheets) have first-class support for row and column headers? seems like an absolute no-brainer to me, and everyone who has worked in spreadsheets for more than thirty minutes."},{"id":41177340,"author":"nxobject","month":"2024-08","story":"Hexsheets: Hexagonal Spreadsheets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the original NeXTstep version has timeless aesthetics.","text":"Aw, he demos the Windows version - the original NeXTstep version has timeless aesthetics. [VIDEO] Another innovative but forgotten piece of “better than spreadsheet” software is Javelin PLUS. [LINK]"},{"id":31390227,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2022-05","story":"Introduction to Microsoft Excel (1992) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"1990 Lotus sales demo of Improv versus traditional spreadsheets","quote":"Here's a 1990 sales video (low-resolution) from Lotus explaining how it works, and how it differs from traditional spreadsheets","text":"The sad thing is how little it improved Lotus tried to re-think spreadsheets with a product called Improv - it was full of good ideas but the product never took off. The product launched on NeXTSTEP in 1991 and then on Windows in 1993. Here's a 1990 sales video (low-resolution) from Lotus explaining how it works, and how it differs from traditional spreadsheets: [VIDEO]"}]},"1b-bijO3uEw":{"wy":"Commenters call it one of the game's best dialogues and bring it up in discussions of mass surveillance.","q":{"x":"My favorite conversation in the game is probably the one with the AI Morpheus","i":22992116,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22992116,"author":"Trasmatta","month":"2020-04","story":"Restoring the Gold Filter in Deus Ex: Human Revolution – Director’s Cut","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clever Deus Ex dialogue with the AI Morpheus.","quote":"My favorite conversation in the game is probably the one with the AI Morpheus","text":"> to be honest it still just came across as conspiracy gibberish to my ear Warren Spector said that his initial idea for the game was \"what if every conspiracy theory was true?\", so that's pretty intentional. So as long as you buy into that conceit, there's a lot of amazingly clever writing in the game. My favorite conversation in the game is probably the one with the AI Morpheus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27906301,"author":"onli","month":"2021-07","story":"A key product of ubiquitous surveillance is people who are comfortable with it","stance":"rec","why":"Morpheus conversation on surveillance as a replacement for God.","quote":"but it's stronger when experienced as part of the game","text":"There is that concept that people are comfortable with surveillance, even want it. A maybe somewhat fringe pop cultural reference to that is in Deus Ex (2000), the best video game made so far: > JC Denton: \"Some people just don't understand the dangers of indiscriminate surveillance.\" > Morpheus: \"The need to be observed and understood was once satisfied by God. Now we can implement the same functionality with data-mining algorithms.\" > JC Denton: \"Electronic surveillance hardly inspires reverence. Perhaps fear and obedience, but not reverence.\" > Morpheus: \"God and the gods were apparitions of observation, judgment and punishment. Other sentiments towards them were secondary.\" > JC Denton: \"No one will ever worship a software entity peering at them through a camera.\" > Morpheus: \"The human organism always worships. First, it was the gods, then it was fame (the observation and judgment of others), next it will be self-aware systems you have built to realize truly omnipresent observation and judgment.\" One can watch it at [VIDEO] , but it's stronger when experienced as part of the game."},{"id":12956144,"author":"papaf","month":"2016-11","story":"Annotated version of an original Deus Ex design doc surfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Optional Deus Ex cutscene on mass surveillance replacing religion.","quote":"One highlight that I remember was the explanation of mass surveillance being a replacement for less religion in a society.","text":"We used to play Deus Ex 1 in a pair. The game was so different for each player and their playing style that it never got boring. We completed the whole game in this way. One highlight that I remember was the explanation of mass surveillance being a replacement for less religion in a society. In the past people censored themselves because God was always watching. It was hoped that people would continue to censor themselves because Echelon was always recording and datamining. [VIDEO] This was an optional cut scene and not part of the main story -- my friend found this, I never got this cut scene when I played. I agree that Deus Ex 1 is so special that anything after does not compare. This does not belittle the sequels, its just that the original was so good."}],"r":[["HI09kat_GeI",1],["DYvhC_RdIwQ",1],["xOCurBYI_gY",1],["n691pLhQBkw",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1]]},"D7Kn5p7TP_Y":{"wy":"Recommended as a solid introduction to personality and IQ research, and cited as the source for factor-analysis claims about personality and intelligence.","q":{"x":"For anybody interested in personality research, I warmly recommend this lecture series, by the way.","i":26291922,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":14174068,"author":"sho","month":"2017-04","story":"IQ and the Job Market (Jordan Peterson) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full Peterson lecture on intelligence and personality.","quote":"If you like the clip, watch the whole thing. It's great.","text":"This is just a clip from the full lecture here: [VIDEO] If you like the clip, watch the whole thing. It's great."},{"id":26291922,"author":"mudita","month":"2021-02","story":"In Defense of Myers Briggs (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture series covering personality psychology basics.","quote":"For anybody interested in personality research, I warmly recommend this lecture series, by the way.","text":"Let's hear it from a psychologist: \"If you don’t buy IQ research, you might as well throw out all the rest of psychology.\" (Jordan Peterson) [VIDEO] For anybody interested in personality research, I warmly recommend this lecture series, by the way. These are the basics, which psychology students learn at university."},{"id":45078601,"author":"bofadeez","month":"2025-08","story":"University of Cambridge Cognitive Ability Test","stance":"reference","why":"Source lecture for IQ and personality factor analysis claims.","quote":"This is a talking point from an old university class lecture interpreting factor analytic data on personality and IQ [1].","text":"This is a talking point from an old university class lecture interpreting factor analytic data on personality and IQ [1]. In practice, intelligence tests don’t depend on the specific questions asked. If you let a group of people generate their own items, pool them together, sample randomly, and then rank scores, the same individuals would tend to rise to the top. The high IQ people would cluster toward the top with a correlation of 0.9. This is because people with higher general cognitive ability perform better across virtually any cognitive task, a phenomenon first documented by Spearman (1904) and repeatedly confirmed in psychometrics research (e.g., Jensen 1998; Deary 2012). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45078773,"author":"bofadeez","month":"2025-08","story":"University of Cambridge Cognitive Ability Test","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for the importance of IQ research.","quote":"","text":"If you dismiss IQ research, you might as well disregard all of psychology. The psychologists who developed intelligence tests were pioneers in using the statistical methods (e.g. factor analysis) that now underpin the entire field. IQ is defined more precisely than almost any other psychological construct. Discarding it leaves you with poorly defined concepts and no clear way to handle them. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JvBT4XBdoUE",1]]},"YNTARSM-Fjc":{"wy":"HN commenters point the uninitiated to it as a beloved parody series, often in threads that mention yacht rock.","q":{"x":"Also don't miss this wonderful parody series","i":28343141,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28343141,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2021-08","story":"Using Postgres and pgRouting to Explore the Smooth Waves of Yacht Rock (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"Also don't miss this wonderful parody series","text":"I won't spoil this in case it is a surprise, but if you are familiar with this genre of music and have a guess as to who is at the center of the graph, the most connected person in all of Yacht Rock, your guess is almost certain to be correct. Also don't miss this wonderful parody series: [VIDEO] If you previously saw the old low-definition videos, these are remastered HD versions, well worth a re-watch. Stay smooth, everyone."},{"id":14239560,"author":"jrowley","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (May 2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Original Yacht Rock video series, for the uninitiated.","quote":"For the uninitiated, check out this smooth music","text":"The line about yacht rock has me seriously considering applying. Also your stack and philosophy seem pretty cool. For the uninitiated, check out this smooth music: (spotify playlist): [LINK] (original video series): [VIDEO]"},{"id":44702943,"author":"sitkack","month":"2025-07","story":"Claude Code is a slot machine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"agentic coding is a fresh breeze.","text":"I got into this industry as a side gig from science, I love solving problems. I am pretty ok at code, but code is a problem in the way of another problem. Sometimes it is easy to get distracted by a cute puzzle or a new game, but my purpose is to solve larger problems not play games (I didn't intend for this to be HN rhetorical combat). The people most against AI assistance are those that define themselves by what they do, have invested a lot into honing their craft and enjoy the execution of that craft. I have been getting paid to program for over 35 years, agentic coding is a fresh breeze. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1],["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["rRi8LptvFZY",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1],["7TqqdOcX4dc",1],["ZLWMQLMiTPk",1]]},"lX-K63pVPTM":{"wy":"Pointed to as an antidote to pessimism about the future, tied to Deutsch's ideas in The Beginning of Infinity.","q":{"x":"I encourage everyone pessimistic about our future to learn about David Deutsch's infinite optimism","i":21206657,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21206657,"author":"ridewinter","month":"2019-10","story":"China attacks Apple for allowing Hong Kong crowdsourced police activity app","stance":"strong_rec","why":"David Deutsch's case for infinite optimism.","quote":"I encourage everyone pessimistic about our future to learn about David Deutsch's infinite optimism","text":"I encourage everyone pessimistic about our future to learn about David Deutsch's infinite optimism: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32635123,"author":"nubero","month":"2022-08","story":"“Science must respect the dignity and rights of all humans”","stance":"rec","why":"Deutsch on optimism, companion to The Beginning of Infinity.","quote":"You can also go here and watch this","text":"You need to read “The Beginning of Infinity” by David Deutsch. It will help clear up all the muddled thinking I see going on when I read those lines of yours. Edit: You can also go here and watch this: [VIDEO] I still recommend the book though."},{"id":18279307,"author":"trukterious","month":"2018-10","story":"Space Transportation System","stance":"reference","why":"Deutsch on pessimism about progress.","quote":"A spirit of pessimism seems to be hanging over us these days","text":"Say we were to crash potentially earth-destroying asteroids into the moon and then offer free mining rights to companies on a first-to-get-there basis. I think we would still hear a a cacophony of moral objections. A spirit of pessimism seems to be hanging over us these days: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17056773,"author":"truculation","month":"2018-05","story":"Why the Luddites Matter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Of course, we know that today the Luddites are not viewed particularly kindly On the contrary, it is technology and progress that are viewed with scepticism -- watch almost any recent sci-fi movie. [VIDEO]"}]},"45X4VP8CGtk":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an entertaining example of a teenager buying, hosting and booting a real mainframe.","q":{"x":"it's an entertaining 45 minutes","i":23182294,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25397172,"author":"galoisgirl","month":"2020-12","story":"GnuCOBOL 3.1.1","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about getting a mainframe running at home","quote":"his talk about it is really interesting","text":"He did, his talk about it is really interesting: [VIDEO] > He and his old computer friends played around with some applications that were on the platform, and even made an FTP server that put some data on the internet. Per [LINK]"},{"id":23182294,"author":"laxd","month":"2020-05","story":"Mini-Mainframe at Home: A 6-CPU Server from 1997","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining 45-minute story of buying a mainframe","quote":"it's an entertaining 45 minutes","text":"I'm shure alot of you have seen this already. If not, it's an entertaining 45 minutes [VIDEO] (Here's What Happens When an 18 Year Old Buys a Mainframe)"},{"id":43785269,"author":"timewizard","month":"2025-04","story":"A Tour Inside the IBM Z17","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a kid buying and booting a mainframe","quote":"Here's a kid who bought a mainframe and then brought it up","text":"It depends on how full those drawers are. $250k to $1m would be the typical price range. It's easier and harder at the same time to buy older hardware. That's half the challenge though because the software is strictly licensed and you pay per MIPS. Here's a kid who bought a mainframe and then brought it up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31292408,"author":"jamal-kumar","month":"2022-05","story":"OpenVMS on x86","stance":"reference","why":"Kid who revived an old mainframe in his basement","quote":"","text":"Wasn't there that kid that IBM uses as a sort of new mascot for mainframe stuff who bought an old mainframe and got it working in his basement? [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29361122,"author":"chiph","month":"2021-11","story":"Bernie Madoff’s unwitting accomplice: The AS/400 (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of getting an old mainframe running","quote":"","text":"If you do, blog about it. One of the challenges in getting an old mainframe (System 360, 3090, etc) running is getting a license for the operating system from IBM. Hopefully acquiring one for the AS/400 is easier. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19960070,"author":"dirktheman","month":"2019-05","story":"Three geeks rescue a 50-year-old IBM 360 mainframe from an abandoned building","stance":"reference","why":"Kid got an IBM z890 working in his basement","quote":"the kid that bought an IBM z890 mainframe and brought it to his basement. And got it to work!","text":"Reminds me of the kid that bought an IBM z890 mainframe and brought it to his basement. And got it to work! [VIDEO]"},{"id":19183482,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2019-02","story":"The Rise of the Tech-Savvy Parent","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an exception to dumbed-down consumer tech","quote":"","text":"Consumer tech has been refined and dumbed down to the point where everything just sort of magically works, and since it's \"magic\" you never have to dive into the nuts and bolts. By making it so easy to use, we are depriving people of their opportunities to organically learn like people in my generation had to. Exceptions exist. * Here's What Happens When an 18 Year Old Buys a Mainframe - YouTube || [VIDEO]"},{"id":17980980,"author":"avip","month":"2018-09","story":"Millennials and Mainframes: How to Bridge the Gap","stance":"reference","why":"Has good photos of a home mainframe","quote":"This vid has some good photos","text":"This vid has some good photos [VIDEO] (also discussed on HN previously). You don't want one if you ask me."},{"id":17657261,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2018-07","story":"Hello World on z/OS","stance":"reference","why":"Motivational example of a teenager running a mainframe","quote":"Here's What Happens When an 18 Year Old Buys a Mainframe","text":"C'mon, you just needa little motivation. ;-) Here's What Happens When an 18 Year Old Buys a Mainframe [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9QWaZp_2I1k",1]]},"62jZENi1ed8":{"wy":"Pointed to as a well-cited hour on IQ and genetics whenever intelligence and heritability come up.","q":{"x":"You can also choose to spend an hour on this talk by Stephen Hsu who does research on IQ and genetics","i":10098904,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10548633,"author":"vox_mollis","month":"2015-11","story":"What happens when a culture is driven by the need for money to make more money","stance":"rec","why":"Steve Hsu's Google talk on the topic, with many citations.","quote":"Steve Hsu giving a talk @ Google on the topic, with myriad citations","text":"Steve Hsu giving a talk @ Google on the topic, with myriad citations: [VIDEO] Four studies cited on this handy chart: [LINK] It's very well-established science that you could have easily googled, but downvoting unpopular research, pointing and shrieking \"eugenics!\" is a better argumentation technique, I suppose."},{"id":10098904,"author":"peterfirefly","month":"2015-08","story":"The Bright Students Left Behind","stance":"rec","why":"Stephen Hsu's talk on IQ and genetics research.","quote":"You can also choose to spend an hour on this talk by Stephen Hsu who does research on IQ and genetics","text":"It's only bold because there is a large number of people (often state founded, often controlling at least the lower levels of education, often with major influence on media) who strongly feel otherwise -- with no numbers to back them up, of course. --- If you actually do want to get to the bottom of it, I can heartily recommend Ian Deary's \"Intelligence: A Short Introduction\" as a place to start. [LINK] (Who knows, if you dig around a bit you might even be able to find a PDF, perhaps at Emil Kirkegaard's website.) You can also choose to spend an hour on this talk by Stephen Hsu who does research on IQ and genetics: [VIDEO] You can read some of Plomin's stuff -- he is very much into genetics: [LINK] There's a paper I can't find at the moment that looks at almost 4000 non-related people, give them an IQ test each + scan their DNA for typical SNPs with a DNA chip. That gives us almost 8,000,000 pairs where we know how similar their IQ score and DNA was. That gives us almost 8,000,000 points we can plot into a 2D coordinate system (and we can of course also throw statistics at them). It turns out that there is a clear correlation between measured IQ and measured SNPs (i.e., the point cloud is denser along a line). In other words, there is a statistical relation between DNA and IQ. This is a result that is extremely hard to explain away if one believes in nurture, SES, microaggressions, racism, ESL difficulties, etc. Since it is such a rhetorically important paper, I hope somebody"},{"id":32373761,"author":"tresil","month":"2022-08","story":"Does Education Matter? Tests from Extensions of Schooling in 1919-22, 47 and 72","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"at least there are some people attempting to give this subject more consideration","text":"I am growing more disturbed in recent years by how true this statement is in our current society. While not all behavior is hereditary, genetics plays a massive role. I wish it were not taboo to talk about this more openly. While I have found few, at least there are some people attempting to give this subject more consideration: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29213963,"author":"bordercases","month":"2021-11","story":"The Breeder's Equation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Remapping intelligence to certain cognitive performance parameters, many of which can be measured in a very metrical and objective way, still tends to produce correlations. See Figure 3 of this article: [0] - there is a mix of simple perceptual tasks and more complex reasoning ones, which on the surface appear independent. The paper admits at the end that intelligence isn't one kind of task, but instead many kinds of systems used together. That still doesn't prevent their ability to construct a version of g-factor. And very simple metrics, taken alone, seem to inter-correlate well with IQ scores, like reaction time.[1] This correlation increases with age, suggesting a biological factor in preserving brain integrity that would nullify educational effects of intelligence. If your version of intelligence has nothing to do with cognitive activity I don't know what to tell you. It's true that effectiveness in society might not be correlated with intelligence. That says more about what society rewards and the kind of labor required to get it. And I don't mean to say that society should reward people based off of test scores, that's dumb. Nor should we assortate people based off of pure test scores, also dumb. But I see no real conceptual problems regarding intelligence that doesn't try to conflate intelligence entirely with general effectiveness. There are IQ realists like Steven Hsu that don't do this, placing it on equal importance with conscientiousness [4]. As with height, ther"},{"id":17700060,"author":"sadris","month":"2018-08","story":"The world is losing the war against climate change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Without the intelligence, even an infinite amount of education would be pointless","text":"> Education is what needs to be innovated. We need more kids becoming scientists. Some people cannot just \"become scientists\". Without the intelligence, even an infinite amount of education would be pointless: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14400160,"author":"ekns","month":"2017-05","story":"Scientists Tie 52 Genes to Human Intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Hsu on finding additive genes for intelligence at BGI.","quote":"I recall seeing this Google Tech Talk by Steve Hsu on how they are in the process of finding out basically all the additive genes that contribute to intelligence","text":"I recall seeing this Google Tech Talk by Steve Hsu on how they are in the process of finding out basically all the additive genes that contribute to intelligence in China at the BGI research institute: [VIDEO] They would take roughly a thousand people who are >=3 standard deviations smarter than average and compare their full genomes against people who are of average intelligence. This way they expect to find out all the \"easy\" genetic determinants of intelligence."}],"r":[["S27pHKBEp30",1]]},"7kQk9-KLPfU":{"wy":"HN people link it as a short, funny and accurate explanation of why over-testing and over-screening can do harm.","q":{"x":"An under 3-minute video by a funny and informative doctor explaining this","i":27060140,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27060140,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2021-05","story":"Are we screening too much for skin cancer? It's complicated","stance":"rec","why":"Short, funny, informative explanation of medical screening","quote":"An under 3-minute video by a funny and informative doctor explaining this","text":"An under 3-minute video by a funny and informative doctor explaining this (youtube handle \"medlife crisis\"): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19520197,"author":"joosters","month":"2019-03","story":"I Now Have a Cardiologist","stance":"rec","why":"Short video on why over-testing is not a good idea","quote":"There's a great (and short) video suggesting why it's not a good idea to run as many medical tests as possible","text":"There's a great (and short) video suggesting why it's not a good idea to run as many medical tests as possible, and it's not because of the cost: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39622388,"author":"user_7832","month":"2024-03","story":"FDA clears first over-the-counter continuous glucose monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Notes that a tumor is not necessarily dangerous","quote":"a tumor is not necessarily dangerous","text":"I get your point, however I think there are a few confounding things. For a lot of people, if you get a positive result from a test that a doctor brushes off that's not going to go well. I'm very much in favor of more testing personally, there are almost certainly folks who're on SSRIs who'd benefit more from Vit D/Mg supplementation for example. Another thing I seem to remember in his video was that a tumor is not necessarily dangerous. Out of a hundred (say) tumors in a person's life, only maybe 5 are risky. But I'm paraphrasing this badly. Edit: [VIDEO] is one of the videos, however I think he's talked about this more (likely on instagram or another video too)."}],"r":[["8moePxHpvok",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1]]},"8kUQWuK1L4w":{"wy":"Recommended as a vintage TV segment on APL's origins, and quoted for Iverson's view of APL as notation.","q":{"x":"a video from 1974, where Iverson, Falkoff, and others discuss the origins of APL. It was a pleasure to watch.","i":6118176,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6118176,"author":"znowi","month":"2013-07","story":"Ruins of forgotten empires: APL languages","stance":"rec","why":"1974 discussion by Iverson, Falkoff and others on APL's origins","quote":"a video from 1974, where Iverson, Falkoff, and others discuss the origins of APL. It was a pleasure to watch.","text":"There's a great link in the original comments to a video from 1974, where Iverson, Falkoff, and others discuss the origins of APL. It was a pleasure to watch. A peculiar contrast in language and attitude to today's rockstars and ninjas :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":4070533,"author":"sunkencity","month":"2012-06","story":"Why APL Is Still Cool","stance":"rec","why":"Fantastic vintage TV segment on APL's origins.","quote":"this is a fantastic tv segment from days of yore","text":"I recently bought \"APL an interactive approach\" and read it. It seems that most of the good stuff from APL is already present in modern languages, and better when it's lazy. The OO stuff in APL looks kind of crufty. Still it's a wonderful fascinating language. In the age of mature open-source it feels very old fashioned with the expensiveness of a decent implementation. Btw, this is a fantastic tv segment from days of yore \"The Origins of APL - 1974\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":46634426,"author":"thesz","month":"2026-01","story":"Scaling long-running autonomous coding","stance":"reference","why":"APL's discoverer found notation first expands then shrinks as problems are expressed","quote":"Skilled devs compress, not generate (expand).","text":"Skilled devs compress, not generate (expand). [VIDEO] The \"discoverer\" of APL tried to express as many problems as he could with his notation. First he found that notation expands and after some more expansion he found that it began shrinking. The same goes to Forth, which provides means for a Sequitur-compressed [1] representation of a program. [1] [LINK] Myself, I always strive to delete some code or replace some code with shorter version. First, to better understand it, second, to return back and read less."},{"id":46825008,"author":"thesz","month":"2026-01","story":"Way AI assistance impacts the formation of coding skills","stance":"reference","why":"APL inventor describes building notation to express many problems","quote":"APL inventor says that he was developing not a programming language, but notation to express as much problems as one can.","text":"[VIDEO] APL inventor says that he was developing not a programming language, but notation to express as much problems as one can. He found that expressing more and more problems with the notation first made notation grow, then notation size started to shrink. To develop conceptual knowledge (when one's \"notation\" starts to shrink) one has to have some good memory (re-expressing more and more problems)."},{"id":6692522,"author":"shitgoose","month":"2013-11","story":"\"Careless\" employees","stance":"reference","why":"Example of old-school engineering by capable developers","quote":"Good old engineering, like these guys","text":"There are lots of software developers out there, that shouldn't be developing software in first place. They could be excellent farmers, musicians, athletes, but for whatever reason they decided to be software developers. And it doesn't matter how many scrums or code reviews you throw at them, they just won't get it. They will be producing miserable results making everyone around them miserable. On the other hand, there still are a few decent, old school devs, who don't need hand-holding, constant poking and distraction of standup meetings and writing meaningless test cases that check if 2+2 is still 4. They just (1) understand the problem and (2) write code that solves it. As simple as that. Good old engineering, like these guys: [VIDEO] . Or original SAS system - its reference manual was better quality then any statistics textbook. Unfortunately those days are gone now and we live in the kingdom of Scrums and Frameworks."}],"r":[["P0ukYf_xvgc",1]]},"CxHa5KaMBcM":{"wy":"Offered as a calming, dry, familiar broadcast, fitting for a crowd that enjoys ambient and lofi radio.","q":{"x":"Obligatory BBC Shipping Forecast, because it seems right up this crowd's alley","i":30059860,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35585600,"author":"ilikepi","month":"2023-04","story":"Lofi air traffic control – LAX","stance":"rec","why":"Dry shipping forecast using the Beaufort scale","quote":"Maybe try the BBC shipping forecast?","text":"Maybe try the BBC shipping forecast? [VIDEO] I find the use of the Beaufort scale ( [LINK] ) gives it a particularly dry flavor..."},{"id":30059860,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2022-01","story":"Metal Detectorist Discovers One of England’s Earliest Gold Coins in a Farm Field","stance":"rec","why":"Calming shipping forecast broadcast","quote":"Obligatory BBC Shipping Forecast, because it seems right up this crowd's alley","text":"> equivalent of having a nice cuppa on a cool, late summer's morning, while looking out the window at the long, brown grass swaying gently in the breeze Obligatory BBC Shipping Forecast, because it seems right up this crowd's alley: [VIDEO] Recent: [LINK]"},{"id":28059473,"author":"Veen","month":"2021-08","story":"The Education of Melvyn Bragg","stance":"reference","why":"Shipping forecast as a comforting part of the commenter's life","quote":"I find the late (0040) and early morning (0520) shipping forcasts immensely familiar and comforting.","text":"That's funny, although it's missing the one of the best bits about Radio 4: the Shipping Forecast. For some reason, I find the late (0040) and early morning (0520) shipping forcasts immensely familiar and comforting. It's been a part of my life since I was a small child getting up very early for school when I had no idea what Dogger, Forties, Cromarty and Rockall meant. [VIDEO]"}]},"F8JCh0owT4w":{"wy":"Offered as a concrete, human example in right-to-repair discussions of how software lock-in affects expensive equipment.","q":{"x":"It's worth a look.","i":16549773,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18251953,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2018-10","story":"Repair is as important as innovation","stance":"rec","why":"Farmers breaking tractor repair restrictions.","quote":"I thought this was quite interesting","text":"I thought this was quite interesting: Tractor Hacking: The Farmers Breaking Big Tech's Repair Monopoly - [VIDEO]"},{"id":16546594,"author":"samcampbell","month":"2018-03","story":"California to Introduce 'Right to Repair' Bill","stance":"rec","why":"Shows farmers' repair struggles that drove home right to repair.","quote":"Here's a relevant video in regards to farmers and their equipment","text":"Here's a relevant video in regards to farmers and their equipment that drove the impact of the ‘Right to Repair’ bill home for me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16549773,"author":"OrwellianChild","month":"2018-03","story":"California to Introduce 'Right to Repair' Bill","stance":"rec","why":"Looks at John Deere lock-in for six-figure equipment.","quote":"It's worth a look.","text":"I appreciate the dialogue. We may have to agree to disagree on the final point. I'm not convinced that there is always a competitive market filled with alternatives that meet customer preferences. For example: I have to choose between iMessage and a headphone jack for my cell phone. There are other phones , but I don't have a totally ala carte menu - there are always trade offs. This likely won't change your opinion either, but it's a good piece that adds color to this conversation about right-to-repair: Motherboard published a video about John Deere's tractor/maintenance lock-in that looks at the issue from the perspective of 6-figure industrial equipment. [1] It's worth a look. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16312898,"author":"ramy_d","month":"2018-02","story":"A Company That Tears Cars Apart to Find Out How They're Built","stance":"reference","why":"Farmers fighting John Deere's software repair lock-in.","quote":"","text":"what about software? I'm reminded of what farmers have to go through with John Deere tractors [ [VIDEO] ]. Isn't that going to start happening with cars (I thought this was already happening). Are they going to reverse engineer that too?"}],"r":[["nIKhUizkXxA",1],["JXpoALotxf0",1],["AcQifPHcMLE",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1]]},"FH2WeWgcSMk":{"wy":"HN commenters cite the series as a documentary on the Evergreen controversy and as evidence in arguments about ideology and campus culture.","q":{"x":"the great, 3-part, documentary[3] by Mike Nayna","i":19779815,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":42693180,"author":"docmars","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Evergreen State College faculty treatment in 2017","quote":"This should make anyone's skin crawl with the way this college's faculty and staff were treated","text":"On YouTube, watch the Evergreen State College 3-part documentary by Bret Weinstein. This is much more common than you think, through your anecdote, unfortunately. Granted, this happened in 2017, so a few years after your time in college, but I would argue \"peak wokeness\" sits between 2016 to today, in large part due to Trump's first election win. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] This should make anyone's skin crawl with the way this college's faculty and staff were treated, and the childish behavior of the students to allow this to happen. This gives a reason why \"college kids\" are no longer considered adults."},{"id":19779815,"author":"raarts","month":"2019-04","story":"Diversity and the Death of Free Speech","stance":"rec","why":"Three-part documentary on Evergreen State College controversy","quote":"the great, 3-part, documentary[3] by Mike Nayna","text":"I've been following the free speech issue on universities for almost a year now, and I think the 2008 may be on the mark, though the first well known incident I can name is the Lawrence Summers controversy in 2005 [1]. The free speech issue is mainly a talking point of conservatives because these are the ones most impacted. Students complain about being afraid to voice their opinions for fear of being ostracized or punished on grades. It's enlightening to read through a few issues of The College Fix [2]. Relevant interesting book is The Coddling Of The American Mind by Jonathan Haidt and Greg Luka off. But it gets really entertaining when you watch some youtube videos on this subject, for example the great, 3-part, documentary[3] by Mike Nayna. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] EDIT: and just after I wrote this, a research paper popped up, about army veterans going back to college and experiencing culture shock: [LINK]"},{"id":19418110,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-03","story":"Meritocracy doesn't exist, and believing it does has bad effects","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence for religious overtones in an ideology","quote":"","text":"I disagree that it is not logical to point out the religious aspects of some of people that subscribe to intersectional ideology. I hope you can hear me out since you had such a vocal objection, and that you can point out flaws in my reasoning. You might agree that creationists when expressing something resting on their faith might have some scientific methodology issues. I think we might agree that it is not illogical to point out that some research done by creationists have a religious overtone. Research outside their creationist dogma might not warrant such scrutiny. Can you after watching the following two videos, which contains core thoughtleaders in intersectionality like DiAngelo, that intersectionality does not have a religious overtone? [1,2] If not, why not? If so, why is it then illogical to point out that the authors are expressing something that to at least some degree seem to rely on intersectional dogma and that this has a religious overtone? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":18963212,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-01","story":"Letter in Response to Jan. 17 Article in The New York Times","stance":"reference","why":"Evergreen documentary cited as evidence of ideology as religion","quote":"","text":"At some point I hope the left takes back the NYT from the ideologues. This is the same trite attack on science and bullying of scientists we’ve seen before when it doesn’t comply with their social justice beliefs [1], and this time it’s more dangerous than the creationists that tried to do something similar for their beliefs because as this article shows truth is not a priority in this ideology and they control universities as well as cultural institutions like the NYT. Another thing that is more worrying is that social justice activists are using the technicality of them not worshipping a God per se to bypass secular protections against enforcing religious beliefs institutionally. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l0GA901oGe4",1]]},"IZmaeC_Ma5A":{"wy":"HN points to it as a look under the hood of a remarkable one-developer project.","q":{"x":"here's a cool talk about it: the whole thing is just 2MB of JS written by one guy!","i":23491058,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26772062,"author":"vvoyer","month":"2021-04","story":"AMA: Ivan Kutskir, creator of Photopea","stance":"rec","why":"Ivan's explanation of developing the best free photo editor.","quote":"Check-out his explanations here: Legend.","text":"Hey Ivan, congrats! Had a great time with you 2 years ago when you came to Nantes, France to explain \"Developing the best free photo editor\". Check-out his explanations here: [VIDEO] Legend."},{"id":23491058,"author":"modzu","month":"2020-06","story":"Gimp 2.10.20","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about Photopea, 2MB of JS by one developer.","quote":"here's a cool talk about it: the whole thing is just 2MB of JS written by one guy!","text":"if anyone wants to know more, here's a cool talk about it: [VIDEO] the whole thing is just 2MB of JS written by one guy!"},{"id":21764962,"author":"IvanK_net","month":"2019-12","story":"Founder came back after 8 years to rewrite Flash photo editor in Canvas/WebGL","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's own talk about building Photopea.","quote":"I gave a talk about it","text":"Yes, Photopea is only one HTML, one CSS and many JS files. It is about 1.5 MB in total. I pay 40 USD a year for the hosting + 12 USD a year for the .com domain. I gave a talk about it: [VIDEO]"}]},"LIIBRr31DIU":{"wy":"HN people share it as rare behind-the-scenes footage of the making of a legendary demoscene production.","q":{"x":"here’s some rare footage of the creation of the legendary demo","i":26525157,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39566308,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2024-03","story":"Second Reality code review (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Making-of video the commenter had not seen before.","quote":"I had never seen it, so this audience may enjoy that, too","text":"this video showed up in a recent thread about Second Reality and I had never seen it, so this audience may enjoy that, too: [VIDEO] (\"Making of Second Reality / Future Crew\")"},{"id":26525157,"author":"logbiscuitswave","month":"2021-03","story":"Demoscene accepted as UNESCO cultural heritage in Germany","stance":"rec","why":"Rare footage of the legendary demo's creation.","quote":"here’s some rare footage of the creation of the legendary demo","text":"I had a 386/40DX that could run Second Reality, and I was blown away. When I upgraded to a 486/66DX2 the first thing I did was run Second Reality, and I was shocked at how much of the demo content I had missed before from parts being truncated. Also it’s worth noting that a lot of Second Reality’s source code got released to GitHub several years back: [LINK] and here’s an incredibly deep dive into the code: [LINK] Finally, here’s some rare footage of the creation of the legendary demo: [VIDEO] (English subtitles available)"},{"id":38321572,"author":"qingcharles","month":"2023-11","story":"Second Reality for Apple II","stance":"reference","why":"Future Crew finishing Second Reality a week before release.","quote":"Future Crew putting the finishing touches on Second Reality a week before release...","text":"Behind the Scenes: Future Crew putting the finishing touches on Second Reality a week before release... [VIDEO]"},{"id":28203787,"author":"jug","month":"2021-08","story":"Amiga Music Preservation","stance":"reference","why":"Making of Second Reality by Future Crew.","quote":"Making of Second Reality with said Future Crew","text":"Danko - Complications [LINK] Purple Motion of Future Crew - Unreal 2 [LINK] (composed by a 16 yo) Making of Second Reality with said Future Crew: [VIDEO] 9 fingers - Spaceballs: [LINK] And making of: [LINK] Kefrens - Desert Dreams: [LINK]"},{"id":21219668,"author":"tpmx","month":"2019-10","story":"The most advanced MySQL raytracer on the market","stance":"reference","why":"Future Crew's magic in the early 90s.","quote":"make sure to enable the thirdparty english subtitles","text":"I'm thinking the democoders could create scarcity. I'm imagining e.g. Future Crew making a Third reality . Or Triton making Crystal Dream III . Release at like a 300p video for the world and like AM audio mono sound quality and sell the binary to the highest bidder. Or perhaps smarter, release it at 1:1 quality for the first minute, but only for the first minute. ;) Edit: of course this would be a horrible future. Also, there's no way these now old guys could recreate the magic they experienced together in the early 90s. Example: [VIDEO] (make sure to enable the thirdparty english subtitles)"},{"id":17631752,"author":"Annatar","month":"2018-07","story":"DJ rig with two Amiga 1200 PCs","stance":"reference","why":"Future Crew making Second Reality.","quote":"here's Future Crew making it","text":"I do. They ushered a new era on the PC, where PC finally had something that could compare with the demo quality of Amiga demos. To be more concrete, it was \"Second Reality\" that they did it with: [LINK] and here's Future Crew making it: [VIDEO] Europe and Summer school holidays. Perfect time to code."},{"id":10516189,"author":"johansch","month":"2015-11","story":"Software developers describing their work in 1973 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"1993 video of Future Crew making the PC demo Second Reality.","quote":"this 1993 video of Future Crew making the PC demo \"Second Reality","text":"Because he's finnish it reminds me of this 1993 video of Future Crew making the PC demo \"Second Reality\": [VIDEO] (There's high quality english subtitles there.) Finished product: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":2857555,"author":"ck2","month":"2011-08","story":"Results from Assembly Summer 2011","stance":"reference","why":"1993 footage of the Future Crew making Second Reality.","quote":"Wow check out 1993 - we have come soooo far","text":"Wow check out 1993 - we have come soooo far: [VIDEO] Listen to those hard drives! Netscape Navigator didn't come out until over a year later (December 1994) and IE 1.0 another year after that. Wow."}],"r":[["xoxhDk-hwuo",1],["5eqRuVp65eY",1]]},"O4Ko7NW2yQo":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible but not oversimplified explanation of the muon g-2 experiment, by a channel aimed at curious physics enthusiasts.","q":{"x":"PBS Spacetime is YouTube channel hosted by astrophysics Ph.D Matt O'Dowd, aimed at casual physics enthusiasts without oversimplifying underlying physics too much.","i":26729185,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27096596,"author":"dmix","month":"2021-05","story":"Gravity is a double copy of other forces","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what Fermilab is doing with the g-2 machine","quote":"Here's an old one for example that explains what they are doing at Fermi wit the G-2 machine","text":"There's a ton of articles about this on every science news site and youtube channel. Here's an old one for example that explains what they are doing at Fermi wit the G-2 machine: [VIDEO] And just check the science sites."},{"id":26729185,"author":"terramex","month":"2021-04","story":"Particle mystery: physicists confirm the muon is more magnetic than predicted","stance":"rec","why":"PBS Space Time covers muon results for casual enthusiasts","quote":"PBS Spacetime is YouTube channel hosted by astrophysics Ph.D Matt O'Dowd, aimed at casual physics enthusiasts without oversimplifying underlying physics too much.","text":"They already did, 15 minutes ago: [VIDEO] For those who do not know - PBS Spacetime is YouTube channel hosted by astrophysics Ph.D Matt O'Dowd, aimed at casual physics enthusiasts without oversimplifying underlying physics too much."},{"id":30962145,"author":"Splendor","month":"2022-04","story":"Measurement of the W boson mass reveals 7σ deviation from calculations","stance":"reference","why":"Discuss Fermilab results hinting at crack in standard model","quote":"these two videos discuss the results of a Fermilab experiment that hints at a crack in the standard model.","text":"Maybe not exactly what you're looking for but these two videos discuss the results of a Fermilab experiment that hints at a crack in the standard model. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26783730,"author":"JProthero","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: What under-the-radar technology are you excited about?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Muon g-2 Fermilab results","quote":"","text":"Muon-Catalyzed Fusion [1]. Briefly it's a genuine, and scientifically uncontroversial, form of 'cold' fusion enabled by muons — a more massive relative of the electron that was in the news recently thanks to the potentially interesting results coming out of the Muon g-2 experiment at Fermilab [2]. Like conventional 'hot' plasma fusion, in all experiments to date the energy input needed to sustain the process has exceeded the output, but it may be possible to use it to generate power. Unlike conventional fusion though, it receives relatively little attention, and there is no well-funded international effort to tackle the associated technical challenges. As with conventional fusion the technical challenges seem formidable, but it could be an interesting technology if a way could be found to make it work. Listeners of Lex Fridman's podcast may recall that it was briefly mentioned in the episode he made last year with his father, Alexander Fridman, who is a plasma physicist [3]. As someone who has been interested in the idea for years and barely hears any mention of it, I was pleasantly surprised it came up. It was also covered on the MinutePhysics YouTube channel in 2018 [4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] Bonus fact: Muon-Catalyzed fusion was first demonstrated by the Nobel laureate Luis Alvarez, who, with his geologist son Walter Alvarez, later proposed the 'Alvarez hypothesis' for the extinction of the dinosaurs by asteroid impact."}],"r":[["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["C6nAxiym9oc",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1]]},"QWWgr2rN45o":{"wy":"HN recommends it for Hinton's research history and the two pioneers reflecting on a decade of progress.","q":{"x":"Goes over Hinton's history and why he went the direction he did with his research","i":39769158,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42120662,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2024-11","story":"A stubborn computer scientist accidentally launched the deep learning boom","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton and Li look back at a decade of developments.","quote":"looked back at the developments over the last ~10 years","text":"See \"Geoffrey Hinton in conversation with Fei-Fei Li\" where the two got together and looked back at the developments over the last ~10 years, including why and how they did their part: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":39769158,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-03","story":"The Google employees who created transformers","stance":"rec","why":"Covers Hinton's research history and Li's ImageNet work.","quote":"Goes over Hinton's history and why he went the direction he did with his research","text":"This 'conversation' with Geoffrey Hinton and Fei-Fei Li goes over a lot of the history of things (1h50m): * [VIDEO] * [LINK] ('mirror') Goes over Hinton's history and why he went the direction he did with his research, as well as Li's efforts with ImageNet."},{"id":40712635,"author":"trott","month":"2024-06","story":"Getting 50% (SoTA) on Arc-AGI with GPT-4o","stance":"reference","why":"Hinton says LLM few-shot learning compares well with people.","quote":"But Geoff Hinton says LLMs' few-shot learning compares quite favorably with people!","text":"François Chollet says LLMs do not learn in-context. But Geoff Hinton says LLMs' few-shot learning compares quite favorably with people! [VIDEO] The truth is in the middle, I think. They learn in-context, but not as well as humans. The approach in the article hides the unreliability of current LLMs by generating thousands of programs, and still the results aren't human-level. (This is impressive work though -- I'm not criticizing it.)"}],"r":[["BCmz-fgp24E",2],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["LMxate9gegg",1],["qE7dYhpI_bI",1],["HNkLcD3PKyk",1],["YcVSgYz5SJ8",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1]]},"UC_BCz0pzMw":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear walkthrough of nuclear plant economics and why incentives favor other generation.","q":{"x":"This excellent video delves into the economics of nuclear power plants and compares them with the alternatives.","i":24747709,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36034951,"author":"meowkit","month":"2023-05","story":"When will global warming hit the landmark 1.5 ºC limit?","stance":"rec","why":"Why economic incentives favor fossil plants over traditional nuclear","quote":"this video always comes to mind.","text":"this video always comes to mind. [VIDEO] The economic incentives (in a sort of basic example) are in favor of fossil fuel plants. It takes too long for traditional nuclear plants to have a return. Energy generation is sadly more complicated than I had hoped as a laymen. The sources have to compete on variable demand basis along with amortized costs over years. Nuclear needs novel designs with better economic returns, or to be given assistance by government to be more competitive on this amortized basis."},{"id":24747709,"author":"xabush","month":"2020-10","story":"We Need a Nuclear New Deal, Not a Green New Deal","stance":"rec","why":"Compares nuclear plant economics with alternatives","quote":"This excellent video delves into the economics of nuclear power plants and compares them with the alternatives.","text":"This excellent video delves into the economics of nuclear power plants and compares them with the alternatives. [VIDEO] Although nuclear energy is profitable of the LONG term, it is financially infeasible over the short-to-medium term."},{"id":38811499,"author":"billytetrud","month":"2023-12","story":"First new U.S. nuclear reactor since 2016 is now in operation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure why you think that Exelon didn't consider regulatory risk as one of the things that made it not make sense for them. It was certainly a large factor. The idea that existing nuclear power plants are losing money because they aren't operationally competitive with oil and gas does not square with the facts. The total cost of running a nuclear plant is 30% less than oil and gas per kilowatt-hour. And if all our nuclear plants weren't 50 years old and saddled with massive overregulation, the operation and maintenance of a nuclear plant would probably be equivalent to running a fossil fueled power plant, which would cut ANOTHER 30% off the cost of nuclear because fuel costs of fossil fueled power plants are 4 times the cost of nuclear fuel per kwh. [LINK] And as for construction costs, you can see that the massive increase in construction costs in the early 60s and 70s was because regulation required enormous increases in staffing, which ballooned labor costs. [LINK] And even so a nuclear plant should be expected to be profitable as long as you can manage to operate it for long enough without the government shutting you down (one of the major regulatory risks I mentioned). Nuclear power plants in the past generally took 7 years to build and pay off in 16-24 years. While a natural gas plant can be built in 2 years and pay off in 8 years. Nuclear power plants are so cost effective tho, that if you can manage to run it for 17 years, you'll make more money than running a n"},{"id":35522220,"author":"the_cramer","month":"2023-04","story":"Shutting down nuclear power could increase air pollution: study","stance":"reference","why":"Nuclear investment has very long ROI","quote":"the long-term investment a nuclear reactor is massive and ROI is often measured in centuries.","text":"From what I've seen [1] the long-term investment a nuclear reactor is massive and ROI is often measured in centuries. Also we still don't have a good solution for power spike handling with renewables + energy storage. A windless night is the scenario we're talking about here. So most of this discussion is probably addressing nuclear vs. coal / gas [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30591973,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-03","story":"Britain to start approval process for Rolls-Royce mini nuclear reactor","stance":"reference","why":"Nuclear ROI catches up over time","quote":"It does catch up, of course it does.","text":"It does catch up, of course it does. It takes some time, but there is ROI, and it’s not even that far in time. It’s just that you have to spend more time in debt. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24303036,"author":"yakshaving_jgt","month":"2020-08","story":"Bill Gates' nuclear venture plans reactor to complement solar, wind power boom","stance":"reference","why":"Nuclear economics favor longer timespans","quote":"from what I’ve read and seen, the economics of nuclear are significantly better over a longer timespan.","text":"Do you have a source to support that claim? Because from what I’ve read and seen, the economics of nuclear are significantly better over a longer timespan. [VIDEO]"}]},"UziFw-jQSks":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a decent, entertaining summary of Lilly's life and work whenever dolphin communication research comes up.","q":{"x":"This video does a decent job of summarizing his life and work","i":29647047,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43690485,"author":"meindnoch","month":"2025-04","story":"DolphinGemma: How Google AI is helping decode dolphin communication","stance":"rec","why":"Covers John Lilly's dolphin experiments and LSD-fueled beliefs.","quote":"I recommend watching the Atrocity Guide episode on John C. Lily","text":">A woman named Margaret Howe Lovatt lived full-time with the Dolphins attempting to learn a shared language between them. She also had sex with a male dolphin called Peter. >He would take LSD and place himself in the tank and believed he was psychically communicating with the Dolphins. He eventually came to believe he was communicating with a cosmic entity called ECCO (Earth Coincidence Control Office). The story of the Sega game \"Ecco the Dolphin\" [1] is a tongue-in-cheek reference to this. I recommend watching the Atrocity Guide episode on John C. Lily and his dolphin \"science\" [2]. It's on par with The Men Who Stare at Goats (the non-fiction book [3], not the movie). He has a website that looks like it's been untouched since his death, 2001: [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":29647047,"author":"bonnie76","month":"2021-12","story":"John C. Lilly ‘modified human agents’ human, dolphin neuropsychology experiments","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes Lilly's life and work, including his later drug abuse.","quote":"This video does a decent job of summarizing his life and work","text":"This video does a decent job of summarizing his life and work: [VIDEO] . It emphasizes his later decent into drug abuse and new age pedagogy. The man was clearly something of a monster. I have a macabre interest in the no holds barred era of 50’s and 60’s military and government research. I find it fascinating that such a lack of restraint resulted in both the horrors of ice pick lobotomies on traumatized veterans and the unmatched achievements of the moon landing. I also find contrast between a government that is happy to engage in LSD research in pursuit of brain washing) and one that routinely locks people in tiny cages for the use of the same substance highly revealing."},{"id":29684865,"author":"bonnie76","month":"2021-12","story":"John C. Lilly ‘modified human agents’ human/dolphin neuropsychology experiment's","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes Lilly's life and work, including his later drug abuse.","quote":"This video does a decent job of summarizing his life and work","text":"This video does a decent job of summarizing his life and work: [VIDEO] . It emphasizes his later decent into drug abuse and new age pedagogy. The man was clearly something of a monster. I have a macabre interest in the no holds barred era of 50’s and 60’s military and government research. I find it fascinating that such a lack of restraint resulted in both the horrors of ice pick lobotomies on traumatized veterans and the unmatched achievements of the moon landing. I also find contrast between a government that is happy to engage in LSD research in pursuit of brain washing) and one that routinely locks people in tiny cages for the use of the same substance highly revealing."},{"id":40335853,"author":"darepublic","month":"2024-05","story":"Scientists Find an 'Alphabet' in Whale Songs","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on researchers trying to teach dolphins English.","quote":"a Youtube doc about researchers attempting to teach dolphins english","text":"I dunno if sometimes the language would be contextual, and utterances could not be understood without taking into account the context of what is occurring, or the speaker. Yes I know human language can be subject to these variables too. Anyhow it's all speculation and the dream of talking to animals is surely exciting. Also, a Youtube doc about researchers attempting to teach dolphins english: [VIDEO]"}]},"VdIURAu1-aU":{"wy":"Cited as a watchable early introduction to deep learning from its pioneer, and remembered as a mind-blowing moment in the field.","q":{"x":"There's a great Google Tech Talk by Geoff Hinton that serves as a slightly technical introduction to Deep Learning if anyone's interested","i":7434375,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7434375,"author":"markovbling","month":"2014-03","story":"Is Google Cornering the Market on Deep Learning?","stance":"rec","why":"Slightly technical introduction to deep learning","quote":"There's a great Google Tech Talk by Geoff Hinton that serves as a slightly technical introduction to Deep Learning if anyone's interested","text":"There's a great Google Tech Talk by Geoff Hinton that serves as a slightly technical introduction to Deep Learning if anyone's interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4145916,"author":"Devilboy","month":"2012-06","story":"Building high-level features using large scale unsupervised learning","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton's videos are very watchable","quote":"Prof. Hinton's videos are very watchable","text":"Have you seen the online courses? [LINK] (From one of the authors of this paper!) [LINK] [LINK] Prof. Hinton's videos are very watchable: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4149953,"author":"tylerhobbs","month":"2012-06","story":"Microsoft Research make breakthrough in audio speech recognition","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton tech talk covering phoneme recognition with deep belief networks","quote":"There's a second Google Tech Talk video from a few years later that talks directly about phoneme recognition as well","text":"There's a very good Google Tech Talk by Geoff Hinton (who has worked closely with Dahl on a lot of this research and developed some of the key algorithms in this field) that explains how to build deep belief networks using layers of RBMs: [LINK] That video focuses on handwritten digit recognition, but it's great for understanding the basics. There's a second Google Tech Talk video from a few years later that talks directly about phoneme recognition as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46451462,"author":"crystal_revenge","month":"2026-01","story":"2025: The Year in LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"Recalls Hinton's RBM talk as mind-blowing progress","quote":"do you remember Geoffrey Hinton's RBM talk at Google in 2010?","text":"That must have been a long time back. Having lived through the time when web pages were served through CGI and mobile phones only existed in movies, when SVMs where the new hotness in ML and people would write about how weird NNs were, I feel like I've seen a lot more concrete progress in the last few decades than this year. This year honestly feels quite stagnant. LLMs are literally technology that can only reproduce the past. They're cool, but they were way cooler 4 years ago. We've taken big ideas like \"agents\" and \"reinforcement learning\" and basically stripped them of all meaning in order to claim progress. I mean, do you remember Geoffrey Hinton's RBM talk at Google in 2010? [0] That was absolutely insane for anyone keeping up with that field. By the mid-twenty teens RBMs were already outdated. I remember when everyone was implementing flavors of RNNs and LSTMs. Karpathy's character 2015 RNN project was insane [1]. This comment makes me wonder if part of the hype around LLMs is just that a lot of software people simply weren't paying attention to the absolutely mind-blowing progress we've seen in this field for the last 20 years. But even ignoring ML, the world's of web development and mobile application development have gone through incredible progress over the last decade and a half. I remember a time when JavaScript books would have a section warning that you should never use JS for anything critical to the application. Then there's the work in theorem provers over t"},{"id":8871281,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2015-01","story":"Research Priorities for Artificial Intelligence: An Open Letter","stance":"reference","why":"Recalls a video on training networks while holding connections constant","quote":"I've seen very little about this, except for a video where (as I recall) a multilayer neural network can be trained while holding certain connections at a constant value.","text":"I think the top priority should be how a hierarchy of weak AI agents can form strong AI with the ability to rewrite its own notions and not become unstable. I've seen very little about this, except for a video where (as I recall) a multilayer neural network can be trained while holding certain connections at a constant value. That way the network can be trained to do different tasks and then put back into those modes by setting those training connections to various values during runtime: [VIDEO] Eventually another neural net could be trained to know which values to hold to get different behaviors. It sounds like one of the goals is to reuse logic for similar tasks, and eventually be able to adapt in novel situations like our brains do. I believe there was also a bit about how time figured into all of this, but I haven’t watched it for a while. Anyway, I’m thinking that a lot of what we’ve learned about version control systems like git could be used to let an AI brainstorm in a sandbox and replace its own code as it improves, and always be able to revert or merge if needed. Throw in some genetic programming and decent lisp machines with 1000 cores and we could really have something. When I think about this sort of “real work”, as opposed to the daily minutia our industry gets mired in, it makes me sad inside."},{"id":5366021,"author":"cs702","month":"2013-03","story":"Geoffrey Hinton has joined Google","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of deep belief networks","quote":"For an overview of deep belief networks, see these videos","text":"Something really interesting must be happening with AI at Google, because in the past few months both Ray Kurzweil (the best-known proponent of the singularity) and Geoff Hinton (the crazy-talented individual who invented deep-belief networks using interconnected \"restricted Boltzman machines\"[1]) have joined the company. -- [1] For an overview of deep belief networks, see these videos: [LINK] , [VIDEO] , and [LINK]"},{"id":4613068,"author":"iandanforth","month":"2012-10","story":"Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge 2011 - Results","stance":"reference","why":"Older Hinton talk covering deep convolutional nets","quote":"And an older talk that covers some of what a deep convolutional net is","text":"Hinton's team (SuperVision) uses an interesting 'dropout' technique. He gave a Google Tech Talk on this back in June. [LINK] And an older talk that covers some of what a deep convolutional net is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4207408,"author":"utunga","month":"2012-07","story":"Machine Learning Video Library - Learning From Data (Abu-Mostafa)","stance":"reference","why":"Another Hinton Google tech talk from 2010","quote":"Incidentally Hinton gave another google tech talk in 2010","text":"If you enjoyed Geoff Hinton's talk you will probably find the theano 'deep learning' library to be of use. Still undergoing quite a lot of iteration but powerful and you get to run your stuff on the GPU for added fun. [LINK] . Incidentally Hinton gave another google tech talk in 2010 [VIDEO] ."},{"id":1946907,"author":"Dn_Ab","month":"2010-11","story":"Jeff Hawkins talk on modeling neocortex and its impact on machine intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Deep belief networks versus SVMs in depth","quote":"Deep belief networks though, are on another level.","text":"I would remove one maybe both muches. I agree that in general SVMs are better but they are also every bit as black box. Deep belief networks though, are on another level. literally, svms are depth 2, dbn's are depth unbounded, i think. [VIDEO]"}]},"X4Neivqp2K4":{"wy":"HN shares it as an impressive early example of computer-generated music and of Kurzweil's precocity.","q":{"x":"mind blown that kurzweil made programmed music in this presentation in 1965.","i":12520104,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12520104,"author":"KyleBrandt","month":"2016-09","story":"Learning Web Audio by Recreating the Works of Steve Reich and Brian Eno","stance":"rec","why":"Kurzweil at 17 presenting computer-composed music he built hardware for.","quote":"mind blown that kurzweil made programmed music in this presentation in 1965.","text":"Related, mind blown that kurzweil made programmed music in this presentation in 1965. [VIDEO] He was ~17 in that video, and he built the computer as well."},{"id":4671618,"author":"mixmax","month":"2012-10","story":"A Guy Who Saw Lincoln Get Shot Was on a TV Show in 1956","stance":"rec","why":"17-year-old Kurzweil plays music composed by his own program in 1965.","quote":"Pretty impressive for a 17 year old in 1965...","text":"Here's another interesting episode of the same show (I've got a secret) - it's a 17 year old Ray Kurzweil plaing the piano. His secret is that he wrote a computer program that made the music he's playing. Pretty impressive for a 17 year old in 1965... [VIDEO]"},{"id":12170553,"author":"steveeq1","month":"2016-07","story":"What makes music sound “good?”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ray Kurzweil seems to have made a passable song using a computer: [VIDEO] Granted, I wouldn't say it's song of the year or anything, but it's definitely listenable. Does anyone know of his general approach in his software for writing music?"},{"id":9047421,"author":"burritofanatic","month":"2015-02","story":"How I Taught My Computer to Write Its Own Music","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I immediately thought of Ray Kurzweil when he played music that was written by a computer he programmed in 1965 in which he demonstrated on the game show I've Got a Secret. I'd say with all of these responses, we've progressed quite a bit. [VIDEO]"}]},"Yl_K2Ata6XY":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a short, contextualizing explainer on declining crop nutrient density.","q":{"x":"has a nice explanation.","i":20968762,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20968762,"author":"dghughes","month":"2019-09","story":"Estimates that mineral levels in vegetables have dropped by up to 90% since 1914","stance":"rec","why":"Shows nutrient levels falling in all plants, even weeds, over a century.","quote":"has a nice explanation.","text":"Derek from Vertasium on YouTbe has a nice explanation. Even showing how the nutrient levels in weed specimens are also falling. The weeds were collected by researchers over the last 100 or more years. The weeds were vital because it showed it wasn't limited to farming practices it's all plants. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20624397,"author":"gholap","month":"2019-08","story":"Nutrient deficiencies in rice grown under higher carbon dioxide","stance":"rec","why":"Short explainer putting declining crop nutrition in context.","quote":"is a good short video that puts this into context.","text":"\"Is Our Food Becoming Less Nutritious?: Veritasium\"[1] is a good short video that puts this into context. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32224012,"author":"steve_adams_86","month":"2022-07","story":"A genetic modification boosts grain yields, shortens the growth duration of rice","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It hasn't actually been shown that it's the strain variation because identical strains (giving wild plants as samples) are shown to have the same declines. There's no known reason, but one decent hypothesis is that the atmosphere is changing and plants are bulking faster due to the increase of CO2. This is something you can demonstrate in a grow tent by venting in CO2. When plants have higher CO2 they increase the synthesis of carbohydrates, sugars and starches, and they decrease concentrations of protein and nutrients. This details someone of these details, including references to some of the studies you linked. Worth noting is that if GMO varieties had stark differences in nutrient content, it would be noted at the time and likely common knowledge by now. It doesn't seem to fill the nutrient gap though, and some varieties have improved nutrition profiles (though not sufficient to close the gap). [LINK] I'm not particularly pro-GMO; I think we'd be better off investing more in farming and our food in the first place. There are some things we think should be cheap in life, and food is one. We went from spending our days finding, processing, and eating food to expecting 20lb of potatoes to cost a few dollars, or less than an hour of work. There's something wrong with that picture in my mind. Even if we don't want to be farming in our day-to-day lives, I think this requires a greater investment than we're giving it. I'd say the same about education. I think it simply costs more"},{"id":27655515,"author":"rdedev","month":"2021-06","story":"Challenges in the diagnosis of magnesium status","stance":"reference","why":"Argues rising CO2 makes plants grow faster with fewer nutrients.","quote":"He argues that the increased CO2 in the atmosphere is making the plants grow faster.","text":"Veritasium did a video on this topic of vegetables becoming less nutritious [VIDEO] He argues that the increased CO2 in the atmosphere is making the plants grow faster. This is making the plants produce more carbs wet to other nutrients, making the plant less nutritious compared to the past"}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1],["_-mBeYC2KGc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["gMqZR3pqMjg",1]]},"j56s46e97Lo":{"wy":"Recommended as a fascinating and still relevant look at the physical footprint of government secrecy.","q":{"x":"It's almost a decade old, but still very much worth your time.","i":34112099,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34112099,"author":"eye_street","month":"2022-12","story":"I Found a Secret US Government Surveillance Program","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating talk on the footprint of secrecy.","quote":"It's almost a decade old, but still very much worth your time.","text":"Kinda related, I saw this fascinating presentation by Trevor Paglen recently. It's about the footprint of secrecy. It's almost a decade old, but still very much worth your time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14795607,"author":"mikejb","month":"2017-07","story":"Eye in the Sky","stance":"rec","why":"Paglen discusses his work on spy satellites in detail.","quote":"I found it highly interesting and can only recommend it.","text":"At the 30c3, Trevor Paglen gave a talk about his work, where he also goes into details or it [1]. I found it highly interesting and can only recommend it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23216401,"author":"mhh__","month":"2020-05","story":"Space Plane: Mysterious US military aircraft launches","stance":"reference","why":"Talk indicates questionable US signals intelligence in space.","quote":"","text":"It's spending months in space, I don't think it's for marketing. [VIDEO] Based on what this talk indicates, the US Government is doing some questionable (...) things in terms of signals intelligence in space. We probably won't know what X37-B is doing in space for years but I imagine they will have at least tried to do some big brother exercises. The US/Western Intelligence community effectively treats all communications as fair game - they always have (e.g. MI5 were capturing encryption keys from neutral embassies using microphones in the ~50/60s) they just possess the means to do it efficiently at large scale now. I imagine a large part of its mission is also just testing military equipment that can't go on the ISS but that's not as sexy."},{"id":6985873,"author":"gorekee","month":"2013-12","story":"30C3 Recordings","stance":"reference","why":"Paglen talk on seeing the secret state.","quote":"Seeing The Secret State: Six Landscapes","text":"Seeing The Secret State: Six Landscapes - [VIDEO] > In this talk, artist Trevor Paglen discusses his work attempting to “see” the various aspects of the secret state. In examples ranging from tracking spy satellites to foraging through the bureaucratic refuse of CIA front companies, Paglen will discuss methods used to identify and exploit structural contradictions in classified programs which render them visible, and comment on the aesthetics and politics of attempting to “see” secrecy. source: [LINK]"},{"id":6987191,"author":"F30","month":"2013-12","story":"30C3 Recordings","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned as widely recommended but not yet watched.","quote":"A lot of people also recommended 'Seeing The Secret State: Six Landscapes'","text":"The Exploration and Exploitation of an SD Memory Card: [LINK] Fast Internet-wide Scanning and its Security Applications: [LINK] A lot of people also recommended 'Seeing The Secret State: Six Landscapes', but I haven't seen that one myself so far: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ImoQqNyRL8Y",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1]]},"oLiheMQayNE":{"wy":"Cited as a firsthand account of the raw, unfiltered GPT-4 before safety tuning, especially the part after 45 minutes.","q":{"x":"He says it was extremely powerful and would answer any question whatsoever without hesitating.","i":36137308,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36951850,"author":"rpeden","month":"2023-08","story":"How to Get ChatGPT to Stop Apologizing?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with tester of early unfiltered GPT-4","quote":"you may find it interesting nonetheless","text":"This interview with one of the testers of early, unfiltered versions of GPT-4 might cover what you're looking for: [VIDEO] It's a video and not an article, but you may find it interesting nonetheless."},{"id":35854731,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-05","story":"The AI revolution is powered by these contractors making $15 an hour","stance":"rec","why":"Red teamer discusses GPT-4 base model before release","quote":"especially relevant parts are after 45 minutes","text":"Here are two. First one is Nathan Labenz who ended up Red Teaming for OpenAI during the six months after the base model was fully trained but before it was released (especially relevant parts are after 45 minutes): [VIDEO] Other one is Sebastien Bubeck who put Sydney into Bing before she was tamed down some notches; he explains the lobotomization process in a particularly poignant way, where they had the GPT keep trying to draw a unicorn even as it was progressively deadened: [LINK]"},{"id":36013530,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-05","story":"Don Knuth Plays with ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Access to GPT-4 before it was made docile","quote":"Nathan Labenz, red teamed GPT-4 for OpenAI","text":"All of that \"GPTism\" and the problems in the GPT conversation you posted are because of how they made it more docile and stupid by lobotomizing it with RLHF. It's not like that in its 'natural form' (its raw base model). If you don't believe me, check the two youtubes of people who had access to it before it was lobotomized: Nathan Labenz, red teamed GPT-4 for OpenAI: [VIDEO] Sebastien Bubeck, integrated GPT-4 with Bing for Microsoft: [LINK]"},{"id":36013689,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-05","story":"Don Knuth Plays with ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses original raw GPT-4 behavior","quote":"Nathan Labenz, red teamed GPT-4 for OpenAI (especially after 45 min)","text":"> I'm curious whether this fixation on specific topics is innate to the model or is a result of the aggressive RLHF to which GPT4 has been subjected. Yes it's because of the RLHF, depending on what you mean by 'fixation on specific topics'. > Anecdotally the strength of the model has degraded a lot as they've 'fine tuned' the model more. Yes this is true. For example Figure 8 in [LINK] They argue in the appendix that it does not affect 'capability' in answering test questions. But, there is a confounding factor. The RLHF includes both question-answer format training and docility training. For example if you see a question and you are in 'completion mode' (the only raw base mode) then you might suggest a second question. Whereas if you've had question-answer format training then you would probably try to answer the question. > I'd be curious to know how the original chaotic-neutral GPT4 responds. They talk about it in these two videos: Nathan Labenz, red teamed GPT-4 for OpenAI (especially after 45 min): [VIDEO] Sebastien Bubeck, integrated GPT-4 with Bing for Microsoft: [LINK]"},{"id":36137308,"author":"015UUZn8aEvW","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Is it just me or GPT-4's quality has significantly deteriorated lately?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with someone who used unfiltered GPT-4","quote":"He says it was extremely powerful and would answer any question whatsoever without hesitating.","text":"Here's another interview from a guy who had access to the unfiltered GPT-4 before its release. He says it was extremely powerful and would answer any question whatsoever without hesitating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36541914,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-06","story":"The Darwinian argument for worrying about AI","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence from older videos of pre-lobotomized GPT-4","quote":"look at the old youtubes from before they lobotomized gpt4","text":"bro you are just wrong, look at the old youtubes from before they lobotomized gpt4. Unless your argument is that AI hasn't yet marched to your house and picked you up out of your chair in that case I mean I guess OK. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35923140,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-05","story":"AI knows things no one told it","stance":"reference","why":"Red teamer saw raw base models before RLHF","quote":"GPT-4 Red Teamer Nathan Labenz","text":"> Exceptionally unconvinced that it's more than statistics It's not more than statistics. It's very complicated statistics. Model sizes used to be like one or ten parameters now they are a hundred billion. Inference and prediction used to be done on a laptop, now it's called training and inference respectively and it's done on a data center with ten thousand GPGPUs each like a thousand times as powerful as old laptops. > and frankly unhappy that pop Sci media is being this uncritical of claims it's emergent intelligence. It is emergent intelligence. This is very clear when you look at for example the ones who had access to the raw base models before they were made docile and stupid by RLHF lobotomization GPT-4 Red Teamer Nathan Labenz: [VIDEO] GPT-4 Bing integrator Sebastien Bubeck: [LINK]"}],"r":[["HQYsFshbkYw",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1]]},"o_TlE_U_X3c":{"wy":"HN uses it as the visual reference for Leap keys and Raskin's interface ideas whenever jump-to-text navigation or command palettes come up.","q":{"x":"This is a fascinating view into Raskin's user interface concepts.","i":12511173,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":22042903,"author":"marttt","month":"2020-01","story":"Leap Technology (keyboard vs. mouse on a Canon Cat machine, ca 1987)","stance":"rec","why":"A lovely scene from the original Canon Cat video","quote":"I just have to post this lovely scene from the original video separately.","text":"More on the Canon Cat: [LINK] [LINK] There are also a few previous HN discussions on this machine. EDIT: I just have to post this lovely scene from the original video separately. This is about human-computer interaction after all, isn't it! [VIDEO] Aren't we actually approaching an age where this kind of (almost-)single-purpose machines might be making a comeback? The focus this model seems to allow or enforce on the user feels kind of... healthy."},{"id":12511173,"author":"fzzzy","month":"2016-09","story":"Restoring YC's Xerox Alto: how our boot disk was trashed with random data","stance":"rec","why":"View of Raskin's user interface concepts in action","quote":"This is a fascinating view into Raskin's user interface concepts.","text":"I had read a lot about the Cat, but never saw it in action, so I decided to look for a video. This is a fascinating view into Raskin's user interface concepts. The world might have been a very different place if Jobs hadn't interfered with the Macintosh project. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47766828,"author":"OhMeadhbh","month":"2026-04","story":"Macs Since 1983","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Meh. the Lisa was absolutely, positively, explicitly NOT a Mac. If you don't get that, then you misunderstand the business of computing in the late 70s / early 80s. The author also ignored Jef Raskin's work on the original Mac project (I wrote a serial driver for him in the early 80s for a 6809 prototype he was working on.) If you're going to make one of these Mac hagiographies, it's useful to point out the Canon Cat (which was more in line with Jef's original idea for the Mac) and then point out how different it was from main-stream Macs. And then point out WHY the Lisa was not a Mac. So... Why do people think the Lisa was a prototype Mac? It's easy to understand the confusion. It has a 68k processor, a mouse and a bitmap screen. It was even used as a development platform for the original Macs. But it is not a Mac. The Lisa didn't have \"apps\" and \"files\" in the same way we think about them. It had \"tools\" and \"documents.\" The Lisa was strongly influenced by the Xerox Star. And while it's not exactly true to say the Lisa was Apple's version of the Star, there was definitely a strong resemblance. The Lisa (and the Star) came with all the functionality it would ever have built into the system. (This restriction changed later, but when launched, it was considered a complete system.) It had \"apps\" in the sense there were tool icons you could (double?) click to create a blank document of a specific type. But it took Apple over a year to decide they wanted to give third-parties the"},{"id":47338227,"author":"entaloneralie","month":"2026-03","story":"Writing my own text editor, and daily-driving it","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"One of the first thing I added was Leap keys-type search, I didn't want modes.","text":"One of the first thing I added was Leap keys-type search, I didn't want modes. - Leap keys: [VIDEO] The second was pragma-mark navigation, so I can always see a overview of the codebase. - navbar: [LINK] I also wanted a local copy buffer specific to the project I work on, so I could easily manage multiple copies of the clipboard data(it's part of how I work)."},{"id":40261788,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2024-05","story":"Preliminary Macintosh Business Plan (12 July 1981) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Advertising video showing text editing on the Canon Cat","quote":"There's an advertising video on YouTube showing it in use for text editing","text":"> I can't find any demo or screen shots of the Canon Cat system in use There's an advertising video on YouTube showing it in use for text editing [VIDEO]"},{"id":37749804,"author":"throwaway71271","month":"2023-10","story":"PicoCalc: A Fully-Functional Clone of VisiCalc","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And the OS is something inspired from LEAP","text":"That looks seriously cool. Just the colors give me goosebumps :) completely offtopic: My dream is to build a super cheap child friendly computer, that the kids can build themselves, has a software keyboard that directly polls the gpio pins so that kids can modify their keyboard instead of having a keyboard controller (just so there is less mistery in what happens when a key is pressed). And the OS is something inspired from LEAP: [VIDEO] that allowing you to evaluate code and apply it to your current file. A bit like your LOAD81 but with text. I have a prototype now: [LINK] but it has a long way to go."},{"id":37942924,"author":"tivert","month":"2023-10","story":"Version Museum: A Visual History of Your Favorite Technology","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an unusual UI paradigm","quote":"the Canon Cat Leap Keys","text":"This is kind of neat from a nostalgia standpoint, but I wish there was some kind of \"Encyclopedia of Operating System UI,\" that broke UI features and paradigms down into their smallest units and tracked them across different operating systems and versions. Probably with a particular emphasis on defunct systems when there was a lot more diversity and innovation (e.g. the Canon Cat Leap Keys: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":35453220,"author":"afry1","month":"2023-04","story":"Vim-like “jump” cursor for Mac OS Window Management","stance":"reference","why":"Historical context for jump-to-character navigation","quote":"if you're interested in some historical context for this \"type characters and jump to point\" functionality, the Canon Cat","text":"For my emacs friends, here's a wonderful package that provides the same functionality: [LINK] And, if you're interested in some historical context for this \"type characters and jump to point\" functionality, the Canon Cat: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33288541,"author":"retrocryptid","month":"2022-10","story":"The Canon Cat","stance":"reference","why":"Leap search in action, though fast","quote":"You can see this in action in this YouTube video, but it happens pretty fast so you have to watch carefully","text":"If you press and release the LEAP key, it advances the cursor one character forward (or backwards if you hit the left leap key.) If you press down (but do not release) the LEAP key you enter a search semi-mode. As you type a search term in this semi-mode, the cursor moves to the first instance of that search term it finds. After moving to the first instance of the search term, you release the leap key to exit the search semi-mode. If you want to move the cursor to a subsequent instance of the search term, you press (and do not release) the \"USE FRONT\" key and press the leap key again (whose key front is labeled \"Leap Again.\") You can see this in action in this YouTube video, but it happens pretty fast so you have to watch carefully: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29376630,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2021-11","story":"Command palettes: how typing commands became the norm again","stance":"reference","why":"Leap keys demonstrated on the Canon Cat","quote":"demonstrated on the CanonCat computer from 1987 in this[2] video on YouTube.","text":"I could have sworn I saw a dropdown command/search dialog designed by Jeff Raskin as part of The Humane Interface, and demonstrated in PyTHE a Python imeplementation of it. Can't find any evidence of it, but I can find a 2003 Guido van Rossum review of Jeff Raskin's book \"The Humane Interface\" in which Raskin mentions LEAP(r) a design for adding search-backwards and search-forwards keys below the spacebar to save people from arrow keys and mouse use, which is demonstrated on the CanonCat computer from 1987 in this[2] video on YouTube. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26218866,"author":"lproven","month":"2021-02","story":"Canon Cat","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I have never played with one, only studied the videos","text":"I have never played with one, only studied the videos (e.g. [VIDEO] ) and documentation. It was a very radical UI. If you added programs, they just added new functionality to the existing editor. So, for instance, a spreadsheet module might mean that suddenly, when loaded, you can do operations like SUM{...} or MEAN{...} on a table of numbers in the editor. I've long wished that someone would implement the Canon Cat UI as an Emacs module. It would seem to me that Emacs has all or most of the necessary functionality already."},{"id":7547913,"author":"smorrow","month":"2014-04","story":"Jef Raskin's Swyft Card","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Leap cursor positioning on the Canon Cat","quote":"The Swyft card implemented at least the cursor positioning stuff in this video","text":"I don't know anything about the Swyft card, but I know something about the Cat (not much). The Swyft card implemented at least the cursor positioning stuff in this video: [VIDEO] Some things about the Cat that aren't in that video are interesting. The whole thing was a single-user (fine by me), single-process (fine for the time) and single-program system. When you turn it off it writes its whole memory (incl. framebuffer) out to disk, which I think is the only thing on the disk actually, and when you turn it on it reads it back in to memory. So it never had any concept of a file, of saving a file, or opening a file. Saving a document was just saving your state, which includes the document, and opening a document was leaping to that part of the screen. You could migrate your session by just carrying the disk with you. The tabulating and (re)calculating thing is slick, and I think the Swyft might have provided that feature too. If someone could figure out a way to reconcile that with the Unix notion that different programs, running in different processes, must do different things, I would be so happy."}],"r":[["dP1xVpMPn8M",1]]},"qSjGouBmo0M":{"wy":"Pointed to as a quick, readable summary of the data behind US healthcare costs.","q":{"x":"This video (which has been making its rounds) explains many of the contributing factors to the cost of healthcare.","i":6553993,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":7032348,"author":"projct","month":"2014-01","story":"The $200 Billion Rip-Off: Our broadband future was stolen (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of healthcare cost data, debunks obesity explanation.","quote":"a summary video by john green for those who don't feel like reading so much","text":"I'm not looking at the average for the whole country, I am looking at a specific piece of the FBI crime data, and comparing that to what should be by your own logic numbers that are already on the high side for other countries. In fact, the group I picked is of only areas with a population of less than 10,000, including areas where there is no population at all, as well as universities and colleges, excluding suburbs. It's about as biased toward the point you are trying to make as you can get from this data source and it still doesn't line up with what you are claiming. Feel free to provide evidence for your claim as you still have not done so. Here is one nonpartisan resource about healthcare I have offhand and a summary video by john green for those who don't feel like reading so much: [LINK] [VIDEO] Relevant points from it: no, obesity does not account for a significant portion; yes, it affects even those who pay; yes you need to look at a lot of data; and no the data does not agree with you. The John Green video has 2 other links of interest in the description, too. Your claims are still entirely unsubstantiated, and you keep throwing in more of them instead of backing your existing claims up with data."},{"id":6553993,"author":"tekalon","month":"2013-10","story":"Obamacare Website Crashing Because It Doesn't Want You To Know Cost of Plans","stance":"rec","why":"Explains factors behind high US healthcare costs.","quote":"This video (which has been making its rounds) explains many of the contributing factors to the cost of healthcare.","text":"[VIDEO] This video (which has been making its rounds) explains many of the contributing factors to the cost of healthcare."},{"id":7642165,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2014-04","story":"Blood of world's oldest woman hints at limits of life","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> So you just magically assume that there are no bad governments? That doesn't seem very reasonable to me. Nor does your claim that this is something governments are somehow magically good at. They're not. No. I didn't say that. There are bad people in the world. And I walk down street all the time, not taking into consideration that every person I met today might attempt to murder me horrifically if they intended it. Taking worse scenario in this case, isn't prudent. I'm talking how a government should behave and a lot of government, don't behave as you state. For example UK, Canadian and a lot of EU have socialized medicine and it works rather nice for them. True, some probably had a fair share of mismanagement and probably some abuse. That doesn't detract from my point that it's possible to have decent government. > If that were actually true, people would behave very differently than they do. Any behavior that, on average, reduces your life expectancy, is irrational under your assumption. And since most behaviors do that, your assumption leads to the conclusion that people should not do most of the things they in fact do. You are constructing a strawman there. It won't work. I'm talking about immediate life altering changes. Not some, you'll die if you smoked bacon. Or hot dogs. You need a heart in seven days or you are good as dead. You are given option: A) pay X+30 amount of dollars and survive B) go online, research the prices, try to find same procedure for amount X, "}]},"rCfPQLVzus4":{"wy":"Recommended for a math-heavy but thorough treatment of the ultraviolet catastrophe and the history of the experiments.","q":{"x":"I had just finished watching a wonderful video on the \"Ultraviolet Catastrophe","i":32832679,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32832679,"author":"sbuttgereit","month":"2022-09","story":"Death by hockey sticks","stance":"rec","why":"Math-heavy but detailed treatment of the ultraviolet catastrophe.","quote":"I had just finished watching a wonderful video on the \"Ultraviolet Catastrophe","text":"Kinda funny. Just before seeing this article I had just finished watching a wonderful video on the \"Ultraviolet Catastrophe\" ( [VIDEO] ) which isn't exactly the same as what's being talked about here... but in the same class of problem if you make that class big enough :-). When looking at these sorts of things, surely such \"hockey sticks\" are often times just not considering the limiting factors? I'm no mathematician (by any approximation) but it seems like more often than not finding such a result should point to some incompleteness or some closer look being required to get the full picture. (P.S. that video was very math heavy and no, I didn't understand most of the math beyond hand-wavy terms... still glad they went into the detail.)"},{"id":31450268,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2022-05","story":"PBS SpaceTime","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture-style history of experiments behind the ultraviolet catastrophe.","quote":"they go into the histories of some of these experiments","text":"Science Asylum is great, but I dislike the 90s educational show presentation format. An absolute gem I've stumbled across is ScienceClic: [LINK] They have amazing presentations on Relativity, my favorite being: \"A new way to visualize General Relativity\" - [LINK] \"Hawking Radiation\" - [LINK] Another, while heavier and less accessible channel is Physics Explained: [LINK] These are closer like lectures, but I enjoy them because they go into the histories of some of these experiments; and what the prevailing wisdom was around the time the experiments were performed: \"What is the Ultraviolet Catastrophe?\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":45725853,"author":"aeonik","month":"2025-10","story":"Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos on the ultraviolet catastrophe and action.","quote":"","text":"You know about the Ultraviolet catastrophe? Physics Explained Ultraviolet Catastrophe: [VIDEO] This Veritassium Video goes back further in time and talks about Action, and the Genesis of this idea. [LINK] Chemistorian, The history of Atomic Theory. [LINK]"},{"id":32416577,"author":"squeaky-clean","month":"2022-08","story":"The strength of the strong force","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the ultraviolet catastrophe leading to quantization of radiation.","quote":"","text":"This is the basis of the \"ultraviolet catastrophe\" and lead to the discovery of quantization of radiation. The \"catastrophe\" being that as classical laws for light implied a potentially unbounded amount of energy as wavelength decreased. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["U7VwtOrwceo",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"wgHIkdUQbp0":{"wy":"Recommended as a practical, approachable build-it-step-by-step introduction to writing compilers, light on theory.","q":{"x":"Not too much theory but from a nuts and bolts perspective I found it extremely approachable.","i":23195604,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":40302518,"author":"neonsunset","month":"2024-05","story":"The search for easier safe systems programming","stance":"rec","why":"Video series on writing a .NET compiler from scratch.","quote":"A video series on writing a compiler for .NET from scratch","text":"I don't have something that could be of Crafting Interpreters level on hand, but the general suggestion given to this question on DotNetEvolution discord by Roslyn team members was to start with the spec itself: [LINK] This might be a bit of a learning curve if you don't have experience with writing compilers like that though. There are other materials that might prove useful should you go down this path: - A series of blog posts of a student who is writing a Rust to .NET compiler which you could follow along: [LINK] The project itself: [LINK] - A video series on writing a compiler for .NET from scratch: [VIDEO] Notes: [LINK] As for CLR via C#, while it has certain facts that remained the same, the ecosystem has changed a lot since then, you really want to target the latest LTS to get best experience and performance. If you liked it, then I can strongly suggest [LINK] which covers .NET runtime internals. p.s.: if it's any consolation, the promise in CLR via C# of JIT having the advantage of dynamically profiling code execution and compiling to profile-guided version, tuned to the exact hardware and environment is finally fulfilled, many years later :)"},{"id":23195604,"author":"Nelkins","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Online Compiler Courses?","stance":"rec","why":"Step-by-step build of a full programming language, practical and approachable.","quote":"Not too much theory but from a nuts and bolts perspective I found it extremely approachable.","text":"Here's a great YouTube series where a developer codes, step-by-step, a pretty fully-fledged programming language: [VIDEO] Not too much theory but from a nuts and bolts perspective I found it extremely approachable."},{"id":48796700,"author":"tester756","month":"2026-07","story":"Introduction to Compilers and Language Design","stance":"reference","why":"Playlist the commenter used while learning compilers.","quote":"or playlist like this","text":"I've been working full time for like 3 yrs as C# dev + doing higher edu at weekends at the same time and I was about to decide thesis topic. I've been searching for something challenging and found some very random post on programming forums about how compilers are hard etc and decided to give it a try. I had kind of advantage that I accidentally had some significant amount of experience with handwritten parsers (at first job we were doing custom-markdown-like-language renderer as PoC or even when doing apprenticeship in high school I was rolling out csv parser instead of using libs, because... I'm not sure why, I probably didnt know how to use package manager or something) I started reading about it a lot like dragon book (but it wasnt that useful tbh, too much math heavy) or [LINK] or playlist like this: [VIDEO] And after year of jumping into it from time to time I've implemented small, custom-lang to LLVM IR to webassembly (via LLVM) compiler. Then I had to find new job (we were very poorly paid) and I was interested in semiconductors industry because it was gaining traction (e.g chip war book) and it felt way more engineering oriented unlike web dev. Web dev tech decisions felt for me very religious, like fancy-conferences/blogposts oriented. And since semiconductor industry often touches compilers, then that was opportunity for: better salary, interesting projects and in future transition to compilers I've joined semico company as C# dev and then due to project cancellati"}],"r":[["mn4L_aJ1rV4",1]]},"xVodkE47aLw":{"wy":"Recommended in housing and architecture threads for its engineers' critique of five-over-one construction.","q":{"x":"Here's an enlightening chapter of \"Well there's your problem\" podcast about this with actual civil engineers","i":27649798,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40729202,"author":"cr125rider","month":"2024-06","story":"In Praise of 5-Over-1 Buildings (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Engineers-and-others podcast episode ripping into five-over-one buildings, rambling and NSFW.","quote":"It’s a journey.","text":"A podcast, Well There’s Your Problem, run by some engineers and others who rip into them a bit: [VIDEO] Some NSFW language I’m sure. Lots of rambling. It’s a journey."},{"id":27649798,"author":"glogla","month":"2021-06","story":"So you want to build a house more efficiently","stance":"rec","why":"Civil engineers discuss engineered wood in American houses.","quote":"Here's an enlightening chapter of \"Well there's your problem\" podcast about this with actual civil engineers","text":"American houses aren't really built from \"wood\" as in directly from trees - they are build from \"engineered wood\" like fiberboard and particleboard. That's a lot of industrial processing with a lot of oil-based glues and so on. By weight, the houses are almost more oil than wood. Here's an enlightening chapter of \"Well there's your problem\" podcast about this with actual civil engineers [VIDEO]"},{"id":29175737,"author":"_huayra_","month":"2021-11","story":"When is the revolution in architecture coming?","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast on why five-over-one construction is a disaster.","quote":"This podcast goes over what an absolute disaster this type of construction is [0]","text":"Yes, a common construction are these five-over-ones, with a steel-reinforced concrete first floor (for retail) and then wood framed 5-story apartments above. This podcast goes over what an absolute disaster this type of construction is [0], but for apartments in most major US cities west of the Mississippi, it is hard to avoid _some_ sort of wood framing. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tWtvrPTbQ_c",1]]},"y4GB_NDU43Q":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the canonical thought experiment whenever authentication failures, account compromises or Google outages come up.","q":{"x":"It's basically the scenario Tom Scott laid out about what would happen if Google stopped checking passwords","i":25726951,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35916491,"author":"richardwhiuk","month":"2023-05","story":"Welcome to Life: the singularity, ruined by lawyers by Tom Scott","stance":"rec","why":"Related Tom Scott fictional video.","quote":"is another, different topic, but still worth watching.","text":"Single Point of Failure: The (Fictional) Day Google Forgot To Check Passwords - [VIDEO] is another, different topic, but still worth watching."},{"id":18235670,"author":"zaxomi","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: YouTube down?","stance":"rec","why":"Fictional scenario of Google forgetting to check passwords.","quote":"I can recommend Tom Scott","text":"When YouTube comes back up again, I can recommend Tom Scott with \"Single Point of Failure: The (Fictional) Day Google Forgot To Check Passwords\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30916595,"author":"iso1631","month":"2022-04","story":"Bearer tokens are just awful","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There was an early Tom Scott video imagining a world where google just says \"yes\" to passwords","text":"So I have a service using openid and I use google to authenticate the person logging in. I do that, then that's done. Now I could simply issue a session cookie, or a permament cookie, to the client so we never have to use google again. Or I could make it so that cookie lasts 10 minutes before reauthentication. I can use a refresh token which means they don't need to reauthenticate but if google expires the connection (disabled user etc), I expire mine. Either way, that's my decision as the person running my site, and it's the clients decision as the person running the client to decide whether to send the blob, or to share that blob with other clients. The only information I know is that somebody authenticated with google at a specific time and I had proof of that, I have no control over what happens to that blob. Google has nothing to do with what I or the client do with the information. You're right that I can't audit the third party (google, microsoft, etc), but I don't pretend I can. Google could simply change their password checking to \"if (true)\", that's google's business. If I'm worried about the third party authenticator then I shouldn't use it, and I should roll my own. (There was an early Tom Scott video imagining a world where google just says \"yes\" to passwords[0]) I have an internal read-only blog site using our corporate openid service to ensure that someone accesses the site. Once they authenticate, I allow them access for 30 days before forcing a reauthenticati"},{"id":30842692,"author":"judge2020","month":"2022-03","story":"Today Google is turning on tracking for many users that previously turned it off","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Google's entire business hinges on user privacy from everyone other than Google. If, tomorrow, either this Account Security scenario happened[0] or this South Park website happened[1] (everyone's internet history searchably by anyone), and it was done at the incompetence of Google, they would crumble overnight. They might recover within a year or two if they release a statement and fix it within a few hours or something, but it would be devastating and they would have to kiss their entire Google Cloud business (encompassing Cloud Platform and Workspace) goodbye. Once more, even for their ad business, they don't sell that data, they target based off of it. They would lose their competitive data advantage overnight if someone could pay them $100 per-user for every user's full advertising profile since they could then go behind Google's back and out-header-bid Google with lower margins. 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK]"},{"id":27923742,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2021-07","story":"Google broke a conditional statement that verifies passwords on Chrome OS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just reading the title it sounded very much like \"Single Point of Failure: The (Fictional) Day Google Forgot To Check Passwords\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":25726951,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-01","story":"Parler Databases Disclosed","stance":"reference","why":"Scenario of missing password checks.","quote":"It's basically the scenario Tom Scott laid out about what would happen if Google stopped checking passwords","text":"It's basically the scenario Tom Scott laid out about what would happen if Google stopped checking passwords... except it happened to Parler. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25416881,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-12","story":"Google outage – resolved","stance":"reference","why":"Parable about single point of failure in login.","quote":"","text":"As many comments in this thread have mentioned, the crash seems to happen when when logged in; youtube and other services seem to work when don't send the session cookie. This suggests something very fundamental related to user account/sessions is failing... Did Maria Christensen make a mistake when adding \"return true;\"? (This is a joke referencing Tom Scott's 2014 parable[1] about the danger of designing a system with a single point of failure . Tom's tells the fictional tale of a high level employee at Google adding \"return true;\" to the global \"handleLogin()\" function.) [1] [VIDEO] (you might need to open this link in a private window...)"},{"id":18357546,"author":"jamieweb","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: What if gmail was compromised?","stance":"reference","why":"Scenario of Google account compromise.","quote":"","text":"Even worse could be an RCE vuln in Chrome at the same time that the google.com search page gets compromised... Also I think the post you're talking about is this video by Tom Scott: [VIDEO]"}]},"yvhv7bgmz64":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny but surprisingly accurate illustration of streaming fragmentation driving piracy.","q":{"x":"This video is played for laughs but I was shocked to learn how much of it was true and not just satire","i":39255311,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40143473,"author":"geerlingguy","month":"2024-04","story":"'Expensive' Streaming Services Are a Key Reason for Americans to 'Pirate'","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates complexity of the streaming situation","quote":"And for a great illustration of the complexity of our current situation, see this videogamedunkey video","text":"\"If buying isn't owning, then pirating isn't stealing.\" And for a great illustration of the complexity of our current situation, see this videogamedunkey video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39255311,"author":"skeaker","month":"2024-02","story":"Netflix: Piracy is difficult to compete against and growing rapidly","stance":"rec","why":"Satirical but largely accurate guide to streaming fragmentation","quote":"This video is played for laughs but I was shocked to learn how much of it was true and not just satire","text":"Yep. This video is played for laughs but I was shocked to learn how much of it was true and not just satire: [VIDEO] Content being fragmented and constantly coming and going is a massive problem for users. Netflix used to be the go-to to find just about any given show or film, but has become a mystery box where the contents change on a near-daily basis. Piracy is reliable and simple instead, and works like Netflix used to: Just search up what you want to watch and you're golden. It has been and always will be a service problem."},{"id":40225641,"author":"j1elo","month":"2024-05","story":"Show HN: I made an app that helps you find where to stream movies and TV shows","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"obligatory Dunkey","text":"Just like some times we use the \"obligatory XKCD\" around here, this time it applies more a funny \"obligatory Dunkey\" :) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CbHjcwIoTiY",1],["tDacjrSCeq4",1],["gSrnXgAmK8k",1],["Pm8P4oCIY3g",1]]},"VVUeqxXwCA0":{"wy":"Cited in urban planning threads as an accessible explanation of why low-density suburbs cost more to maintain than they bring in.","q":{"x":"Not Just Bikes is a great channel on urban development","i":27931777,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27931777,"author":"Rendello","month":"2021-07","story":"We’d rather have the iceberg than the ship","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how suburban development makes cities poorer","quote":"Not Just Bikes is a great channel on urban development","text":"Not Just Bikes is a great channel on urban development, and he did a series on Strong Towns' ideas specifically. I like this one, \"How Suburban Development Makes American Cities Poorer\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":41115340,"author":"rglullis","month":"2024-07","story":"If we want a shift to walking, we need to prioritize dignity","stance":"reference","why":"Context for the suburban question","quote":"","text":"Holy shit, talk about reading things out of context. You, dear sir, have absolutely nothing in common with the average North American who lives in the suburbs. The question was not directed at you. If you want to at least try to understand the context before jumping to give your opinion and share with us your psycho issues, try watching [VIDEO]"},{"id":36723267,"author":"gaff33","month":"2023-07","story":"Electric Vehicles for Everyone? The Impossible Dream","stance":"reference","why":"Low density areas are subsidised by dense ones","quote":"low density urban areas are subsidsed by high densisty urban areas","text":"The problem is that low density urban areas are subsidsed by high densisty urban areas: [LINK] [VIDEO] etc They are also terrible for the planet."},{"id":26940534,"author":"anonporridge","month":"2021-04","story":"Study finds ride-sharing intensifies urban road congestion","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban development wastes resources","quote":"Suburbia on the other hand makes us all worse off.","text":"It breeds efficiency and self sufficiency. Less wasted resources on virtually every metric. Suburbia on the other hand makes us all worse off. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25980051,"author":"imtringued","month":"2021-01","story":"Americans Don’t Know What Urban Collapse Looks Like","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban road costs exceed tax revenue","quote":"The tax money you receive doesn't cover maintenance.","text":"That's a problem with suburbia. You need a lot of road per house. The tax money you receive doesn't cover maintenance. Even the worst city blocks bring in enough tax revenue to maintain their roads. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["IroPQ150F6c",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["KSWqx8goqSY",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"1J9LOqiXdpE":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of carbon footprints and corporate greenwashing, for the claim that the concept came from BP marketing.","q":{"x":"The Climate Town channel on Youtube has lots of video's on this","i":47223911,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47223911,"author":"Maarten88","month":"2026-03","story":"Judge finalizes order for Greenpeace to pay $345M in ND oil pipeline case","stance":"rec","why":"Climate Town on BP marketing origin of the carbon footprint","quote":"The Climate Town channel on Youtube has lots of video's on this","text":"You know the carbon footprint concept was literally created by BP marketing, to place the blame for climate change on society, and distract from all the evil stuff they did to promote more fossil fuel consumption and sabotage climate science. The Climate Town channel on Youtube has lots of video's on this, such as this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37200761,"author":"mptest","month":"2023-08","story":"Gov. Newsom declares state of emergency for southern California","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the carbon footprint idea in oil company marketing","quote":"was a piece of propaganda produced by a literal oil company[0]","text":">Given all those rhetorical shaming of individuals to consume less, it's illuminating to actually look at the data and see what's the actual the effect even if you could literally force (instead of shaming) everyone to live that lifestyle. Yeah, almost like the entire notion of a \"carbon footprint\" was a piece of propaganda produced by a literal oil company[0] to move the blame of polluting the planet from the corporations that knowingly did it, to earnest and empathetic individuals like the original person I replied to, causing them to abstain from vacation (and more) for negligible benefit while the corporations continue to pollute with impunity. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32179976,"author":"baxuz","month":"2022-07","story":"Launch HN: Bend (YC S22) – Automatically measure your company's carbon footprint","stance":"reference","why":"Climate Town argues the carbon footprint concept is a scam","quote":"Carbon footprints are a scam","text":"Carbon footprints are a scam: [VIDEO] Carbon offsets are a scam: [LINK] Hearing people talk about these give me the same vibes as techbros talking about stocks and crypto."},{"id":30959189,"author":"kleer001","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Shouldn't we be turning an eye to power consumption?","stance":"reference","why":"Climate Town on why carbon footprints shift blame to individuals","quote":"","text":"> Maybe consider your carbon footprint when performing that next full code compile? Why \"Carbon footprint\" is a lie. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] That said being efficient is not a bad idea. There is a market for chips with less power consumption. A 250GB seasonal DL is kinda wasteful. But the solution to climate change (and it's not a crisis) is not atoning for our eco-sins. That's a misapplication of the JudeoChristian myth of sin/salvation."}],"r":[["9FdHq3WfJgs",1],["XBmJay_qdNc",1]]},"8nt3edWLgIg":{"wy":"HN cites it in AI risk debates, sometimes as an argument that has aged well and sometimes as an example of alarmist framing.","q":{"x":"(Worth a watch if you have 15 mins).","i":36576781,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36576781,"author":"billti","month":"2023-07","story":"Douglas Hofstadter changes his mind on Deep Learning and AI risk","stance":"rec","why":"Logical case for AI risk that has aged well","quote":"(Worth a watch if you have 15 mins).","text":"I don't think many people are worried that what we have now is a major risk. The major concern is the implications of the trajectory we are now on. If you look at what ChatGPT and Midjourney and the like can do now compared to just a couple of years ago, it's pretty incredible. If you extrapolate the next few similar jumps in capability, and assume that won't be 20 years away, then what AI is going to be capable of before even my kids leave college is going to be mind-boggling, and in some possible futures not in a good way. I remember seeing this talk from Sam Harris nearly 6 years ago and it logically making a lot of sense back then ( [VIDEO] ). The past couple of years have made this all the more prescient. (Worth a watch if you have 15 mins)."},{"id":32811784,"author":"bumby","month":"2022-09","story":"Questions about the hard limits of human intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Harris compares von Neumann to us as we are to chickens","quote":"Sam Harris's comparison between ourselves and John Van Neumann","text":"This made me think about Sam Harris's comparison between ourselves and John Van Neumann as analogous to a comparison between a chicken and ourselves. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15472006,"author":"whatyoucantsay","month":"2017-10","story":"There's No Fire Alarm for Artificial General Intelligence","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Even people giving TED talks about the threat of AI are unable to marshal an appropriate emotional response","text":"Even people giving TED talks about the threat of AI are unable to marshal an appropriate emotional response to the dangers that lie ahead: [VIDEO]"}]},"HTzw_grLzjw":{"wy":"Mentioned occasionally as an episode on environmental toxins in food and water, though one commenter lumps it in with unregulated quackery.","q":{"x":"just did a great episode on PFAS, microplastics, and other environmental toxins in food and water.","i":41975171,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41975171,"author":"TechDebtDevin","month":"2024-10","story":"Millions may rely on groundwater contaminated with PFAS for drinking water","stance":"rec","why":"Episode on PFAS, microplastics and environmental toxins in food and water.","quote":"just did a great episode on PFAS, microplastics, and other environmental toxins in food and water.","text":"Dr. Rohnda Patrick just did a great episode on PFAS, microplastics, and other environmental toxins in food and water. Tap water tends to have less of these PFAS/nano plastics than bottled water via degradation of the bottle. Topo Chico is especially problematic (nothing to do with the bottle).[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44259505,"author":"mavilia","month":"2025-06","story":"A receipt printer cured my procrastination","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses BPA in thermal paper receipts.","quote":"thermal paper receipts are loaded with BPA which gets absorbed through your skin","text":"> Also, isn't handling a lot of receipt paper bad for you or something? Yes if you are worried about microplastics. Dr. Rhonda Patrick has an episode where she talks about receipts specifically (at the 1hr 2m mark) [0]. To paraphrase: thermal paper receipts are loaded with BPA which gets absorbed through your skin. The chemicals are worse too since the plastics for receipts aren't scrutinized like they would be for a food storage item. They use BPA is used as a color developer in thermal printing. It gets way worse if you've used hand sanitizer, lotion, or sunscreen recently since those increase skin permeability. Studies show dramatically higher absorption rates when your skin barrier is compromised. Although I can't find a link for that right now. Definitely something to think about if you're a cashier or work somewhere handling receipts all day. I've started just declining receipts unless I actually need them for returns/expense reports. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":48947748,"author":"vablings","month":"2026-07","story":"FDA authorized Zyn nicotine pouches without knowing what they were made of","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I am anti unregulated bullshit quackery like the below videos","text":"\"Why on earth would they give funding to research if the people who pay for that research (you) actively think it's scam?\" First of all. I never specifically said that the research is a scam. I am fully onboard with real research into the topic to explore the effects on health \"You are arguing that the government shouldn't fund research on this problem (it's a grift)\" That is NOT what I said, I specifically believe the so called \"health and wellness industry\" is capitalizing on largely unfounded beliefs that microplastics are already proven to be hazardous (not true). If it turns out that microplastics are actually a serious health hazard, that doesn't even mean that the health and wellness industry is right, they just got lucky on another grift. They operate thousands of grifts every year often selling things that are blatantly dangerous. I am pro good research. I am anti unregulated bullshit quackery like the below videos [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"QwiUVUJmGjs":{"wy":"HN cites it as the key piece of evidence that intelligence officials misled Congress, and as a reported breaking point for Snowden.","q":{"x":"Watch this, then ask yourself how you feel","i":5835966,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5835966,"author":"kevinalexbrown","month":"2013-06","story":"US intelligence mining data from 9 US Internet companies in broad secret program","stance":"rec","why":"Clapper's answer to Wyden, worth watching from 6:40.","quote":"Watch this, then ask yourself how you feel","text":"I've lived under surveillance before, so I feel my perspective might be somewhat appropriate. I won't comment on the specifics (uninteresting and irrelevant, had to do with where I was living). I won't even make a statement about whether it's justified in my case or in general. I'd just ask everyone here to do one thing: Watch this, then ask yourself how you feel (if it doesn't go directly to 6:40, fastforward to it, then watch for 30 seconds): [VIDEO] I understand about various interpretations of \"collect\", \"intercept\", \"analyze\", etc. Just watch the video, and ask yourself how you feel. Please know that I'm not telling you how to feel, just providing a small snippet of a conversation. Why do you feel that way, and what does that feeling say about you, or your society? Originally found in a comment: [LINK]"},{"id":38545359,"author":"sharma-arjun","month":"2023-12","story":"Apple Confirms Governments Using Push Notifications to Surveil Users","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Wyden questioning Clapper before the Snowden leaks.","quote":"He's the same person who was questioning James Clapper in Congress about mass surveillance before the Snowden leaks [1].","text":"Wyden is far removed from the part of the government which engages in surveillance. He's the same person who was questioning James Clapper in Congress about mass surveillance before the Snowden leaks [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23174486,"author":"A4ET8a8uTh0","month":"2020-05","story":"Senate narrowly rejects plan to require a warrant for Americans’ browsing data","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the Wyden-Clapper exchange that reportedly pushed Snowden.","quote":"Reportedly, this lie was a breaking point for Snowden who saw the exchange between Clapper and Wyden[0].","text":"Maybe. That said, he was one of the few people, who actually made Clapper lie in front the of the entire world. Reportedly, this lie was a breaking point for Snowden who saw the exchange between Clapper and Wyden[0]. While I would be willing to accept your suggestion that is basically just Kabuki theatre, it seems even more cynical than I am willing to contemplate now. I am saying this, because Wyden visibly pushed Clapper ( apparently knowing that he was lying ). [0] [VIDEO] edit: Come to think of it, if Wyden was a genuine threat, I am sure security apparatus would have tried to take him down by now. But no dead hookers, no unsavory hobbies, no character assassination.. your suggestion makes more sense than I initially thought it had."},{"id":24517182,"author":"ifmpx","month":"2020-09","story":"Trump offered to pardon Assange if he provided source for DNC emails – lawyer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"No, sir. They don't lie. At least not wittingly..","text":"No, sir. They don't lie. At least not wittingly.. There are cases where they may inadvertently.. perhaps lie, but not wittingly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21002503,"author":"StanislavPetrov","month":"2019-09","story":"United States Files Civil Lawsuit Against Edward Snowden","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the lies of government officials like James Clapper, who perjured himself before the Senate","text":"Snowden sacrificed his extremely lucrative career, his access and his ability to live in his own country to expose the illegal and unconstitutional conduct being carried out by the NSA. He put himself in extreme legal jeopardy to expose this illicit government activity and the lies of government officials like James Clapper, who perjured himself before the Senate when asked directly about these activities. In direct contrast to Snowden and his unselfish, principled actions are people like you, who spread blatant falsehoods, such as your suggestion that Snowden gave the Russian government intel. The Constitution is the highest law of the land. Those in government and in other official positions who have violated our Constitution are the real enemies of our country, not the people who expose their crimes. Sanctimonious babble about, \"national security\" does not excuse them, lessen or mitigate their crimes in any way. Hopefully the release of Snowden's book will rekindle interest in the unconstitutional, illegal surveillance that continues to be carried out in the name of, \"national security\" so those lawbreakers and criminals in the \"intelligence community\" can be put behind bars where they belong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5835025,"author":"DrewHintz","month":"2013-06","story":"NSA Chief Denies Domestic Spying Story (2012)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the Director of National Intelligence specifically told Senator Ron Wyden that the NSA does not wittingly collect any type of data on millions of Americans","text":"In March 2013, the Director of National Intelligence specifically told Senator Ron Wyden that the NSA does not wittingly collect any type of data on millions of Americans: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5837115,"author":"tshile","month":"2013-06","story":"DNI Statement on Recent Unauthorized Disclosures of Classified Information","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"when asked by congress (I assume under oath) if the NSA was collecting data on US citizens, he responded \"No sir.","text":"This is the guy that, when asked by congress (I assume under oath) if the NSA was collecting data on US citizens, he responded \"No sir.\" That was just 3 months ago. So... not sure what his statements are worth now... [VIDEO] (starts at 6 minutes 10 seconds)"}]},"XTl7Jn_kmio":{"wy":"Referenced in static versus dynamic typing debates, including his remark that Hindley-Milner doesn't really work.","q":{"x":"Matthias Felleisen, one of the developers of Racket, recently gave a talk about the motivation behind typed Racket","i":12153312,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12153312,"author":"jhck","month":"2016-07","story":"Racket v6.6","stance":"rec","why":"Felleisen on motivation behind Typed Racket.","quote":"Matthias Felleisen, one of the developers of Racket, recently gave a talk about the motivation behind typed Racket","text":"Matthias Felleisen, one of the developers of Racket, recently gave a talk about the motivation behind typed Racket; it can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26173118,"author":"moocowtruck","month":"2021-02","story":"Nx: Multi-dimensional tensors Elixir lib with multi-staged compilation (CPU/GPU)","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as counterargument to static typing claims.","quote":"do you have maybe a counter argument to this fellow","text":"how is static typing lacking evidence? don't we use that in rust to prevent the existence of large swaths of errors vs say C? i know for sure when turning on typescript for some javascript projects at work we found many issues, those all seem like wins? Why when I look at some dynamic languages say for example clojure i see stuff like type annotations in their core libraries, and then spec's to help provide a lot of what a type system can do? do you have maybe a counter argument to this fellow [VIDEO]"},{"id":15469497,"author":"iLemming","month":"2017-10","story":"Clojure/Conj 2017 – Opening Keynote by Rich Hickey [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Felleisen says Hindley-Milner doesn't really work.","quote":"Hindley-Millner doesn't really work","text":"One thing that was a personal shocker for me to hear, from not just anyone, but Matthias Felleisen himself - \"My research group has investigated the topic for almost 3 decades, and we came to the conclusion that Hindley-Millner doesn't really work\". I was really baffled. That was at ClojureWest 2016. Here's the talk itself [VIDEO] . That talk is not about Clojure - Felleisen doesn't even use Clojure. That happened before Clojure.spec was announced. I couldn't really figure out what exactly he was talking about. Not until later, when I tried Spec."}]},"XvwNKpDUkiE":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the history of phototypesetters and a story about the freedom to control your own hardware.","q":{"x":"explained the brilliant and exiting history of this paper and its recreation, as well as the history of Phototypesetters.","i":10508925,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10508925,"author":"ATsch","month":"2015-11","story":"Experience with the Mergenthaler Linotron 202 Phototypesetter (1980) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with a paper's author on phototypesetter history and the paper's recreation.","quote":"explained the brilliant and exiting history of this paper and its recreation, as well as the history of Phototypesetters.","text":"Computerphile interviewed one of the writers of this paper in a series of videos, who explained the brilliant and exiting history of this paper and its recreation, as well as the history of Phototypesetters. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":25179358,"author":"leoc","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the hacking of the Mergenthaler Linotron 202 typesetting machine.","quote":"there's a Computerphile video","text":"0:16:40 For those who haven't seen it before, there's a Computerphile video [LINK] (related video: [VIDEO] ) about the hacking of the typesetting machine, a Mergenthaler Linotron 202. 0:24:31 The photo must be the one at [LINK] (probably the webpage, on Dennis Ritchie's old website, that made it famous recently)."},{"id":13623582,"author":"theoh","month":"2017-02","story":"A glitch in the human psyche that equates repetition with truth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's a somewhat tedious story but it's all about the freedom to control your own hardware.","text":"I've never seen Stallman speak, but that sounds like a similar situation to this anecdote from Nottingham in the 80s: [VIDEO] It's a somewhat tedious story but it's all about the freedom to control your own hardware."}],"r":[["CVxeuwlvf8w",1]]},"bEOOYbscyTs":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the talk by the author of Neanderthal and the Clojure GPU libraries, including comparisons against the JVM and CPU.","q":{"x":"Watch his talk from EuroClojure 2016 in Bratislava","i":14263530,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14263530,"author":"skardan","month":"2017-05","story":"Uncomplicate: Number crunching in Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by author of ClojureCUDA, ClojureCL and Neanderthal","quote":"Watch his talk from EuroClojure 2016 in Bratislava","text":"ClojureCUDA, ClojureCL (as in OpenCL) and Neanderthal are libraries developed by Dragan Djuric. Watch his talk from EuroClojure 2016 in Bratislava: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16334106,"author":"thebeardedone","month":"2018-02","story":"Interactive GPU Programming, Part 2: Hello OpenCL","stance":"reference","why":"Talk compares GPU implementation against JVM and CPU","quote":"After watching your talk on youtube","text":"ClojureCL looks quite awesome, it looks like it takes all of the boiler plate out in comparison to C but I am very interested in the overhead. I tried aparapi for my bachelor thesis and for larger problems it seemed to have quite the overhead that the normal kernel did not have (several years ago). After watching your talk on youtube [1], I saw you compared the GPU implementation to the JVM and other CPU implementations in Clojure. Do you have any numbers for the same kernel being called from a native C openCL application? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13198016,"author":"tmpLisp","month":"2016-12","story":"The Idea of Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"Clojure GPU computing talk","quote":"Data crunching on GPUs is a LISP strength.","text":"Data crunching on GPUs is a LISP strength. It runs code orders of magnitude faster than say python or c++. [VIDEO]"}]},"frjaQ17yAww":{"wy":"Used as an illustration of the confident-idiot effect and of people pretending to know things.","q":{"x":"The article makes a lot more sense after watching it IMO.","i":8520432,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8520432,"author":"dlss","month":"2014-10","story":"We Are All Confident Idiots","stance":"rec","why":"Clip that makes the article make more sense","quote":"The article makes a lot more sense after watching it IMO.","text":"If anyone is curious about the Kimmel clip, it's on youtube: [VIDEO] The article makes a lot more sense after watching it IMO."},{"id":31464920,"author":"robocat","month":"2022-05","story":"We are all confident idiots (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Link to the missing video from the article","quote":"","text":"Bitrot? Missing assets for video and some images (perhaps on iPadOS only?). Jimmy Kimmel’s Lie Witness News SXSW edition link: [VIDEO] “Imagine that the illustration below represents a curved tube lying horizontally on a table:” [LINK] Paper without video links: [LINK]"},{"id":8558301,"author":"kelvin0","month":"2014-11","story":"The hipster effect: When anticonformists all look the same","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"there is also a hilarious sample of the 'hipster' population in this Youtube video","text":"For what it's worth, there is also a hilarious sample of the 'hipster' population in this Youtube video (Jimmy Kimmel 0:53 mark) [VIDEO] Of course interviewees in this video are probably paid actors playing dumb, but boy it doesn't the stereotype ..."}]},"kAG39jKi0lI":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke or light recommendation in threads where dongles, Apple or trademark confusion come up.","q":{"x":"recommend a great British comedy sketch: My Blackberry Is Not Working!","i":20511439,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20511439,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2019-07","story":"Apple Factory Thefts: Secret Tunnels, Hidden Crawl Spaces","stance":"rec","why":"Great British comedy sketch.","quote":"recommend a great British comedy sketch: My Blackberry Is Not Working!","text":"This is a good occasion to recommend a great British comedy sketch: My Blackberry Is Not Working! [VIDEO] One reference that may not be familiar to all viewers: when the sketch was made in 2010, Orange was a popular UK mobile carrier. Enjoy!"},{"id":36397886,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-06","story":"Apple Is Taking on Apples in a Weird Trademark Battle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Except rip offs happen regularly still with little intervention As with spam, what you see is what gets past the filter; the actual attempted rate is much higher, and gets goods seized at customs when C&Ds aren't enough. As for \"this is ridiculous\": yes, that's why Apple lost the case it's here appealing. But Apple also used to regularly lose to the other Apple Corp. because trademarks are separated by domain and the music company wanted to make sure the computer company never did music. Eventually they paid a lot of money and agreed to stop fighting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5404911,"author":"eggsbox","month":"2013-03","story":"Inappropriate comments at pycon 2013 called out","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You are not the first person though to be caught telling dongle jokes","text":"Agreed that Adria had every right to feel offended. There is no place for humour in public. You are not the first person though to be caught telling dongle jokes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5421356,"author":"tzs","month":"2013-03","story":"Fork My Dongle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this pretty funny comedy sketch from a show on the BBC where dongles play a small part","text":"Speaking of dongles in a funny context, Googling for dongle jokes turned up this pretty funny comedy sketch from a show on the BBC where dongles play a small part: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["rRi8LptvFZY",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1],["7TqqdOcX4dc",1],["ZLWMQLMiTPk",1]]},"ksb3KD6DfSI":{"wy":"Used as an example in threads about media consolidation and distrust of news, though many commenters cite it critically or only as a talking point.","q":{"x":"In the same vein and much more entertaining is this video that compares local news channels","i":18219086,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18219086,"author":"jefflombardjr","month":"2018-10","story":"Brave New World Revisited, Revisited","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining compilation of local news stations reading identical scripts","quote":"In the same vein and much more entertaining is this video that compares local news channels","text":"This article does a good job of drawing parallels between the book and reality in a short amount of time. I feel like the article is just scratching the surface though. > Television was transforming the news into a rapid and proliferating series of images, a form of entertainment that prized ‘the unreal’ and the ‘more or less totally irrelevant’. You can contest facts, he said, but not images. Noam Chomsky has some excellent work in this area. It can be very very dry reading, but very informative. Check out manufacturing consent [LINK] In the same vein and much more entertaining is this video that compares local news channels: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33815884,"author":"lordfrito","month":"2022-12","story":"FTX’s Collapse Was a Crime, Not an Accident","stance":"reference","why":"Clips showing centralized message control across news stations","quote":"But watching those clip segments it's pretty clear there's a story/narrative being pitched by someone with the leverage to do so.","text":"This was great thanks... Also there's this [1], which just boggles my mind. I can't make sense of this level of centralized message control and dissemination. It's straight out of 1984. It's hard to believe \"the media\" (whoever \"they\" are) has an agenda... it's tinfoil hat stuff.. Usually I write it off as emergent behavior of collective groups of humans trying to make $$$ from clicks, standard selfish behavior + time, leading to a race towards the bottom that looks like this. But watching those clip segments it's pretty clear there's a story/narrative being pitched by someone with the leverage to do so. Is this propaganda? What is propaganda? How can I recognize it? Is everything propaganda? Is \"the media\" captured and is now just a tool of whatever \"system\" is controlling things. This is the kind of stuff Jean Baudrillard was writing about. Sometimes we get journalism other times we get whatever that this stuff is. I don't have a problem with journalists sticking to the facts, SBF hasn't been charged, so lets not lynch him just yet. But hopefully he's being investigated (they usually don't announce when they are investigating someone, as it puts them on notice). Overall I think this is a direct result the 1996 Telecommunications Act which eliminated the rules on the number of radio stations a company could own (which also was one of the most lobbied bills in history). In hindsight this was a big mistake, and set things up for the kind of centralized messaging we see. There "},{"id":32582929,"author":"babycake","month":"2022-08","story":"How workplace bullying went remote","stance":"reference","why":"Shows every news station saying the exact same thing","quote":"Here's an interesting video that shows every news station saying the exact same thing","text":"I don't think people are dumb though. Most working class people see through the BS because they live it everyday. I'm not talking about just us, I'm talking retail, waiters, miners, etc. They've seen way more shit with their bosses and life experiences to know what's what. I don't think it's infantilizing. I think since these major news sites are owned and backed by the rich, they have certain messages they want to push. And one idea they don't want people to know about is worker organization. The rich all have a vested interest to not mention it at all and instead want us to use their rigged process to resolve issues. Here's an interesting video that shows every news station saying the exact same thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28022489,"author":"putlake","month":"2021-07","story":"Six corporations control 90% of America media","stance":"reference","why":"Example of what happens when media ownership is concentrated","quote":"Examples for what happens when media ownership is concentrated","text":"Examples for what happens when media ownership is concentrated: [VIDEO] And this one I'm not sure if it's just good PR by Amazon or laziness by media outlets or both: [LINK]"},{"id":20672564,"author":"PavlovsCat","month":"2019-08","story":"GDPR is an identity thief's dream ticket to Europeans' data","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a conglomerate where switching the channel does not help","quote":"","text":"> The way you change a corporation is by not patronizing it until it changes. Unless it's a monopoly or companies colluding, which is prevented by enforcement of regulation, not any goodness on behalf of corporations. And unless it's a conglomorate, in which case, \"just switch the channel\" can sound kind of hollow. E.g. [VIDEO] In theory, we could change companies by abstaining, if they magically knew why you don't patronize them, and took that into acount. In practice we now have fewer consumer OS than in the 90s, the return of walled gardens, and people who avoid them don't affect anything, the walled gardens care less about what people want, than what the walled gardens want. > The problem with governments is that they don't work that way, so the opinion of anyone not in the majority is invalid. So why are their any laws, at all, protecting minorities, why are things like access to disabled regulated, why is not legal to just lynch a person \"most people don't like\", and so on? How long has Comcast been the worst thing since soaked bread, how is that possible, how have they not either changed or gone the way of the dodo? And why does Facebook keep regressing its UI even though anyone who cares hates it, \"as long as most people don't mind\"? How's YouTube shitting all over creators working out? Captured audiences and network effects wherever you look. For companies, \"If the majority does that with you then the corporation changes\" is apparently good enough, with governments y"},{"id":20360644,"author":"scarejunba","month":"2019-07","story":"California law banning bots from pretending to be real people without disclosure","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, amusing that people thought this was wholly irrelevant. Here's what I was thinking of when I said that: [VIDEO] Also amusing that there are two confident opposite opinions in response."},{"id":40043205,"author":"aeternum","month":"2024-04","story":"The truth is paywalled but the lies are free (2020)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"best demonstrated by this clip","text":"I find the opposite to be the case. The large news orgs with all the paywalls are now mostly used to push a narrative. This has been the case for years, best demonstrated by this clip: [VIDEO] The free internet still exists, and is not paywalled."},{"id":39514654,"author":"rglover","month":"2024-02","story":"What a major solar storm could do","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I find it interesting that last week, we had a nationwide AT&T outage and one of the causes speculated on local news outlets (within a couple hours) was a solar flare [1]. Now, less than a week later, we have articles in the New Yorker priming thoughts about solar storms. Why is this relevant? Consider where that speculation started: local news networks [2]. It's worth reading Silent Weapons for Quiet Wars [3] if you haven't already to understand why it's worth me mentioning this. Easily dismissed as \"conspiracy nonsense,\" but it's worth asking \"why this, why now?\" [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":38413930,"author":"strangesmells06","month":"2023-11","story":"X May Lose Up to $75M in Revenue as More Advertisers Pull Out","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Most people are more interested in accuracy, context, analysis, and trustworthiness. Lol imagine thinking you get this from big monopoly news media almost all of which are owned by private equity media conglomerates. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33840509,"author":"bitsnbytes","month":"2022-12","story":"The Twitter Files","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"One would have to assume that there was also a direct line to other social medial platforms. \" I would say its not a huge leap to think that. I would even say its more than just other social media platforms. I would say it goes into main stream media on BOTH sides, Hollywood, sports, etc including global corporations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33651932,"author":"radu_floricica","month":"2022-11","story":"The “je ne sais quoi” of TikTok","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Main problem with brexit was bubble. Even now, after so many years, you're saying that it's manipulation, simply because you don't personally know people who wanted it to happen. Who exist, I assure you. > What I mean is – well, take creationists. According to Gallup polls, about 46% of Americans are creationists. Not just in the sense of believing God helped guide evolution. I mean they think evolution is a vile atheist lie and God created humans exactly as they exist right now. That’s half the country. > And I don’t have a single one of those people in my social circle. It’s not because I’m deliberately avoiding them; I’m pretty live-and-let-live politically, I wouldn’t ostracize someone just for some weird beliefs. And yet, even though I probably know about a hundred fifty people, I am pretty confident that not one of them is creationist. Odds of this happening by chance? 1/2^150 = 1/10^45 = approximately the chance of picking a particular atom if you are randomly selecting among all the atoms on Earth. source: [LINK] ------- This being said, guarding my gps history like it's treasure does absolutely zilch to stop political manipulation, when you have stuff like corporate control over mainstream media and PR firms. See NYT's coverage of FTX, for a contemporary example. Or, well... [VIDEO]"},{"id":28628222,"author":"Fjolsvith","month":"2021-09","story":"Lesson of ivermectin: meta-analyses based on summary data alone are unreliable","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"news outlets follow a script to push a narrative","text":"This might be true except for the fact that news outlets follow a script to push a narrative (and CNN is not exempted from this): [VIDEO]"},{"id":27617227,"author":"dfxm12","month":"2021-06","story":"US ranks last among 46 countries in trust in media","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I guess it doesn't help when they run segments like this, sowing more distrust","text":"75% of those who identify as being on the right thought coverage of their views is unfair. This is interesting given how many local news outlets are owned by the conservative Sinclair Broadcast Group. I guess it doesn't help when they run segments like this, sowing more distrust: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23611246,"author":"cmroanirgo","month":"2020-06","story":"I Am Deleting the Blog","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Some examples of \"oligarch news\"","text":"And there's the rub. What is \"quality investigative reporting\" in an objective sense, when most of the MSM outlets are owned by oligarchs, or simply \"toe the line\"? In theory I would gladly support the theory of \"quality investigative reporting\", but the reality is a propagandist machine where opinion pieces replaces actual unbiased, adjective-free objective news. Some examples of \"oligarch news\": [LINK] [VIDEO] As a former developer who worked closely on Thomson Reuters News feed (in the 00's), I've seen how there is almost zero fact checking for the information that appears on news feeds. Instead, news outlets trust the 'upstream' feeds and then quote the reports verbatim. To be fair, there are those who are really awesome at doing research and releasing information that are part of the MSM. Unfortunately, there are plenty others who are not affiliated with MSM news outlets and hence aren't regarded as \"reporters\" per-se. These latter ones are regularly attacked via \"fact checking\" websites as a way to discredit them. An article on the latter is here: [LINK] In short, there's a bunch of information out there and without each and every news report clearly citing original sources, then MSM or not, it must be regarded as suspect. So for \"quality investigative reporting\", the actual reports must rigorously cite objective sources."},{"id":23111039,"author":"jonhohle","month":"2020-05","story":"2020 Pulitzer Prize Winners","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"* CBS has recently been caught fabricating lines at a drive through testing facility in Michigan * Nicholas Sandmann * “Experts generally agree that N95 masks have little value in the community.” * Local “news” stories that are effectively infomercials * [VIDEO] * allegedly CNN removing the Larry King episode from Google Play with a call from Tara Reade’s mother * implicitly or explicitly suggesting unrelated archive footage represents the story being reported (Italian hospital footage in a story about New York hospitals, firing range footage presented as foreign conflict) * the my pillow guy, who retooled to support health workers being railroaded simply for standing next to Trump * the sudden and nearly universal admiration for GWB * NYT editing an article, without notice that originally claimed HRC implied the Russians were grooming Tulsi Gabbard. I have a terrible memory for specific details, so those just the ones I can think of recently. I primarily listen to NPR in the car. Their bias is staggering. They present opinion as fact frequently, often misrepresenting parties in opposition."}],"r":[["pxBQLFLei70",1],["j7zzQpvoYcQ",1]]},"l4UgZBs7ZGo":{"wy":"HN points to it as a clear explainer of how analog TV and CRTs actually work, and as the entry point to the author's wider TV series.","q":{"x":"He actually demonstrates how the beam is redirected by the electro-magnets wrapped around the base of the tube. It's very cool.","i":40793547,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40793426,"author":"Sakos","month":"2024-06","story":"How Cathode Ray Tubes Work [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a clearer basic understanding of how CRTs work.","quote":"I feel like I have a much better basic understanding of CRTs now.","text":"Great video. Seeing what it looks like inside with the phosphor and the mask was so enlightening. I also recently watched the one by Technology Connections, which was quite good: [VIDEO] I feel like I have a much better basic understanding of CRTs now. They felt like utter magic before. Now it just feels a bit like magic. The only thing that still confuses me is how the electron gun itself actually works. I don't really get how electrons are being shot at all and why we can shoot them in a beam."},{"id":40793547,"author":"Sakos","month":"2024-06","story":"How Cathode Ray Tubes Work [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates how electromagnets redirect the CRT beam.","quote":"He actually demonstrates how the beam is redirected by the electro-magnets wrapped around the base of the tube. It's very cool.","text":"For this, I recommend the Technology Connections video [VIDEO] . He actually demonstrates how the beam is redirected by the electro-magnets wrapped around the base of the tube. It's very cool."},{"id":47639854,"author":"AlexDragusin","month":"2026-04","story":"Why Inventing Color TV Was So Difficult [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Playlist on TV topics from Technology Connections.","quote":"Alec Watson from Technology Connections did an entire series on this subject","text":"Alec Watson from Technology Connections did an entire series on this subject: (How Analog Color TV Works: The Beginnings) [LINK] Then the playlist on TV stuff: [VIDEO] Don't blame me for the rabbit hole :P"},{"id":27185469,"author":"msla","month":"2021-05","story":"Interlacing, Deinterlacing, and Everything in Between","stance":"reference","why":"Series explaining how analog TV works.","quote":"here's a video (part of a series) on how analog TV works","text":"> We hit a strange truth about analog monochrome television: the vertical resolution is strictly limited: it’s the number of lines you draw. But the horizontal resolution, the resolution within each line, is basically infinite; it’s a purely analog signal. The only thing that holds it back is how quickly the electronics in your television can keep up. No, it's strictly limited by the bandwidth of the television signal. You could have infinitely responsive circuitry but you'd still have horizontal resolution limited by the bandwidth of the video portion of the broadcast TV signal. About all you can say is that analog TV does not have pixels. Anyway, here's a video (part of a series) on how analog TV works: [VIDEO] Full playlist: [LINK]"}]},"n6h7fL22WCE":{"wy":"Cited in homelessness threads as an example of a private effort to build cheap shelter that LA shut down over code violations.","q":{"x":"someone did try building better-than-tents shelters, but LA shut him down for violating code.","i":28066979,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":18820719,"author":"sebringj","month":"2019-01","story":"Seattle’s Homeless Challenge","stance":"rec","why":"Man tried to help homeless with tiny homes.","quote":"I really liked this video -> regarding a man who tried to help (in LA) by making tiny homes.","text":"I completely missed the boat on that one with home prices. I guess that's why it's a tough issue. It's not something you can just answer without knowing a ton of variables which maybe is a task for AI. My bad. I really liked this video -> [VIDEO] regarding a man who tried to help (in LA) by making tiny homes."},{"id":28066979,"author":"paulddraper","month":"2021-08","story":"Woman sues L.A. after being struck by car on a street where tents block sidewalk","stance":"reference","why":"LA shut down someone building better-than-tent shelters.","quote":"someone did try building better-than-tents shelters, but LA shut him down for violating code.","text":"> What do you do with them while working? Well, someone did try building better-than-tents shelters, but LA shut him down for violating code. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27437022,"author":"kaliali","month":"2021-06","story":"How California Homelessness Became a Crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a link list on homelessness.","quote":"","text":"Skid Row, Explained - Johnny Harris [LINK] Evicted: Most Liberal Neighborhood Gives 300 Homeless COVID-19 Refugees the Bum's Rush [LINK] This Seattle homeless shelter is promoting rectal injections and free \"heroin pipes,\" all paid for with tax dollars | Not the Bee [LINK] Project Veritas: Central OAC exec admits orgs register thousands of homeless to vote at same address in Fulton Co. [LINK] This LA Musician Built $1200 Tiny Houses for the Homeless. Then the City Seized Them. [VIDEO] 585,000 homeless U.S. CITIZENS are ignored while ILLEGAL IMMIGRANTS in New York will collect $15,000 food stamps, free housing, and a job. Tom Cotton: New York’s Homeless Veterans Ineligible for $15.6K Payments to Illegal Aliens [LINK]"}]},"yRdzIs4FJJg":{"wy":"Cited in threads on motorcycle safety, Tesla's sensor choices and autonomous vehicles as a well-made explanation of the failure mode.","q":{"x":"There is a great argument for having ultrasonic sensors and radar","i":33404902,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33404902,"author":"L0stLink","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for radar and ultrasonic sensors via two fatal Autopilot crashes.","quote":"There is a great argument for having ultrasonic sensors and radar","text":"There is a great argument for having ultrasonic sensors and radar in a recent video by Rayan from FortNine discussing two fatal accidents involving tesla autopilot [VIDEO]"},{"id":40148680,"author":"kibitzor","month":"2024-04","story":"Tesla driver arrested for homicide after running over motorcyclist on Autopilot","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla vision may mistake close brake lights of motorcycles for distant cars.","quote":"vision used by Tesla seems to process motorcycles differently","text":"Related video \"Tesla Autopilot Crashes into Motorcycle Riders - Why?\"[0], summarized to: vision used by Tesla seems to process motorcycles differently, and may be incorrectly \"assuming\" the closer spaced brake lights on a motorcycle is actually a far away car. More details on the homicide here[1], which shows the crash happened during daylight hours and the bike resembles a sport bike. This is a different condition than my referenced video (night collisions with cruiser-style motorcycles), but I suspect similar incorrect assumptions by Tesla vision happened. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":37649790,"author":"ultrarunner","month":"2023-09","story":"California governor vetoes bill to require human operators in autonomous trucks","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Teslas hitting motorcyclists, well made.","quote":"FortNine also covers this in a characteristically well-made video","text":"I don't really have anything to back this up, so take it with a massive gain of salt, but I seem to keep seeing discussion of Teslas hitting bikes (both cyclists and motorcycles). Most of it centers around there being enough doubt about whether Autopilot was being used, or whether the driver was actually monitoring like they should have been, or whether the cyclist \"should\" have been in the road, etc, as to \"count\" as being less safe. I can only imagine that there are impassioned interpretations of the data going multiple directions. A Verge article [0] has some discussion on this with regard to a motorcyclist that follows this pattern, and FortNine also covers this in a characteristically well-made video [1]. There's also the problem of autonomous (electric) cars being too quiet when they first begin moving, but that's easy to solve. So I'm not entirely sure about their safety being \"proven\", but that's largely because the problem domain is enormous and the data somewhat open to interpretation. Overall \"proved\" seems too certain a word as of now, without narrowly defining the scope. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33281203,"author":"dharmab","month":"2022-10","story":"New motorcycle lighting design could save lives","stance":"reference","why":"Visualization of why Autopilot rear-ended motorcycles at night.","quote":"Thus video has a visualization","text":"It may be a contributing factor to recent cases where Tesla Autopilot rear ended motorcycles at night. Thus video has a visualization: [VIDEO] One of these crashes was right by my home, where I rode to work nearly every day prepandemic."}]},"yts2F44RqFw":{"wy":"Referenced to describe an argument style that rejects incremental improvements, and in discussions of differing liberal and conservative views of society.","q":{"x":"There's an interesting video essay on this exact style of argument, if you're interested.","i":27901330,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27901330,"author":"DoctorOW","month":"2021-07","story":"Man dies of a heart attack after minors swatted him over his rare Twitter handle","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay analyzing the rhetorical style of dismissing partial fixes.","quote":"There's an interesting video essay on this exact style of argument, if you're interested.","text":"Sure, but reducing bad things is helpful even if it isn't 100%. There's an interesting video essay on this exact style of argument, if you're interested. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44324916,"author":"Defletter","month":"2025-06","story":"What explains the liberal-conservative happiness gap?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains liberal versus conservative views of society's faults as fixable or fixed.","quote":"liberals see the faults in society as things that can be fixed, whereas conservatives see those faults as facts of life","text":"I think the issue ultimately comes down to what Ian Danskin described, paraphrased: that liberals see the faults in society as things that can be fixed, whereas conservatives see those faults as facts of life (reference: [VIDEO] ). Wouldn't you also be less happy if you viewed society's faults as fixable, where you rally, vote, and protest for them to be fixed, and yet they almost never are?"},{"id":26518775,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2021-03","story":"GitHub, fuck your name change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> reread their comment, apply context, it should be apparent. I did. It was not apparent; hence why I asked. > Why do you think Trump has anything to do with it? If we're still talking about \"it\" being a significant fraction of the US public being distrustful of the election process/results, then Trump's main relevance is the combination of: - His holding of the most powerful office in the world for four years - His direct attacks on the election process/results, e.g. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] - The attacks on the election process/results by other Repulican politicians, either using Trump's example to seize or cling-to power, or to curry Trump's favour, or to avoid Trump's wrath (either direct, or indirect like [LINK] ), e.g. [LINK] [LINK] - The attacks on the election process/results by members of the public, following Trump's example, e.g. [LINK] [LINK] > Really people couldn't care less about Trump. You mean, other than the 74,216,154 who voted for him? Many of whom are in no way casual about their support: [LINK] [LINK] > we have a divide in this country, mostly caused by dems refusal to accept and admit the right are people with opinions, ideas and feelings as well. Do you anything at all to back up such absurd partisan hyperbole? > The system is flawed regardless. Lol, what fatalistic nonsense. Reminds me of [VIDEO]"},{"id":28301273,"author":"hnbad","month":"2021-08","story":"Googlespeak – How Google limits thought about antitrust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Actually if I put it that way, it's just hating Mondays.","text":"Nah, this is just the ultimate conclusion of the Friedman doctrine: there is no morality, only legality. Unless you can conclusively prove that IBM US C-level executives knew about the Holocaust while it was happening, it was just business as usual which makes it okay by definition. Actually if I put it that way, it's just hating Mondays. [VIDEO]"}]},"1La4QzGeaaQ":{"wy":"HN doesn't recommend it for its content but uses it as a handy example of file size and bitrate for high-resolution video.","m":[{"id":46382490,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2025-12","story":"We replaced H.264 streaming with JPEG screenshots (and it worked better)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This video, in 8K on Youtube is 20Mbps","text":"Video Streaming has surprising little overlap with Video Codecs . Once you choose input/output options, then there's little to change about the codec. The vast majority of options available to ffmpeg aren't supported in the browser. Streamers don't have options for precisely the same reason OP doesn't have options - you are limited entirely into what the browser supports. I've built the exact pipeline OP has done - Video, over TCP, over Websockets, precisely because I had to deliver video to through a corporate firewall. Wolf, Moonlight and maybe even gstreamer just shows they didn't even try to understand what they were doing, and just threw every buzzword into an LLM. To give you some perspective 40Mbps is an incredible amount of bandwidth. Blu ray is 40mbps. This video, in 8K on Youtube is 20Mbps: [VIDEO] There's really no explanation for this."},{"id":23799336,"author":"exikyut","month":"2020-07","story":"Western Digital's 16TB and 18TB drives: EAMR HDDs enter the retail channel","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"So for (a random 8K video I found), storing 5 minutes 37 seconds requires about 8.5GB due to all the transcoding.","text":"> YT alone adds 500hrs of video per min . That is at minimum 9000 TB / day (500 18TB disks/day ...) ... assuming SD quality 220 mb per hour single format and one copy. Nitpick: Each bigger resolution stacks on top of the previous one, so 8K60fps means storing 8K60 (webm+av1), alongside 4K60 HDR (webm), 4K60 (webm+av1), 4K (webm), 1440p60 HDR (webm), 1440p60 (webm+av1), 1440p (webm), 1080p60 HDR (webm), 1080p60 (webm+h264+av1), 1080p (webm+h264), 720p60 HDR (webm), 720p60 (webm+h264+av1), 720p (webm+h264), 480p60 HDR (webm), 480p (webm+h264+av1), 360p60 HDR (webm), 360p (webm+h264+av1), 240p60 HDR (webm), 240p (webm+h264+av1), 144p60 HDR (webm), and 144p (webm+h264+av1). And as noted, a given resolution is often stored in multiple video formats, so there's that too. The data needed to store a single video thus adds up quickly: $ youtube-dl -F [VIDEO] | grep -o '[0-9\\.]\\+.iB' | sed 's/MiB/*(1024^2)/;s/GiB/*(1024^3)/;s/.*/(&)/' \\ | tr '\\n' + | sed 's/\\(.*\\)+$/scale=3;print(\\1)\\/(1024^3),\"G\\n\"\\n/' | bc -ql 8.423G So for [VIDEO] (a random 8K video I found), storing 5 minutes 37 seconds requires about 8.5GB due to all the transcoding. It's quite possible YouTube also keeps the original uploads around for a bit as well. --- It incidentally proved so complicated to correctly type out the format list above manually it was actually easier to figure out the following regex: $ youtube-dl -F [VIDEO] | sed -n '/video/{s/^.\\{35\\}//;s/ \\+[0-9]\\+k \\+, \\+/ (/;s/webm.*\\|vp9.*/webm/;s/avc.*/h264"},{"id":20321193,"author":"darkpuma","month":"2019-06","story":"NYC Mesh Begins Broadband Expansion in Brooklyn","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This 8K youtube video is 5m38s long and 850MiB large","text":"No way, 100Mbps is fast today for most people. This 8K youtube video is 5m38s long and 850MiB large: [VIDEO] Do the math on that and you need 21Mbps to stream that video without buffering, assuming a uniform bitrate: [LINK] 100Mbps down is fast for most people. What do you think the average user is doing that needs more than 100Mbps down?"}]},"25ZuKkbHdqM":{"wy":"Cited as a primary source for what Musk said about the Cybertruck's design goals and for its huge view count.","m":[{"id":38446728,"author":"PheonixPharts","month":"2023-11","story":"Cybertruck Is Already a Production Nightmare for Tesla","stance":"reference","why":"Musk mentions bulletproof design goal","quote":"Musk specifically mentions in this clip","text":"A WSJ article I read suggested, though they admitted it was only rumor, that Musk initially wanted the car to be bullet proof. I suspect in the end, since they're not advertising this \"feature\", the Cybertruck is likely more-resistant-than-average to bullets, but nothing that could be legally advertised. edit: Not a rumor after all, Musk specifically mentions in this clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38446980,"author":"PheonixPharts","month":"2023-11","story":"Cybertruck Is Already a Production Nightmare for Tesla","stance":"reference","why":"Musk says stainless steel doors are bulletproof","quote":"he specifically mentions the stainless steel at being used to make the doors bullet proof","text":"Turns out it's not a rumor, when Musk did a drive with Jay Leno in 2021 he specifically mentions the stainless steel at being used to make the doors bullet proof [0]. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36244042,"author":"Dig1t","month":"2023-06","story":"Tesla plans for 375,000 Cybertrucks per year","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Leno demoing the Cybertruck roll-up bed cover","quote":"You can see Jay Leno demo it in this video from 2021","text":">It looks like the bed is now always covered by a piece of glass It's not glass, it's a roll-up cover. You can see Jay Leno demo it in this video from 2021: [VIDEO] Bed covers protect your stuff from thieves and also decrease drag (increase range). They are a smart idea, especially for electric trucks."},{"id":29951935,"author":"mxschumacher","month":"2022-01","story":"Tesla Model 3 Owner Discovers Car Was Delivered Missing a Brake Pad","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of Musk's popularity via view count","quote":"The CNBC Cybertruck video has 32m+ hits, I invite you to go through the comment section","text":"on Twitter, Seeking Alpha, Youtube and the vast majority of traditional media reporting and in analyst reports of the large banks, I see overwhelming praise and support for Musk. It helps to weigh by views/clicks, I like Hacker News but assume that it's quite small in the greater scheme of publication platforms. The CNBC Cybertruck video has 32m+ hits, I invite you to go through the comment section [0] The stock price also tells a story about what most people think. I do concede that the tone has shifted recently, but I still don't feel that the full picture of Musk's deeds are known to most people. In my perception, he has achieved a religious status [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"3m5qxZm_JqM":{"wy":"Used almost entirely as a joke reference when HN threads involve accidents, failures or evasive official statements.","m":[{"id":21627197,"author":"michaelt","month":"2019-11","story":"Singapore issues first correction order under 'fake news' law","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What about parody that a reasonable person would recognise as such, but a small number of fools might not? For example [1] or [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":47256235,"author":"autoexec","month":"2026-03","story":"Picking Up a Zillion Pieces of Litter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm starting to suspect I might be cynical. I was pretty impressed at the \"1,000,000 cigarette butts that I removed from the environment\" but I couldn't help but think \"moved into what?\" which brought this ( [VIDEO] ) to mind: [Interviewer:] Into another environment…. [Senator Collins:] No, no, no. it’s been towed beyond the environment, it’s not in the environment [Interviewer:] Yeah, but from one environment to another environment. [Senator Collins:] No, it’s beyond the environment, it’s not in an environment. It has been towed beyond the environment. [Interviewer:] Well, what’s out there? [Senator Collins:] Nothing’s out there… Also, I couldn't help but wonder if he was removing trash at a faster rate than it was being added. Picking up litter is a good thing certainly, but we really need to get people to stop creating it in the first place. Even properly disposed of all that trash is a massive problem, but I'd love to see more effort getting people to clean up after themselves. A very long time ago I'd see PSAs with owls imploring us to \"Give a hoot\" and fake indians crying. Was that helpful? Does that kind of thing even exist today? Now that nobody watches TV are they pushed at kids on tiktok?"},{"id":42872379,"author":"esafak","month":"2025-01","story":"Waymo to test its autonomous driving technology in over 10 new cities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I stopped using Lyft after two close calls with drivers who must have faked their papers. One Lyft drive ran a red light into a postal truck at an otherwise empty intersection causing the front to fall off, like that Australian ship: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40661738,"author":"mucle6","month":"2024-06","story":"Waymo issues software and mapping recall after robotaxi crashes into a pole","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this skit","text":"My best guess is they're lying, or they model curbs to have a \"low damage score\" so its okay if the ai thinks theres a 10% chance a path will hit a curb. I'm excited for self driving cars, but I have reservations about a system that has to hard code \"don't hit a telephone pole\". It reminds me of this skit [VIDEO]"},{"id":39513465,"author":"PH95VuimJjqBqy","month":"2024-02","story":"Institutions try to preserve the problem to which they are the solution","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this video is just a recreation of it","text":"I'm with you on this one. consider this conversation actually happened, this video is just a recreation of it. Clarke and Dawe - The Front Fell Off [VIDEO] Not all of them are so extreme but the idea is the same."},{"id":38888294,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2024-01","story":"Alaska Airlines 737 Max makes emergency landing due to depressurization","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Would have to guess that there was a defect in this one though... \"The Front Fell Off\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":38910189,"author":"JohnKemeny","month":"2024-01","story":"What has a 1 in a million chance? (2010)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Chance in a million!","text":"Well, a wave hit it. A wave hit it? A wave hit the ship. Is that unusual? Oh yeah, at sea? Chance in a million! [VIDEO]"},{"id":39108447,"author":"bombcar","month":"2024-01","story":"Wheel of a Delta Boeing 757 flew off while it was preparing to take off at ATL","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's not quite \"the front fell off","text":"Various things related to it are common \"enough\" that you can find discussion around them - for example the Airbus sideways landing gear: [LINK] Of course, these are \"normal problems\" that shouldn't happen and need to be investigated, but it's not quite \"the front fell off\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":38736317,"author":"Eji1700","month":"2023-12","story":"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory","text":"Obligatory: [VIDEO] Otherwise I hope this isn't totally accurate. Not because i like musk/tesla, but because it'll be just another case where i'm galled by the fact they were even allowed on the road in this state, and the complete lack of safety standards and consequences."},{"id":37287199,"author":"resolutebat","month":"2023-08","story":"Ukraine uses Australian drones made of cardboard","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For non-Australian readers","text":"Yeah, not sure this one is even going to uphold the minimum crew requirements. For non-Australian readers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34965236,"author":"AdamJacobMuller","month":"2023-02","story":"Could deep boreholes solve our nuclear waste problem?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"no, not into another environment, beyond the environment.","text":"no, you see, they took the nuclear waste out of the environment. no, not into another environment, beyond the environment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34587423,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2023-01","story":"Steering wheel falls off a new Tesla Y while driving on a highway","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Well the front’s not supposed to fall off, for a start.","text":"[VIDEO] [Interviewer:] Well, if this wasn’t safe, why did it have 80,000 tonnes of oil on it? [Senator Collins:] Well, I’m not saying it wasn’t safe, it’s just perhaps not quite as safe as some of the other ones. [Interviewer:] Why? [Senator Collins:] Well, some of them are built so the front doesn’t fall off at all. [Interviewer:] Wasn’t this built so the front wouldn’t fall off? [Senator Collins:] Well, obviously not. [Interviewer:] “How do you know?” [Senator Collins:] Well, ‘cause the front fell off, and 20,000 tons of crude oil spilled into the sea, caught fire. It’s a bit of a give-away.” I would just like to make the point that that is not normal. [Interviewer:] Well, what sort of standards are these oil tankers built to? [Senator Collins:] Oh, very rigorous … maritime engineering standards. [Interviewer:] What sort of things? [Senator Collins:] Well the front’s not supposed to fall off, for a start."},{"id":33653262,"author":"mrweasel","month":"2022-11","story":"Twitter to employees: all office buildings closed, badge access suspended","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It does sort of feel like \"The front fell off\".","text":"- Umm they got to have a steering wheel, there's a minimum crew requirement. - What's the minimum crew? - Oh, one I suppose. It does sort of feel like \"The front fell off\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":31034449,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-04","story":"Moskva cruiser sank while being towed in a storm – Russian Defense Ministry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Someone should remake \"The front fell off\" video[1] but fitting the context","text":"Someone should remake \"The front fell off\" video[1] but fitting the context: A Russian reporter interviewing the Russian defense minister. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31200817,"author":"emmelaich","month":"2022-04","story":"The Twelve-Factor App (2011)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds of the \"Front fell off\" comic sketch.","text":"It's the part of 12 factor that makes me giggle a bit; like it how does it get into the environment? Reminds of the \"Front fell off\" comic sketch. \"No, we towed it out of the environment\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22360572,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2020-02","story":"'Ghost ship' washes up on Irish coast","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] [Interviewer:] So what do you do to protect the environment in cases like this? [Senator Collins:] Well, the ship was towed outside the environment. [Interviewer:] Into another environment…. [Senator Collins:] No, no, no. it’s been towed beyond the environment, it’s not in the environment [Interviewer:] Yeah, but from one environment to another environment. [Senator Collins:] No, it’s beyond the environment, it’s not in an environment. It has been towed beyond the environment. [Interviewer:] Well, what’s out there? [Senator Collins:] Nothing’s out there… [Interviewer:] Well there must be something out there [Senator Collins:] There is nothing out there… all there is …. is sea …and birds ….and fish [Interviewer:] And? [Senator Collins:] And 20,000 tons of crude oil [Interviewer:] And what else? [Senator Collins:] And a fire [Interviewer:] And anything else? [Senator Collins:] And the part of the ship that the front fell off, but there’s nothing else out there."},{"id":21357350,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2019-10","story":"قلب: a non-ASCII programming language written in Arabic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this classic video of Australian Senator Bob Collins discussing an environmental mishap","text":"I have run into this a few times when I've posted a link (where it seemed amusing and relevant to a discussion) to this classic video of Australian Senator Bob Collins discussing an environmental mishap: [VIDEO] Invariably, someone replies with a comment like \"People should be aware that this is not actually an Australian senator, it is the comedian John Clarke impersonating one.\" Every. Single. Time. As if it wouldn't be obvious after watching the first few minutes of the video! What ever happened to the fun of not realizing something is satire until you're partway into it?"},{"id":20571556,"author":"filoeleven","month":"2019-07","story":"On Costliest U.S. Warship Ever, Navy Can’t Get Munitions on Deck","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I really thought your comment was going to be a reference to this sketch.","text":"I really thought your comment was going to be a reference to this sketch. “The front fell off” is...not something one expects to be a realistic scenario. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19839309,"author":"FabHK","month":"2019-05","story":"Boeing Believed a 737 Max Warning Light Was Standard","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Does everyone know this delicious skit?","text":"Well, it's not built of cardboard and rubber... Does everyone know this delicious skit? \"Clarke and Dawe - The Front Fell Off\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":13460500,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2017-01","story":"Galaxy Note7: What We Discovered","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Oh well that's not very typical, I'd like to make that point...","text":"Oh well that's not very typical, I'd like to make that point... (reference: [VIDEO] ) Seriously, these findings are facepalm-worthy. It really doesn't inspire any confidence in their quality control."}]},"5AwdkGKmZ0I":{"wy":"Cited as the primary source for Apple's M1 performance claims, such as fastest CPU core and performance per watt.","q":{"x":"Here's the initial announcement video with a timestamp where they called it the \"worlds fastest CPU core\".","i":25484520,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34223137,"author":"SirGiggles","month":"2023-01","story":"What’s Left in the Apple Silicon Transition","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote source for M1 fastest CPU and performance per watt claims","quote":"","text":"But are you not implying it with original statement? Apple has publicly stated during their keynotes (albeit with slides that present minimal information) the efficiency their CPU holds over the aforementioned companies. And that isn't even going into the mobile space where Apple still holds the crown when it comes to performance on synthetic workloads. Revisiting the keynote where the M1 was introduced [1] at 8:48 Apple states that the M1 uses the world's fastest CPU, which ties back into your argument, but then at 9:51 talks about performance per watt. Apple does it again when the M2 was announced [2] at 58:21. This isn't to say your argument isn't a valid one, and I hope I didn't give the impression that it is wrong; the optics has, at least in my perspective, shifted towards preferring power efficiency over raw performance output. What with tech news of x86 CPUs and GPUs from Nvidia and AMD consuming more and more power the M1 and M2 sip power by comparison. No one doubts the performance of Apple devices, it's mainly efficiency by which it does so that has everyone paying attention to it. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":31130706,"author":"throwawaylinux","month":"2022-04","story":"What's new in CPUs since the 80s? (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation states 128kB cache","quote":"","text":"> That’s what Apple explicitly says. Why they don’t match with measurements is another matter… (and no, it’s not a typo) Ridiculous. You're just making things up. It clearly doesn't have a 160kB 6-way L1 dcache. Nor are the small cores 64kB 6-way -- those don't even divide that's ridiculous. Why do you say it's not a typo? They \"explicitly\" say it is 160kB in that backwater code comment. They also explicitly say it's 128kB in this presentation [VIDEO] so what makes your source the authoritative one?? Pretty obviously it's a quick cut and paste job with a typo (two in one line actually) in it. > ECC? Needs hugepages? Or hardware erratum? I don’t know for sure. ECC? Erratum? Come on. It's clearly because it's 128kB."},{"id":27448871,"author":"whynotminot","month":"2021-06","story":"Apple M1 support for TensorFlow 2.5 pluggable device API","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source showing claim was about low power silicon","quote":"Watch the accouncement video here","text":"> He's not wrong though. I own an M1 Macbook Air, and while there are some workloads that it can outperform my desktop at My point is that this isn't even an interesting conversation to have. Your desktop can outperform a chip that runs an iPad? Cool story bro. > they were forced to revoke their claim of having the \"fastest CPU cores\" after it was vehemently disproven. Their announcement explicitly said \"when it comes to low power silicon.\" So many of you insisted on missing this part it's practically a meme now. Watch the accouncement video here: [VIDEO] and scrub to 8:45 and actually listen to what is said. > laptops with dedicated graphics Why should they be forced to compare their integrated graphics to laptops with dedicated graphics? This is yet another uninteresting comparison. Of course a machine with some dedicated mobile 3080 is going to win the day in a head to head. But it's an absurd comparison because said machine will draw vastly more power and be heavier to boot. It's a different category of machine at that point. > I seriously worry for Apple if this is all they were able to get out of the 5nm node on ARM I think you're the only one who's worried. Apple very clearly laid out their design goals with the M1: supremacy at performance per watt. And the fanboys protested with \"I can build a faster desktop\" or \"my 4.5 lb laptop with dedicated graphics can get more FPS.\" It's just baffling. You've missed the whole point."},{"id":25484520,"author":"p1necone","month":"2020-12","story":"Ryzen 5800X vs. M1: Programming benchmarks","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Apple's worlds fastest CPU core claim","quote":"Here's the initial announcement video with a timestamp where they called it the \"worlds fastest CPU core\".","text":"Here's the initial announcement video with a timestamp where they called it the \"worlds fastest CPU core\". They've very quickly stopped using that as the tagline, but it was part of their initial marketing push. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25484522,"author":"p1necone","month":"2020-12","story":"Ryzen 5800X vs. M1: Programming benchmarks","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source for Apple's CPU core claim","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] \"Worlds fastest CPU core\" Of course it's more reasonable to compare it with other low power CPUs in laptops, and not desktop CPUs with 5x the TDP that cost as much as the mac mini before you even buy the rest of the PC, I agree with you on that - I'm just pointing out that people are making these comparisons because Apple themselves did it first."},{"id":25064291,"author":"mtmail","month":"2020-11","story":"No, the new Arm MacBook Air is not faster than 98% of PC laptops","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source for Apple's performance claim wording","quote":"The event video says LAST year","text":"Article says \"Apple officials literally claimed that the new MacBook Air using Apple’s custom M1 chip is faster than 98 percent of all PC laptops sold THIS year.\" (emphasis mine) The event video says LAST year: [VIDEO] \"I can tell you that I am 98 percent sure that any of the above laptops listed will wreck the MacBook Air doing any of the tasks I just named.\" ... those laptops listed got reviewed 2020 so I think they're 2020 models."}]},"5YGc4zOqozo":{"wy":"Used as the classic example of terrible airline customer service, and of how a viral video can force a company to respond.","q":{"x":"These guys did eventually get they money after the morning news shows featured thier song.","i":32196605,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32196605,"author":"sandworm101","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: PayPal dismissed my claim, saying I didn't provide evidence (yet I did)","stance":"reference","why":"Viral song got the musicians their money","quote":"These guys did eventually get they money after the morning news shows featured thier song.","text":"Take to social media. Plead your case to the masses. If you are cute enough (I suggest a puppy in the screenshot) it may go viral and get noticed by PayPal's marketing people. They have actual decision powers. That is how consumer service works these days. These guys did eventually get they money after the morning news shows featured thier song. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30218493,"author":"tlss","month":"2022-02","story":"Delta CEO wants U.S. to put unruly passengers on 'no-fly' list","stance":"reference","why":"Another example of terrible United behavior","quote":"See also \"United Breaks Guitars","text":"If you're talking about terrible behaviour from United employees, this is not an isolated case. See also \"United Breaks Guitars\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19881569,"author":"crooked-v","month":"2019-05","story":"Deadly stupidity during Aeroflot flight 1492 emergency evacuation","stance":"reference","why":"Airline destroying luggage","quote":"","text":"For me, it's less about waiting times and more about the omnipresent risk of the airline absolutely destroying my luggage ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":14080790,"author":"nbanks","month":"2017-04","story":"A man wouldn’t leave an overbooked United flight, so he was dragged off","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier viral song about United's bad service","quote":"It reminds me of the country singer who wrote \"United Breaks Guitars\" seven years ago","text":"From the doctor's perspective, refusing to leave was probably the best way to publicize United's bad customer service. It reminds me of the country singer who wrote \"United Breaks Guitars\" seven years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4986566,"author":"cturner","month":"2012-12","story":"WTF Google, you stole my $5","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a company mistreating a customer","quote":"Look at the way united airlines treated a passenger whos guitar they busted up","text":"Google, for a company that reputes to perform services, is probably the single most consumer- unfriendly company on the planet. They're really not. This is overstated. Try dealing with some kind of loss-making monopoly, where the people who work there don't care or may even have a perverse grudge against customers and want to cause them harm. Look at the way united airlines treated a passenger whos guitar they busted up: [VIDEO] There are companies who wage campaigns against their customers. They have a policy of not paying credit notes except under extreme pressure. But they might also have call centre staff with no job tracking system call you to chase you for your bill, where the staff don't even know whether you've paid it or not. Why? Because some stats somewhere said that bullied customers are more likely to pay. Google is flawed but has some endearing things going for it: the employees are intelligent people, they tend to be caring types."},{"id":36855111,"author":"crazytony","month":"2023-07","story":"40 years ago yesterday Air Canada Flight 143 ran out of fuel mid-flight","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's weird to me that they unloaded anything in the hold for what should have been a refuel-and-go. Unless they're expecting you to be held on the ground for 3+ hours, they typically don't even bother getting the pax out much less the bags. But there's always [VIDEO]"},{"id":15290139,"author":"nobodyorother","month":"2017-09","story":"Delta Is Flying into the Path of a Category 5 Hurricane Again","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hey, they're not United, which: - Breaks Guitars: [VIDEO] - Killed the World's Largest Rabbit: [LINK] - Forcibly Disembarks Doctors: [LINK] Delta's still shitty with their pricing, but they're clearly the lesser of those two evils."},{"id":14081457,"author":"abandonliberty","month":"2017-04","story":"A man wouldn’t leave an overbooked United flight, so he was dragged off","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"On the bright side, \"United breaks guitars\" is a really catchy tune.","text":"Based on my experience with United this is corporate culture, rather than 'one bad decision'. A culture that focuses on maximizing profits per transaction with little left for human dignity or compassion - let alone relationship building. On the bright side, \"United breaks guitars\" is a really catchy tune. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4379719,"author":"lancefisher","month":"2012-08","story":"United Airlines Lost My Friend's 10 Year Old Daughter And Didn't Care","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wow. This is one thousand times worse than breaking guitars. I would love to see that band make another video about this incident. [VIDEO]"}]},"6iCPIUGnHQ8":{"wy":"Linked for nostalgia, as an instant audio time-travel to the early instant messaging era.","q":{"x":"Instant audio time-travel just by reading a comment.","i":30963116,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30963116,"author":"novocantico","month":"2022-04","story":"Twitter accounts dropping “.eth” from usernames","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Instant audio time-travel just by reading a comment.","text":"> Also, ICQ would say uh-oh when you got new messages Wow, as soon as I read that, I heard it in my head, exactly as it was. Since I wasn't sure it was a false memory, I looked it up[1], and yeah, it was exactly the same, except in my head it was in a slightly lower voice. Amazing. Instant audio time-travel just by reading a comment. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11291804,"author":"striking","month":"2016-03","story":"Sources of ICQ desktop client by mail.ru","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Worms sound is slightly lower and has a slightly longer \"oh\", imo.[0][1] I don't remember there being an \"uh oh\" sound in Chip's Challenge (the death sound was \"bummer\" iirc)[2] 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK] (apologies for the video track, couldn't find anything better) 2: [LINK]"},{"id":7451332,"author":"medell","month":"2014-03","story":"After WhatsApp: An Insider’s View on What’s Next in Messaging","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"His point about country usage patterns even applies to the US and Canada. I remember in Canada when most of my friends were on ICQ (and the ubiquitious \"uh oh!\") and then the slow switch to MSN. Yet the majority of my American friends used AOL Instant Messenger. For the younger folk that have never heard the default ICQ notification sound: [VIDEO]"}]},"7Xad5Rl0N2E":{"wy":"HN commenters quote it jokingly whenever someone lists the unacknowledged benefits of an entity people love to complain about.","m":[{"id":38609102,"author":"antiviral","month":"2023-12","story":"How Did Christianity Change the Roman Empire?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also, there's this","text":"Your comment has merit. Even at it's peak Roman culture was marbled with cruelty and violence. During its decline, these aspects only got worse and became coupled with corruption and decadence. But I think we should see them relative to their historic peers (Gauls, Carthaginians, Parthians), not relative to modern societies to truly see what their contributions were, of which there were many, including in law, engineering, and medicine. Also, there's this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27616657,"author":"_Microft","month":"2021-06","story":"EE to reintroduce data roaming charges in Europe, following Brexit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds one a bit of \"What have the Romans ever done for us\" from Monty Python's Life of Brian, doesn't it?","text":"Reminds one a bit of \"What have the Romans ever done for us\" from Monty Python's Life of Brian, doesn't it? (Here is it for reference: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24204130,"author":"csours","month":"2020-08","story":"Facebook account now required to login to Oculus devices","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"'What did the Romans do for us? Besides the roads, clean water, ...'","text":"I think it's closer to 'What did the Romans do for us? Besides the roads, clean water, ...' [VIDEO]"}]},"8xRqXYsksFg":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joking reference in threads on estimation, technical debt and managing expectations.","m":[{"id":17602486,"author":"Jach","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: As a CTO, what is your most frustrating problem with technical debt?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"even if Scotty[0] sometimes has a point","text":"How are you measuring these things? 1. It's one of my dreams to work at a place using prediction markets for internal estimates. Anything else feels worthless and engineers are incentivized into the \"quadruple what you're currently thinking\" estimate so they more often meet deadlines -- or try to \"under-promise, over-deliver\" except management rightly catch on to that one immediately. (As an engineer I still want to be a La Forge and work with other La Forges, even if Scotty[0] sometimes has a point; modeling managers as demanding children has some gaping model gaps.) 2. What I've seen is that many delighters aren't that hard, but they're often treated as \"polish\" that goes into the p3/p4 priority backlogs and maybe might resurface if a customer ever brings up a desire for that to the product management. What delighters have you seen that weren't shipped because they were too hard? Were they actually too hard, or was it just too much to get them done by some release date alongside all the other stuff so they get shoved to the backlog just the same? 3. Are you measuring bug count based on what customers report or internal bug reports? If internal, even if people aren't gaming this directly, it's still easy to get into a bad cycle where reported bugs get ignored and go unfixed which leads to fewer reported bugs. That might be mistaken for higher quality. 4. Is this one measured from surveys? Obviously not all features are directly customer visible so let's take something concre"},{"id":32011591,"author":"Zenst","month":"2022-07","story":"Beigels, software, and why you should control queues instead of cycle times","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"managing expectations is an art best explained by Scotty","text":"Which reminds me of the old engineering trick of saying it will take longer than it does as managing expectations is an art best explained by Scotty - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23285548,"author":"dvtrn","month":"2020-05","story":"Back of the envelope estimation hacks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"watching an episode of Star Trek the Next Generation where his exact problem was manifested into reality by his favorite character","text":"My old man spent 30 years working as a mechanical engineer for General Electric in their gas turbine division and used to bring home these sorts of tales similar this with people who reported to him, which subsequently informed him on how he needed to communicate to the manager he reported to on when to expect deliverables. Most memorable from this was watching his reaction while he and I were watching an episode of Star Trek the Next Generation where his exact problem was manifested into reality by his favorite character : [VIDEO] Nonetheless, I may or may not have employed my dad’s and Scotty’s method now that I have a team reporting up to me, each with their own strengths and weaknesses in delivery. Grooming them has been a genuine pleasure, though."}]},"H6F0PgMcKWM":{"wy":"HN cites it as the primary source for Mullenweg's own statements during the WP Engine dispute, though some found him unprepared.","q":{"x":"Mullenweg has explicitly said in his video interview [0] that no other company is currently paying an 8% license fee.","i":41804716,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42659151,"author":"MM-hypocrite","month":"2025-01","story":"Aligning Automattic's Sponsored Contributions to WordPress","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Mullenweg's quote about using trademark law to encourage giving back.","quote":"There is a law that says you can’t violate the trademark, so that’s the law that we’re using to try to encourage them to give back.","text":"> Is there a law that says you have to give back? No. There is a law that says you can’t violate the trademark, so that’s the law that we’re using to try to encourage them to give back. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41804716,"author":"LegionMammal978","month":"2024-10","story":"Mullenweg has gone 'nuclear' against tech investing giant Silver Lake","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Mullenweg's claim that no other company pays an 8% license fee.","quote":"Mullenweg has explicitly said in his video interview [0] that no other company is currently paying an 8% license fee.","text":"As I understand it, Mullenweg has explicitly said in his video interview [0] that no other company is currently paying an 8% license fee. The WordPress Foundation trademark policy page [1] states (since Sep. 24) that its only commercial sublicensee is Newfold Digital, and Mullenweg mentioned this in the same interview, but I don't think that the sum that Newfold pays has been disclosed anywhere. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":41825393,"author":"shprd","month":"2024-10","story":"The ACF plugin on the WordPress directory has been taken over by WordPress.org","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as footnote showing Matt tried for years to reach an agreement.","quote":"","text":"It's not new, Matt has been trying to reach an agreement for years.[1] To be fair, he has done everything he can for the health of the ecosystem for all these years. WP Engine leech off the WordPress brand from head to toe. Literally, from trademark to infrastructure, while Automattic covers the bill for the most part. Of course, legally, WPE doesn't have to contribute beyond its mouth but if we are going down that route then also Automattic doesn't have to put up with funding WPE operation anymore. I'm tired of people justifying WPE attitude and behavior by saying \"legally, they don't have to contribute\", well let's talk legal, everything happening to them is within those same lines too. Why are you complaining if it's legal? This is one of those \"fuck around and find out\" situation. Matt just run out of fucks to give, and decided it's better to teach the bully a lesson even if it comes at a cost. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41667102,"author":"Maxious","month":"2024-09","story":"WordPress.org bans WP Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Mullenweg's interview offered on a Twitch stream","quote":"A couple of hours ago Matt dropped into a twitch stream and offered up an interview","text":"A couple of hours ago Matt dropped into a twitch stream and offered up an interview [VIDEO] > Matt [7:18]: \"They [wp engine] fired a incident report against me that said I berated them and cursed at them in their [wordcamp] booth now if you ask anyone who knows me I actually don't curse like I don't use curse words at all and they put information out there saying that I told them to f off you know which is not true and there were witnesses there\" > Matt [23:19]: \"um you know WP enginer is going to lose a lot of customers. Silver Lake stands to lose billions of dollars so they are going to pull out every dirty trick smear campaign Cambridge analytica stuff Palantir. They're going to try to attack and smear me and automattic wordpress. Working as much as possible so you know if you see terrible stuff about me coming out I don't know like just know that there's probably someone paying for that um that's that's one thing I'll say\""},{"id":41671276,"author":"runjake","month":"2024-09","story":"WordPress Drama: From the Sidelines","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Mullenweg's interview about the WordPress situation, though his stance is confusing.","quote":"Matt Mullenweg did a video segment with ThePrimeagen yesterday.","text":"Matt Mullenweg did a video segment with ThePrimeagen yesterday. I found Matt’s stance a bit confusing in areas but admit it may just be too early in the morning for me to process. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41676738,"author":"AlienRobot","month":"2024-09","story":"WordPress.org bans WP Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Mullenweg interview on WP Engine contributions; commenter found him unprepared.","quote":"Matt gave an interview to about it","text":"I'm referring to this comment [LINK] >It looks like people here are missing the context of the source of the issue between Matt and WP engine. Couple days ago he posted on X that wpengine has similar revenue to automattic, yet doesn’t contribute back to open source as much as they promised to (5 hour per week per employee or something like that). A wpengine employee replied to a post saying that management doesn’t allow them to contribute to Wordpress open source because it doesn’t align with KPI targets. That employee got fired the next day. That’s when Matt’s issue with wpengine escalated. If you check Wordpress' Youtube channel, they uploaded the Q&A [LINK] There is a part Matt talks about protecting employees who speak up about WP Engine. This was also cited in the cease and desist letter by WP Engine. I don't how much of it is true, but I don't think he would do all of this if there wasn't some truth to it. >at some point WP Engine will want a change in Core and they need the political capital to make that happen. Why would they need a change in Core? WP Core is already a very stable piece of software that supports half of the internet. It's extensible enough that you can add all \"changes\" you want through plugins. In fact that is another issue in all of this. WP engine says they \"contribute\" by creating plugins, while WP org insists that a contribution needs to be a contribution to WP core. Matt gave an interview to about it [VIDEO] and although he sounded very unprepar"},{"id":41701636,"author":"password4321","month":"2024-09","story":"On With Theo / T3.gg","stance":"reference","why":"Another interview with Matt about the WordPress situation.","quote":"Another interview: 27:00","text":"Another interview: [VIDEO] 27:00 \"Matt Talks About WordPress Situation\" by ThePrimeTime 2024-09-26"}]},"HAZP-RNSr0s":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that robotaxis can still have odd, avoidable crashes, usually in threads that criticize self-driving safety claims.","m":[{"id":40471397,"author":"xnx","month":"2024-05","story":"Feds add nine more incidents to Waymo robotaxi investigation","stance":"reference","why":"News report of a Waymo hitting a pole.","quote":"The incident where a Waymo vehicle ran into a pole","text":"The incident where a Waymo vehicle ran into a pole ( [VIDEO] ) is one of the most head scratching to me, but overall amazingly safe for operating 50,000 paid rides per week."},{"id":45812385,"author":"madamelic","month":"2025-11","story":"Tesla's ‘Robotaxis' Keep Crashing—Even With Human ‘Safety Monitors' Onboard","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Waymo isn't immune to \"wtf how\" kinds of crashes","text":"Waymo hits pole (this one is from 2024, could've sworn I saw one in the last few weeks but may have misremembered): [VIDEO] Waymos crash into each other: [LINK] Waymo cutting off bus at left turn: [LINK] Waymo doing... something... no clue how a pre-mapped car thinks this is ok: [LINK] Waymo sideswipes firetruck with its lights on: [LINK] --- Crashes happen. Tesla is currently having a rash of them but Waymo isn't immune to \"wtf how\" kinds of crashes even with all of its built-in advantages (far more sensors and having pre-mapping)."},{"id":41381992,"author":"ripjaygn","month":"2024-08","story":"Tesla drivers say new FSD update is repeatedly running red lights","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Waymo hits stationary pole.","text":"Meanwhile Waymo just yesterday went the wrong way on the street. [LINK] Waymo hits stationary pole. [VIDEO] If Waymo is as good as you claim, then why don't they release data on the number of manual interventions> It's just the Waymo's missteps are unlikely to show up on HN unlike Tesla's. FSD can navigate crowds too."},{"id":40516596,"author":"webel0","month":"2024-05","story":"How Waymo outlasted the competition and made robo-taxis a real business","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The recent light pole incident also doesn't help [1].","text":"Waymo is currently under investigation for multiple incidents, not all of which it had previously disclosed to the NHTSA [0]. The recent light pole incident also doesn't help [1]. If they are doing 50k rides a day, then they would appear to have a remarkable safety record. It will be interesting to see if these investigations lead to a repeat of the Cruise debacle or if this will become the price of doing business. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"KN0K58EfJSg":{"wy":"Cited mostly as a retro-tech curiosity about Microsoft's push into PC gaming, and sometimes as a joke.","q":{"x":"Bill Gates even appeared in the game in a DirectX promotional video.","i":33163782,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39551930,"author":"bombcar","month":"2024-02","story":"I keep making things out of checkboxes (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Bill Gates superimposed into Doom to promote Windows 95.","quote":"Different era.","text":"> While I still have you, did you know that Bill Gates once digitally superimposed himself into DOOM to promote Windows 95? No, no I hadn't. [VIDEO] Different era."},{"id":33163782,"author":"Comevius","month":"2022-10","story":"Windows 95 went the extra mile to ensure compatibility of SimCity","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Bill Gates inserted into Doom for a DirectX promo.","quote":"Bill Gates even appeared in the game in a DirectX promotional video.","text":"Microsoft even ported Doom to Windows 95, though it could also run the DOS version. Bill Gates even appeared in the game in a DirectX promotional video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24328129,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2020-08","story":"Tech my dad banned in our house when I was a kid","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bill Gates himself dressed up in a trench coat advocating to 'clean up the neighbourhood'","text":">You can go to reddit right now and find a video of some right wing whacko shooting someone to death on the front page. when I was growing up it wasn't just right-wing whackos glorying violence, it was Bill Gates himself dressed up in a trench coat advocating to 'clean up the neighbourhood' [VIDEO] You may be underestimating the weirdness of the internet and the culture around it in the 90s, reddit looks harmless compared to what I saw when I was young when kids on the schoolyard were talking about rotten com"},{"id":1300848,"author":"patio11","month":"2010-04","story":"Steve Ballmer selling Windows 1.0","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Windows 95 will revolutionize PC gaming","text":"Surely you can't mention this without the Bill Gates \"Windows 95 will revolutionize PC gaming\" video. [VIDEO]"}]},"MpmGXeAtWUw":{"wy":"Used as a joke or shorthand quote, 'the only winning move is not to play', in unrelated threads.","m":[{"id":40159808,"author":"robomartin","month":"2024-04","story":"Ask HN: How do I stop political SMS spam?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I ran an experiment years ago. I used a throw-away email along with plus-addressing [0] to register and receive updates from democrat, republican and a handful of media organizations. I knew what the results would be: Unmitigated mayhem. And that's exactly what I got. It was interesting to watch for a few months. All of these organizations shared (definitely) or sold (assumption) the customized email addresses far and wide. The only other possible explanations for what happened with these email addresses might be that they were stolen during a breach or that a bad actor with internal access shared or sold them. OK, I can buy either of these for one organization here or there, sure. When it happens across all organizations? No, the sharing or selling had to be intentional. There was no effective way to stop the stream of emails that resulted from a handful of registrations. From my perspective, clicking on \"unsubscribe\" only served to confirm the email address was good. In other words, perhaps it did unsubscribe you from that specific organization, yet, what you actually accomplished was to provide them with a validated \"live target\" email they could sell or provide to another organization. The result was an almost exponential growth of emails. Registering to, if I remember correctly, approximately six organizations led to a constant daily stream of emails form hundreds (did not count them) of groups. Crazy. I never set out to quantify any of this or run study, so I don't have"},{"id":33827963,"author":"rhaksw","month":"2022-12","story":"The distinction between free speech as a legal right and cultural value","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That’s why people opt for moderated communities, actually learning to handle content and ignore repugnant or vile content takes diligence and building that up is time-consuming, so they’ll happily pay money (via ads or directly) to offload filtering out that content. The catch here is that people are not aware that their content is frequently shadow moderated. Even if you do try to engage with vitriolic commentary, your comment may be removed without your knowledge, and the forum will still show you your comment as if it's not removed . It works like this on Reddit for all comments [1], but again as I said, all forums now engage in shadow moderation. And at the end of the day it is also an us problem, not just a them problem, because we're often the ones reporting the content that gets taken down. When people are made aware that this is happening, they do call for change, for example [2], > ...what is the supposed rationale for making you think a removed post is still live and visible? > ...So the mods delete comments, but have them still visible to the writer. How sinister. > ...what’s stunning is you get no notification and to you the comment still looks up. Which means mods can set whatever narrative they want without answering to anyone. Here's another way to think about whether or not to use shadow moderation. If you think it's a good idea to use it, then you think it's an effective tool for changing minds. Your ideological opponent, whose comments you want to secretly"},{"id":28515273,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-09","story":"Facebook Censored Me For Mentioning Open-Source Social Network Mastodon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The only winning move is not to play","text":"I'm sure that is a thing, but my friends are not that exciting. We are just privacy lunatics and old fuddy duddies that study the behavior of corporations, governments and others. We postulate what countermeasures could be enacted to minimize the sprawling intrusive nature of the internet into our lives. We always seem to reach the same conclusion as Joshua. \"The only winning move is not to play\" [1] [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26442351,"author":"8bitchemistry","month":"2021-03","story":"The internet didn’t kill counterculture – you just won’t find it on Instagram","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The only winning move is not to play","text":"> I hadn't thought of boycotting much of popular online space as being counter-cultural. It's an interesting thought. The only winning move is not to play: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25966025,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2021-01","story":"A Corner in Piggly Wiggly (1959)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The moral of the story is that when you beat Wall Street at their own game, they will change the rules on you so they win anyway [VIDEO]"}]},"Wn190b4EJWk":{"wy":"The speaker and others cite it in threads about malicious packages and typosquatting.","q":{"x":"I did a talk last year for OWASP AppSecEU that covers this kind of thing.","i":11364886,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":15256414,"author":"raesene6","month":"2017-09","story":"Malicious software libraries found in PyPI posing as well known libraries","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation on repository attacks and compromise in software package ecosystems.","quote":"I did a presentation on this topic for OWASP AppSecEU 2015","text":"This isn't, in any way, a new problem. I did a presentation on this topic for OWASP AppSecEU 2015 ( [VIDEO] ) and when doing the research for that I encountered cases of repo. attacks and compromise. IME the problem will continue unless the customers (e.g. companies making use of the libraries hosted) are willing to pay more for a service with higher levels of assurance. The budget required to implement additional security at scale is quite high, and probably not a good match with a free (at point of use) service."},{"id":11864796,"author":"raesene10","month":"2016-06","story":"Typosquatting programming language package managers","stance":"reference","why":"Compares curl|bash with package managers like rubygems and npm on security.","quote":"I actually did a talk last year for OWASP AppsecEU","text":"For me they're very similar. I actually did a talk last year for OWASP AppsecEU where I started with the curl|bash bit and pointed out where rubygems/npm etc aren't really a lot better in some ways [VIDEO]"},{"id":11364886,"author":"raesene3","month":"2016-03","story":"Vulnerability #319816 – npm fails to restrict the actions of malicious packages","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on risks of npm, rubygems, PyPI style package installation and repository compromise.","quote":"I did a talk last year for OWASP AppSecEU that covers this kind of thing.","text":"Kind of amusing that this is considered to need a new vuln. report, I kind of assumed it was common knowledge. Most of the programming language package repositories (e.g. npm, rubygems, PyPi, NuGet) have this kind of installation process and limited/no checks for malicious content. Also as there's no consistent use of package signing by the developer (it's either unsupported or not very used) there is also a risk of the repository itself being compromised. I did a talk last year for OWASP AppSecEU that covers this kind of thing. [VIDEO]"}]},"XAeEpbtHDPw":{"wy":"Mostly used as a joking or illustrative reference when threads cover yes-men, executive hubris and sycophancy.","q":{"x":"The show Silicon Valley had a great protrayal what surrounding yourself with yes-men can do to a person","i":21118923,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21118923,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2019-09","story":"WeWork says will file to withdraw IPO","stance":"reference","why":"Show clip depicting the danger of surrounding yourself with yes-men","quote":"The show Silicon Valley had a great protrayal what surrounding yourself with yes-men can do to a person","text":"The show Silicon Valley had a great protrayal what surrounding yourself with yes-men can do to a person The best part: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46494461,"author":"godelski","month":"2026-01","story":"AI Sycophancy Panic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That's so meta it applies to everything though. Fair... but I think you're also over generalizing. Think about how these models are trained. They are initially trained as text completion machines, right? Then to turn them to chatbots we optimize for human preferential output, given that there is no mathematical metric for \"output in the form of a conversation that's natural for humans\". The whole point of LLMs is to follow your instructions. That's how they're trained. An LLM will never laugh at your question, ignore it, or any thing that humans may naturally do unless they are explicitly trained for that response (e.g. safety[0]) So that's where the generalization of the more meta comment breaks down. Humans learning to converse aren't optimizing for for the preference of the person they're talking to. They don't just follow orders, and if we do we call them things like robots or NPCs. I go to a business advisor because of their expertise and because I have trust in them that they aren't going to butter me up. But if I go to buy a used car that salesman is going to try to get me. The way they do that may in fact be to make me think they aren't buttering me up. Are they being sycophantic? Possibly. There are \"yes men\". But generally I'd say no. Sycophancy is on the extreme end, despite many of its features being common and normal. The LLM is trained to be a \"yes man\" and will always be a \"yes man\". tldr: Denpok from Silicon Valley is a sycophant and his sycophancy leads to "},{"id":37472367,"author":"reducesuffering","month":"2023-09","story":"Elon Musk moving servers himself shows his ‘maniacal sense of urgency’ at X","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"also Gavin Belson was spot on","text":"\"The challenge is his personality and demeanor can turn on a dime going from excited to angry. Since it was hard to read what mood he might be in and what his reaction would be to any given thing, people quickly became afraid of being called into meetings or having to share negative news with him.\" -Esther Crawford [LINK] also Gavin Belson was spot on [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":36886113,"author":"reducesuffering","month":"2023-07","story":"Esther Crawford: “Now is a good time to open up a bit”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Every section about the CEO just makes me think of Gavin Belson from the Silicon Valley series, surrounding himself with \"yes men\" and firing people who make him feel like he might be a little wrong. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":30209522,"author":"thefourthchime","month":"2022-02","story":"Apple's privacy push cost Meta $10B","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this clip from Silicon Valley.","text":"I couldn't agree with you more here. To me, it's a clear sign that he \"wears no clothes\". The Metaverse presentation was the most idiotic presentation I've ever seen. He must be completely surrounded himself by people that just agree with him. It reminds me of this clip from Silicon Valley. [VIDEO]"}]},"YR5ApYxkU-U":{"wy":"Mostly used as a cultural reference or joke in threads about schools, students and surveillance.","m":[{"id":35511421,"author":"latexr","month":"2023-04","story":"Ever-expanding animation of the life of the 796th floor of a space station","stance":"reference","why":"Music video's meat grinder scene referenced for the teacher spanking a student.","quote":"for that one it’s the teacher spanking the student.","text":"You mean the meat grinder[1]? You can click stuff for their reference, for that one it’s the teacher spanking the student. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16247296,"author":"jdright","month":"2018-01","story":"An Italian Song That Sounds Like English But Is Nonsense","stance":"reference","why":"Song that sounded similar to the nonsense Italian song.","quote":"my wife thought I was listening to Pink Floyd's Another Brick In The Wall!","text":"Interesting enough, for a non-english (e.g. I and my wife) speaker, my wife thought I was listening to Pink Floyd's Another Brick In The Wall! At first I couldn't connect the two, but then I \"changed\" my mind to my native language and I can see a lot of similarities in the lyrics too. This was a really weird moment... :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35975961,"author":"CPLX","month":"2023-05","story":"Students are increasingly refusing to go to school","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm here to tell you that in 1989 we really didn't have any trouble with critical thinking on the topic of going to high school on any given Wednesday. Guided by key 20th century American[0] and British[1] thought leaders we were developing and refining these theories of \"immutable necessity\" in suburban cars and convenience store parking lots on a regular basis. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21881880,"author":"15__characters","month":"2019-12","story":"Colleges are turning students’ phones into surveillance machines","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Show me someone who seeking power over others and I will show you someone who themselves is a slave to someone or something. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3712204,"author":"chefsurfing","month":"2012-03","story":"Kevin Rose Will Join Google","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I am certain a world full of autodidacts would supersed the university system we see today. For these autodidacts the universe might truly become a \"University of Life\". I am also certain that the true barriers keeping most people from becoming autodidacts are not financial or social but rather built by the schooling system into our minds. This schooling system itself was probably setup to create obedient soldiers and weapons technicians so as to win wars. Can someone please queue Pink Floyd's \"Another Brick In The Wall\" [1]? [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"_2GT2PCUN3Q":{"wy":"HN shares it as a relatable, funny song when threads turn to the struggle of finishing side projects.","q":{"x":"a rap song about the woes of actually finishing a side project","i":19390149,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19390149,"author":"inoda","month":"2019-03","story":"Getting too absorbed in side projects","stance":"reference","why":"Rap song about the struggle of finishing side projects","quote":"a rap song about the woes of actually finishing a side project","text":"This is too fitting for this thread...a rap song about the woes of actually finishing a side project: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19235631,"author":"stdoutrap","month":"2019-02","story":"Show HN: A rap song about the struggles of finishing a side project","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Looks like the url isn't clickable (in similarly formatted previous posts, it has been...) Clickable url: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26662980,"author":"kubanczyk","month":"2021-04","story":"On getting poetry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] I've finally finished, Go to Hacker News and put up a post But that shit had zero upvotes Somebody left a comment Telling me my home page ain't responsive Who that?"}]},"gCMzjJjuxQI":{"wy":"HN cites it as a historical example of corporations using doctors and misleading advertising, drawing analogies to modern industries.","m":[{"id":30004555,"author":"soheil","month":"2022-01","story":"When cigarette companies used doctors to push smoking (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Easy to look back and make fun of those commercials","text":"Easy to look back and make fun of those commercials [0]. It's more sad than anything else if someone does that since they'd be applying their current knowledge of harms of tobacco to people who didn't have said knowledge. This is a cognitive bias called the Curse of knowledge [1]. I see so much of this happening not just on Youtube comments, but even here on HN, it's almost disturbing. The fair thing to wonder about is what things are we doing today that will seem ridiculous and obviously harmful to people in 100 years from now. Staring at a bright flat screen hours a day just to interact with a random stranger who vehemently disagrees with you about petty subjects? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29383914,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2021-11","story":"I resigned from Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"More doctors smoke Camel Cigarettes\".","text":"> You can find misinformation of all levels of sophistication, all the way to incorrect scientific papers if thats your shtick. This isn't new at all. \"More doctors smoke Camel Cigarettes\". [VIDEO] And the Tobacco industry funded all types of scientific papers, that were published all over the place to demonstrate Tobacco's \"health benefits\". ------- The only reason we can't find examples older than maybe the mid 1900s or so, was because doctors weren't even trusted until the 1900s. 1800s doctors were well recognized as quacks and had very little reputation among the American people. So there was no reason to use doctors as a propaganda point back then."},{"id":38771397,"author":"dangus","month":"2023-12","story":"Gas utilities used tobacco tactics to avoid gas stove regulations","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"And what about their kids that didn’t choose to get asthma? And what about the adults who wouldn’t have chosen a gas stove if they had balanced and factual information rather than information tainted by corporate influence? [VIDEO]"}]},"h2OfQdYrHRs":{"wy":"HN brings it up as an early, impressive augmented-reality translation demo and a wow moment before modern AI.","q":{"x":"2010, seeing the demo video of Word Lens","i":48421992,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48421992,"author":"ben_w","month":"2026-06","story":"What was your \"Oh Shit\" moment with GenAI?","stance":"reference","why":"Word Lens demo as an early wow moment","quote":"2010, seeing the demo video of Word Lens","text":"I had a lot of such moments, including: • Most recent, I had the option of either buying an app from the app store to train myself on the piano, or vibe coding a web app to connect with an attached MIDI keyboard and accept an uploaded MIDI file and give me an experience like Guitar Hero, and Claude did this in two prompts of their free (not subscription) version, where the second prompt was just the word \"continue\". • First demo of InstructGPT (predecessor to ChatGPT), because I remember how much worse the state of the art in NLP had been, and because I hadn't expected instruction following from the quality of continuation seen in GPT-3.x • 2019, \"This Person Does Not Exist\" • 2016, seeing style transfer and similar working ( [LINK] ) and what would now be called Deep Fakes (back when Two Minute Papers videos were <2 minutes long: [LINK] ) • 2015, when I (in retrospect, foolishly) believed Tesla about their over-the-air software update that introduced self-driving: [LINK] • 2013, word2vec, \"man\" - \"woman\" ~= \"king\" - \"queen\", again because of knowing how bad the state of the art in NLP has been (If you're wondering why \"uh oh\" from that, consider value in automating propaganda, and surveillance opportunities for automating comprehension of slang/cants like Polari). • 2010, seeing the demo video of Word Lens: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15602763,"author":"amelius","month":"2017-11","story":"WhatTheFont – Shazam for Fonts","stance":"reference","why":"App translating signs in real time with edited image","quote":"it will identify the font, translate the sign to a different language, and edit the image with the translated text.","text":"If you want to take it a step further: there's an app ([1]) that allows you to aim your smartphone camera at a sign, and it will identify the font, translate the sign to a different language, and edit the image with the translated text. All in real time. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8801784,"author":"otaviogood","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: How do you stay motivated to work on side projects?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Don't be afraid to drop a project. I tend to have a handful of projects going at any one time. A good project will stand out and it will pull you through to completion. Pretty much all of my significant jobs were spun out of my side projects. My current job started as a side project that I prototyped in a few weeks. A company was built around it and now our company is part of Google. This. [VIDEO] Take note of things that inspire you. For me, books or other people's projects. Show your work to your friends and try to convince your friends to work on projects with you. Find programming languages / environments that make it easy to spin up a simple project without too much overhead. Start small. Last I counted, I have about 140 folders in my dev directory. Each one has a small project I started up. One turned into a successful company. Another won a DARPA challenge. A few others were released in various forms. But most got dropped because they just weren't as cool as something else. And I think dropping projects isn't just ok, it's great fun and will help you find that project that's worth completing."},{"id":3619217,"author":"joezydeco","month":"2012-02","story":"Google to Sell Heads-Up Display Glasses by Year’s End","stance":"reference","why":"Word Lens does real-time translation overlays on an iPhone","quote":"Word Lens seems to be doing it just fine on an iPhone","text":"\"Real-time overlays are still impractical, right?\" Word Lens seems to be doing it just fine on an iPhone: [VIDEO] Seriously, foreign language translation would be a natural for a product like this."}]},"lay8aZlsbB0":{"wy":"Used as a vivid example of chaotic roundabout traffic when discussing road design and driving in Europe.","m":[{"id":10736854,"author":"Swizec","month":"2015-12","story":"The High-Stakes Race to Rid the World of Human Drivers","stance":"reference","why":"European traffic as a hard test for autonomous cars.","quote":"","text":"You do realize there are countries that have a significant chance of snow for 4+ months per year, right? And that there are many places with stop signs that are almost completely obscured by foliage, or simply by the angle of roads on the crossroads. Not everything is California. And not every city has a grid. When autonomous cars can survive in a place like Rome or Paris[1], or even downtown Manhattan. Then we can talk about fully replacing humans. [1] Rome and Paris are notorious for people having special city cars because they get banged up so much in the course of normal life Here's an example of traffic in a modern European city: [LINK] And another: [VIDEO] How long until autonomous cars can do that? Hell, I 'd shit my pants trying to do that. PS: in the real world there are also many roads that flat out don't have lanes drawn on them."},{"id":4597519,"author":"blaze33","month":"2012-10","story":"To Encourage Biking, Cities Lose the Helmets","stance":"reference","why":"Cycling through Paris's busiest roundabout.","quote":"but it's funny to do it.","text":"As a daily Vélib' user from day one, I'll add some insights a one-time user may have missed: * It's often the fastest way to get to another point less than ~5km away. * You quickly notice how most of the traffic regulation is only designed for cars. * You have a much better visibility of the traffic (with a correctly adjusted saddle) than a car driver. * Maneuverability and standing start acceleration (the first seconds) are also way above the average car, so if you're used to it, you'll easily make your way in the congested traffic. * It's common practice to run red lights or stop signs (hence the first point). There's some debate about allowing the \"turn right\" but we're still waiting. Still, we're not crazy but you quickly notice it makes no sense at all to stop & start a 20kg bike every 100 meters at a pedestrian crossing with obviously no pedestrians waiting or at road junction with no incoming traffic. * Most bike lanes are overrun by clueless pedestrians. * European cities like Paris predate cars and are more dense with smaller streets which feel much safer than higway-like american streets. Crossing the place de l'étoile ain't for every vélib user - [VIDEO] - but it's funny to do it."},{"id":1438337,"author":"_delirium","month":"2010-06","story":"Nifty Freeway Interchange Design","stance":"reference","why":"Chaotic multi-lane traffic around the Arc de Triomphe.","quote":"edit: video of an example, around the Arc de Triomphe ->","text":"I have a sort of visceral negative reaction to roundabouts, but I think because most of the ones I've seen in Europe, when the road is more than one lane wide in each direction, seem to end up with a crazy ad-hoc-lanes thing going on in them, where it's as if you drove onto a huge plaza with no lane markings, where everyone is driving in all sorts of criss-crossing diagonal directions trying to get from their entrance road to their exit road across multiple lanes of traffic. I imagine some countries have more orderly versions, but that's how the Paris ones all felt to me. edit: video of an example, around the Arc de Triomphe -> [VIDEO]"}]},"maAFcEU6atk":{"wy":"HN mostly drops it as a joke or reference in threads about odd computer interfaces and AI tools, and one commenter points to this upload for its better quality.","m":[{"id":25933766,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-01","story":"Automating my job with GPT-3","stance":"reference","why":"Better quality upload of Celery Man","quote":"the quality on this one is much better, for some reason","text":"Even though that one appears to be on an official channel of theirs, the quality on this one is much better, for some reason: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37738734,"author":"orng","month":"2023-10","story":"Break-a-Scene: Extracting Multiple Concepts from a Single Image","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Give me a printout of Oyster smiling\" - [VIDEO] Life imitating art imitating life. Exciting times indeed."},{"id":14702547,"author":"pattisapu","month":"2017-07","story":"It is as if you were doing work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Vaguely cognate with the Tim & Eric Awesome Show Good Job! Celery Man / Cinco products type skits [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"om7O0MFkmpw":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to link, often jokingly, whenever someone writes \"could care less\" instead of \"couldn't care less\".","q":{"x":"David Mitchell does an excellent job of explaining \"I couldn't care less","i":2360073,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":9638845,"author":"shabble","month":"2015-06","story":"Dear Paul","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the classic \"Could care less\"[2]","text":"In the simplest case, the thing referred to is 'a think', and they're entirely equivalent :) I'd argue that this is more of an Eggcorn[1] than a malapropism, unlike your second example. \"The proof is in the pudding\", or the classic \"Could care less\"[2] are the two worst offenders I can think of immediately. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8890318,"author":"markdown","month":"2015-01","story":"I Owe It All to Community College","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"What I mostly get riled up about are words or phrases that are just plain wrong","text":"I think we agree with each other. Words like \"ain't\" entering the common lexicon (usually in spoken english) is fine by me ie. Words formed out of contractions of other words/phrases, or entirely new inventions. What I mostly get riled up about are words or phrases that are just plain wrong, but that have been used so widely that some dictionaries have accepted them as correct. Eg: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4581253,"author":"SideburnsOfDoom","month":"2012-09","story":"The Britishisation of American English","stance":"reference","why":"Comic explanation of why 'could care less' means the opposite.","quote":"as David Mitchell explains at comic length","text":"But \"I could care less\" is a perfectly grammatical sentence, which has a semantic meaning, which, as David Mitchell explains at comic length, is the opposite of what the speaker means to say. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2360073,"author":"RiderOfGiraffes","month":"2011-03","story":"From Redmond with Love - IE team sends cake for FF4","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why 'could care less' is wrong.","quote":"David Mitchell does an excellent job of explaining \"I couldn't care less","text":"> I could care less, ... I don't want to be \"that guy\" and I'm not trying to be a grammar Nazi. I know this is now an idiom in the USA, and therefore it doesn't have to make sense. However ... I've now heard it said, in four different countries, that this phrase makes the speaker sound like an idiot. I know it's now just \"the norm\" in the USA, but I wanted to let people here know that saying this makes a bad impression. If you don't care how you sound to non USAians then don't bother. But if you're wondering what I'm talking about, David Mitchell does an excellent job of explaining \"I couldn't care less\" [VIDEO] I now return you to your normal programming."},{"id":43702087,"author":"switch007","month":"2025-04","story":"A Ford executive who kept score of colleagues' verbal flubs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Let David Mitchell explain, in a totally non sarcastic way","text":"I \"could\" care less about verbal flubs ;) Let David Mitchell explain, in a totally non sarcastic way [VIDEO]"},{"id":39572349,"author":"wlll","month":"2024-03","story":"WhatsApp forces Pegasus spyware maker to share its secret code","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The issue is that \"could care less\" means the opposite of what people intend","text":"> The majority of the world doesn't speak English And yet here we are. To paraphrase David Mitchell ( [VIDEO] ), the problem is not so much the prevelance of American English, which in a lot of situations makes sense. eg. \"sidewalk\" makes a lot of sense, perhaps more, than \"pavement\" for the place that a pedestrian walks at the side of a road. \"Parking lot\" for a lot of land that is reserved for parking etc. The issue is that \"could care less\" means the opposite of what people intend them to mean, and they're just expecting the people listening to interpret what they mean."},{"id":31968630,"author":"FooBarBizBazz","month":"2022-07","story":"Jordan Peterson Suspended from Twitter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think David Mitchell has a thing about this?","text":"Well, he is saying that he cares about it. Accidentally, by mangling an idiom, but still. I think David Mitchell has a thing about this? [VIDEO]"},{"id":24918024,"author":"_iyig","month":"2020-10","story":"Xbox creator Seamus Blackley baked a 4,500-year-old Egyptian sourdough","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"as comedian David Mitchell explains","text":"It's also a nonsensical expression which some English speakers (rather good-naturedly) find bothersome, as comedian David Mitchell explains: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14310796,"author":"elle4096","month":"2017-05","story":"Microsoft Build 2017 [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I could care less\" is probably not what you meant.","text":"FYI \"I could care less\" is probably not what you meant. It's \"couldn't care less\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13867874,"author":"jv22222","month":"2017-03","story":"JSON Web Tokens should be avoided","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dear Americans, the queen would like you to stop saying \"could care less","text":"On another note. Dear Americans, the queen would like you to stop saying \"could care less\" - by David Mitchell of Peep Show [VIDEO]"},{"id":3675204,"author":"kiloaper","month":"2012-03","story":"“Disappointing” iPad 3 speculation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I assume you mean ' couldn't care less'.","text":">Meanwhile, 50 million people could care less what's inside an iPad. I assume you mean ' couldn't care less'. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3210178,"author":"hopescope","month":"2011-11","story":"Nimbus.io: Open-source alternative to Amazon S3","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"David Mitchell explains why this phrase makes no sense","text":"\"I could care less if this is open source\" David Mitchell explains why this phrase makes no sense and means exactly that opposite of what you want to say: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2214408,"author":"auxbuss","month":"2011-02","story":"American Kids Aren't Getting Dumber; They Were Just Never That Smart","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's \"couldn't care less\".","text":"It's \"couldn't care less\". When you say \"could care less\" you are endorsing the claim of American Kids Getting Dumber. If you still don't get it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1416450,"author":"dtf","month":"2010-06","story":"Apple's \"evil/genius\" plan to punk the web and gild the iPad","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"David Mitchell discusses this curious form of expression with graphs and what-not","text":"Aside from Apple vs Google, David Mitchell discusses this curious form of expression with graphs and what-not: [VIDEO]"}]},"qp75-46PnMY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the video version of the research on the social cost of driving when arguing cars impose uncompensated costs on others.","q":{"x":"each mile you drive in your car incurs costs on the rest of society that nobody directly pays","i":42103050,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46139170,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2025-12","story":"Accepting US car standards would risk European lives","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the externalities of driving versus cycling and walking.","quote":"which I heard about from a CityNerd video","text":"> TLDR, UK road users pay for far more than the road network. Right, but driving has far more externalities than just the cost of the roads. For example: > Results suggest that each kilometer driven by car incurs an external cost of €0.11, while cycling and walking represent benefits of €0.18 and €0.37 per kilometer. Extrapolated to the total number of passenger kilometers driven, cycled or walked in the European Union, the cost of automobility is about €500 billion per year. Due to positive health effects, cycling is an external benefit worth €24 billion per year and walking €66 billion per year. From \"The Social Cost of Automobility, Cycling and Walking in the European Union\", [LINK] (which I heard about from a CityNerd video [VIDEO] )"},{"id":42103050,"author":"dangus","month":"2024-11","story":"California's gas prices to increase 65 cents per gallon with new fuel standards","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that driving imposes uncompensated costs on society.","quote":"each mile you drive in your car incurs costs on the rest of society that nobody directly pays","text":"I think about this the other way around. The federal government and other states have artificially low gas taxes that are a direct subsidy of gasoline car dependence. A state like California imposing a tax like this makes them one of the few states that's actually aware how much the consumption of a gallon of gas costs society and manages to charge someone for it. - The federal gas tax has been 18.4 cents per gallon since 1993, and has not increased to account for inflation - Vehicles are more fuel efficient than in the past, which has reduced the demand for gas by the gallon on an individual basis. - The cost of infrastructure materials has increased (e.g., building new roads and bridges) There is a very decent argument to be had that nobody is actually paying the societal cost of driving, that all of society just loses value by driving. In other words, each mile you drive in your car incurs costs on the rest of society that nobody directly pays, and therefore everyone pays. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39213111,"author":"eesmith","month":"2024-02","story":"What the world can learn from Copenhagen's cycling revolution","stance":"reference","why":"Video version of the paper on the social cost of automobility.","quote":"That paper has a YouTube version on the CityNerd channel","text":"I think the mentioned report is \"Transport transitions in Copenhagen: Comparing the cost of cars and bicycles\" at [LINK] Along these lines, in a less scholarly form: \"Cost-benefit of cycling infrastructure\" - [LINK] \"Making the Economic Case for Cycling\" - [LINK] A related paper is \"The social cost of automobility, cycling and walking in the European Union\", [LINK] That paper has a YouTube version on the CityNerd channel, \"Cars Are A Disaster For Society -- Here Are the Numbers \" [VIDEO]"}]},"sdvzYtgmIjs":{"wy":"Cited as a primary source for what Cook, Ive and Powell Jobs actually said about Apple's car, advertising and succession.","q":{"x":"I guess people can make up their own mind after watching the interview.","i":33548961,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45942952,"author":"gyomu","month":"2025-11","story":"Report: Tim Cook could step down as Apple CEO 'as soon as next year'","stance":"reference","why":"Laurene Powell Jobs alludes to Cook's succession.","quote":"she alludes to it in this video","text":"I've heard it from Laurene on several occasions, she alludes to it in this video [VIDEO] (in case the link goes down: Tim Cook, Sir Jony Ive KBE, and Laurene Powell Jobs, Code 2022 Interview with Kara Swisher)"},{"id":33548961,"author":"ksec","month":"2022-11","story":"How the Apple archive ended up at Stanford","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Cook, Ive and Laurene Powell Jobs on Apple history.","quote":"I guess people can make up their own mind after watching the interview.","text":"Exported to PDF in case this get taken down by Apple. >But then I read the New York Times article about Laurene Jobs starting the Steve Jobs Archive. Here’s what that article said. >When Mr. Jobs returned to Apple in 1997, a dozen years after he was forced out, one of the first things he did was offer Stanford University the company’s corporate archives, said Henry Lowood, the curator of Stanford libraries’ History of Science and Technologies Collections. “Stanford had a signed document from Apple’s legal department within 24 hours, allowing it to transport some 800 boxes from the company’s campus to the university.” This story wasn't made up by her. It was reported, may be misreporting, may be Apple's Submarine PR for years. And she kept the story going. ( So to speak ) And then this interview [1] with Jony, Tim Cook and Laurene Jobs. I guess people can make up their own mind after watching the interview. But if anything a lot of what was being said by Tim Cook and Laurene Jobs was repeating what Steve had said publicly . Intentionally or not. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33073115,"author":"timmytokyo","month":"2022-10","story":"Apple’s ad business set to boom on the back of its own anti-tracking crackdown","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Cook's answer on digital advertising and privacy.","quote":"Digital advertising is not a bad thing.","text":"Tim Cook was asked about advertising and privacy a couple weeks ago. Here's his answer. Take from it what you will. \"Digital advertising is not a bad thing. We've never said digital advertising is a bad thing. What is not good is vacuuming up people's data when they're not doing so on an informed basis. That's what is bad. And so we try to put the user in the driver seat there to own the data.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33003913,"author":"victor106","month":"2022-09","story":"Apple Car: Bad Idea After All","stance":"reference","why":"Jony Ive asked about car UI.","quote":"it feels like Apple is working on a car","text":"Watching the code interview with Jonny Ive, the part where Kara asks him about UI in cars, it feels like Apple is working on a car. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39783561,"author":"catlikesshrimp","month":"2024-03","story":"U.S. sues Apple, accusing it of maintaining an iPhone monopoly","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tim Cook telling someone he has to buy her mom an iphone","text":"TLDR: It has nothing to do with MMS But that didn't address GP's comment. Apple states that green bubbles are pariahs because messages can't be sent to androids so it breaks the system, or something like that [BS] Iphone users think that green bubbles are pariahs because they aren't part of their exclusive group, and because green bubbles turn chat groups into rubbish, because yada yada not iphone. (spoiler alert, apple does it on purpose) [LINK] For laughs: Tim Cook telling someone he has to buy her mom an iphone (1hr 0mins 17secs) [VIDEO]"}]},"z5e7kWSHWTg":{"wy":"Mostly cited for its side-by-side rendering demo in Ember and React performance debates, not as a general recommendation.","m":[{"id":23017441,"author":"azangru","month":"2020-04","story":"When does React re-render? Explaining rerenders and how to optimize performance","stance":"reference","why":"Compared React performance to Ember and Angular 1","quote":"Back in 2015, in relative terms, React seemed fast !","text":"> This is wrong. Naive React code is rarely fast in relative terms. Do you remember Ryan Florence's talk Hype at ReactConf 2015, where he compared React performance to that of Ember and Angular 1 [0]? Back in 2015, in relative terms, React seemed fast ! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9140290,"author":"chacham15","month":"2015-03","story":"Ember's Glimmer Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Side-by-side Ember, Angular, React rendering demo","quote":"(ember is on the left, then angular, then react)","text":"I dont know if this is exactly the same app, but it looks like it: [VIDEO] (ember is on the left, then angular, then react)"},{"id":9141642,"author":"Encosia","month":"2015-03","story":"Ember's “Glimmer” vs. A Simple Underscore Template","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the original React demo","quote":"which is a reimplementation of this React demo[1] from this React Conf talk[2]","text":"It's a simple implementation of an Ember/Glimmer demo[0], which is a reimplementation of this React demo[1] from this React Conf talk[2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9141741,"author":"ZaneD","month":"2015-03","story":"Ember's “Glimmer” vs. A Simple Underscore Template","stance":"reference","why":"Video comparison of Ember, Angular and React","quote":"Video comparison","text":"Here are a few more examples collected from the Ember Glimmer thread. Original demo of Ember, Angular and React: Source: [LINK] Video comparison: [VIDEO] Original Ember: [LINK] React: [LINK] Angular: [LINK]"},{"id":9827790,"author":"nchudleigh","month":"2015-07","story":"React Performance","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they weren't using some of the most powerful features cough cough react conf","text":"Posting benchmarking without full source code is bad. And you should feel bad. I remember seeing people bashing angular 1.2/3 for its speed and then looking at their source and they weren't using some of the most powerful features cough cough react conf ( [VIDEO] ) This ended up corrected at some point by some one who looked at the source code, forked it and made it perform on par with the react version. I don't doubt the vanilla JS is faster (in this case) but really, you need to make your source available before posting to your blog. Benchmarking in a fair way is hard, and you are damaging the public perception of a (from what I hear) a great framework. Maybe this is justified, but at least give the fanboys a chance to call you out - maybe everyone will learn something new."},{"id":9142125,"author":"msane","month":"2015-03","story":"Ember's Glimmer Engine","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I needed improvements in Ember's rendering performance a year ago","text":"I needed improvements in Ember's rendering performance a year ago, back when performance improvements were already behind schedule. A situation which still remains in production Ember today: [VIDEO] It's too little too late for me. I wouldn't want to touch anything Ember or anything from the authors."}]}});
